\par\par \b \uc2 RPT-EXPLAINER-What else can Russia do to save its troubled rouble?\b0\par\par\uc2 890 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 August 2023\par 10:42\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 (Repeats Wednesday's story with no changes to text)               \par \par \uc2 Aug 16 (Reuters) - Russia's battered rouble was given a reprieve with this week's emergency 350-basis-point rate hike to 12% but further action is likely to be needed for a more lasting impact.               \par \par \uc2 The Russian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has lost more than a fifth of its value since Russia invaded Ukraine in February 2022 and tumbled past the symbolic threshold of 100 per dollar on Monday.               \par \par \uc2 The impact of sanctions in reduced export revenues and higher import costs has contributed to rouble weakening, as has Moscow's sharply increased spending on its military to prosecute its war in Ukraine.               \par \par \uc2 With accelerating inflation causing prices for basic goods to rise and a presidential election approaching in March 2024, the rouble's fate is a sensitive subject for Russian authorities.               \par \par \uc2 The surest way to stabilise the rouble would be for Russia to withdraw its troops from Ukraine and cut back on soaring military spending, but assuming Moscow does not take those steps, here are some things Russia may try:                              \par \par \uc2 1. HIKE RATES AGAIN               \par \par \uc2 One available option would be to hike rates again. Last year's key rate increase to 20% calmed markets down and supported the rouble before capital controls started to take hold.               \par \par \uc2 But higher borrowing costs squeeze economic growth prospects and make life harder for companies and, in particular, the government as it finances military \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Ukraine.               \par \par \uc2 The finance ministry has said that lower rates would ease pressure on the budget deficit and its financing needs.               \par \par \uc2 Furthermore, future hikes are unlikely to deter ongoing capital outflows, UBP said in a note.               \par \par \uc2 "Higher interest rates will merely slow the pace of rouble depreciation."                              \par \par \uc2 2. CAPITAL CONTROLS               \par \par \uc2 Russian authorities are discussing bringing back the compulsory sale of foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  revenues for exporters, five sources familiar with the matter told Reuters on Wednesday, among other capital control measures that should buttress the rouble.               \par \par \uc2 One source said four measures were being considered:               \par \par \uc2 * Exporters returning up to 90% of export revenues to Russia.               \par \par \uc2 * Exporters who do not comply with this losing access to government support.               \par \par \uc2 * Bans on dividends and loans being paid abroad, including in jurisdictions that Russia considers "friendly".               \par \par \uc2 * Ban on import subsidies.                              \par \par \uc2 Russia ordered exporters to convert 80% of \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  revenues last year to support financial stability, which ultimately pushed the rouble to a more than seven-year high in June 2022.               \par \par \uc2 Sources said that if the rouble does not show signs of improving by Friday, capital controls could be reintroduced.                              \par \par \uc2 3. REDUCE LIQUIDITY SURPLUS               \par \par \uc2 The remaining options may be too limited in scope to make a large difference, but the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  does have other tools at its disposal.               \par \par \uc2 It could try to reduce the rouble liquidity surplus by raising banks' reserve requirements, thereby reducing the money supply that could be exchanged to foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .               \par \par \uc2 The Bank of Russia has shown that it is not indifferent to rouble volatility and the prospect of new steps to curb devaluation will at least temporarily weaken foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  demand, said \uc2 Promsvyazbank\uc2  analysts.               \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has already said it will stop holding one-year repo, or repurchase agreement, auctions that help banks manage their liquidity. Banks currently owe the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  1.28 trillion roubles ($13.46 billion) as a result of these auctions.               \par \par \uc2 Deputy Governor \uc2 Alexei Zabotkin\uc2  said the decision to stop repo auctions was not tied to the rouble's weakening and merely a coincidence.                              \par \par \uc2 4. \b \highlight22\uc2 FX INTERVENTIONS\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 One way to defend the rouble is for \b \highlight22\uc2 Russia\b0 \highlight\uc2  to sell foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from its National Wealth Fund (\uc2 NWF\uc2 ). After a months-long hiatus, \b \highlight22\uc2 Russia\b0 \highlight\uc2  resumed \b \highlight22\uc2 FX interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  in January, selling China's yuan.               \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Russia\b0 \highlight\uc2  tried to switch to \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  purchases in August, but that was swiftly aborted, with Russia effectively abandoning its own budget rule as the rouble fell.               \par \par \uc2 However, selling large amounts of foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  would deplete Moscow's \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves. Sanctions have frozen around half of Moscow's pre-war pile of more than $600 billion in \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2 .               \par \par \uc2 Zabotkin last week said that the bank would take that step as and when it was deemed necessary.                              \par \par \uc2 5. INCREASE OIL EXPORTS               \par \par \uc2 Analysts from \uc2 Alfa Bank\uc2  on Tuesday said Russia's decision to limit oil exports to support global prices is the structural reason behind the weak rouble.               \par \par \uc2 Moscow is extending an oil export cut into September, with next month's oil exports to be slashed by 300,000 barrels per day.               \par \par \uc2 Russia's oil and gas exports are down sharply this year, primarily due to a Western oil price cap and embargo. Other markets have opened up for Russia, but Moscow is forced to sell at a discount.               \par \par \uc2 Any increase in supply would see global prices fall.                              \par \par \uc2 6. CREATIVE TAXES               \par \par \uc2 A more outlandish option could be to impose new taxes on foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  transactions, as proposed on Tuesday by a lawmaker in the lower house of parliament, the State Duma.               \par \par \uc2 Duma lawmaker Vladimir Koshelev proposed taxing \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  transactions on the stock market and tightening the regulation of profits in foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .               \par \par \uc2 The Interfax news agency quoted a source as saying that authorities had discussed a new excess profit tax for those exporters that refuse to cooperate with returning revenues. ($1 = 95.1310 roubles)               \par \par \uc2 Released: 2023-8-17T09:42:55.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020230817ej8h010f6\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw103\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal1545\pichgoal600
