\par\par \uc2 CE Noticias Financieras English\par \b \uc2 Conflict with Ukraine sinks the ruble again\b0\par\par\uc2 576 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 August 2023\par \uc2 CE NoticiasFinancieras\par \uc2 NFINCE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 Content Engine LLC \par \par \uc2 The military \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Ukraine, which Russian President \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  ordered in February 2022, and subsequent Western sanctions against Russia are shaking the ruble. The Russian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  depreciated sharply after the "special military \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," as \b \highlight22\uc2 Russia\b0 \highlight\uc2  calls it, but over the months and with the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  of the Russian monetary authorities it recovered. This summer it depreciated again and on August 14 it broke the barrier of 100 units per dollar.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Russia\uc2  explains the weakening of the ruble by the imbalance in the trade balance, i.e. between export and import \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The value of exports, it has noted, has fallen by a third since the second half of 2022.\par \par \uc2 December 2022, major Western countries imposed a $60 cap on the price of Russian oil, the country's main source of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Western sanctions significantly reduced revenues from hydrocarbon exports. Natural gas costs ten times less than last year.\par \par \uc2 But the fall also seems to be influenced by the political and military developments surrounding the current conflict. In fact, the ruble has been depreciating steadily since the end of June, following the failed armed rebellion of the Russian mercenaries of the \uc2 Wagner Group\uc2 , led by St. Petersburg businessman \uc2 Yevgeny Prigozhin\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 For the time being, the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Russia\uc2  sees no risk to financial and economic stability. But experts and observers believe that the Russian state will have to take measures to strengthen the national \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 In an opinion piece for the Tass news agency, \uc2 Maxim Oreshkin\uc2 , Putin's economic advisor, blamed the ruble's more than 30% drop in 2023 on the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's policies.\par \par \uc2 "The main source of the weakening of the ruble and accelerating inflation is a soft monetary policy. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has all the tools to normalize the situation in the near future and ensure that interest rates are reduced to sustainable levels," wrote Oreshkin.\par \par \uc2 The advisor assures that the Kremlin wants a strong ruble. "A weak ruble complicates the structural transformation of the economy and negatively affects the real incomes of the population," he said.\par \par \uc2 Before the war against Ukraine broke out, one dollar exchanged for about 75 rubles. One euro, for about 85 rubles. This Monday, August 14, the drop is already over 101 rubles to the dollar, 111 to the euro.\par \par \uc2 Last year the fall was greater and reached 120 rubles per dollar on March 11, 2022. But the Russian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  recovered in the following months thanks to capital controls and increased export revenues.\par \par \uc2 Alexei Antonov, head of investment advisors at Alor Broker, explains to the RBK newspaper that for this period of decline to end it is necessary either for imports to stop falling or for the monetary authorities to take decisive steps. "Before those factors come about, we do not rule out the exchange rate reaching 115-120 rubles to the dollar," he warns.\par \par \uc2 Last week the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  halted the Finance Ministry's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  purchases in an attempt to thereby reduce volatility. But the effect was limited and did not translate into significant support for the Russian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 To stem its slide, Russia could reintroduce tighter capital controls. Another option could be to raise interest rates, but that would limit the potential for economic growth and would mean higher government borrowing rates at a time when it has to continue to fund its military activity in Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 Dokument NFINCE0020230815ej8e00304\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw103\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal1545\pichgoal600
