\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 2-Rouble edges up from over 16-month low as market digests new  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  purchases\b0\par\par\uc2 456 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 August 2023\par 15:52\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 (Updates at 1343 GMT)\par \par \uc2 By Alexander Marrow\par \par \uc2 Aug 3 (Reuters) - The Russian rouble firmed marginally on Thursday after clipping a more than 16-month low earlier in the session, with traders reacting to the finance ministry's decision to switch to foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  purchases from sales in August.\par \par \uc2 Speculation over what the ministry might do had led to jittery trade, with the Russian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  dropping around 2% in the previous session, only to rebound on Thursday following a Vedomosti report that the government would hold off on \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  purchases, fearing negative consequences for the rouble.\par \par \uc2 By 1343 GMT, the rouble was 0.1% stronger against the dollar at 94.05, earlier hitting 94.6325, its weakest since March 28, 2022.\par \par \uc2 It gained 0.2% to trade at 102.76 versus the euro and was unchanged against the yuan at 13.07 .\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Russia\b0 \highlight\uc2  restarted foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  this year, selling China's yuan to cover a shortfall in oil revenues following the imposition of price caps and embargoes as the West sought to punish Russia over its February 2022 invasion of Ukraine and limit its fiscal muscle.\par \par \uc2 Seeking to benefit from high oil prices, and in line with its budget rule, the finance ministry switched to purchases after seven months of sales, although the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's own \b \highlight22\uc2 FX interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  mean the country will still conduct net sales.\par \par \uc2 The volume of sales was only small, said Alfa Capital in a note, so the impact on the rouble could only be marginal. Furthermore, the \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  purchases are offset by separate sales the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  launched in August.\par \par \uc2 Maintaining net \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  sales until September will slightly support the rouble, said Dmitry Polevoy, head of investment at Locko-Invest, and the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's dynamics will play a key role in whether purchases end up being suspended.\par \par \uc2 Brent crude oil, a global benchmark for Russia's main export, was up 0.8% at $83.83 a barrel, paring early losses as Russia and Saudi Arabia extended oil output cuts into September.\par \par \uc2 Russian stock indexes were higher.\par \par \uc2 The dollar-denominated RTS index was up 1.1% at 1,051.7 points. The rouble-based MOEX Russian index was 1% higher at 3,138.0 points, its strongest since before Russia's February 2022 invasion of Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 The index remains well below the record highs above 4,000 points hit in late 2021, stung by geopolitics.\par \par \uc2 "The market looks overbought and its main indicator has reached our year-end target," said Sinara Investment Bank in a note. (Reporting by Alexander Marrow; editing by Robert Birsel, Nick Macfie and Christina Fincher) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2023-8-3T14:52:16.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020230803ej830161x\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
