\par\par \b \uc2 *S&PGR Affirms North Macedonia 'BB-/B' Ratings; Outlook Stable\b0\par\par\uc2 3,663 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 Juli 2023\par 22:06\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2023, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 28 Jul 2023 16:06 ET  Press Release: S&PGR Affirms North Macedonia 'BB-/B' Ratings; Outlook Stable\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par The following is a press release from S&P Global Ratings: \par  \par OVERVIEW \par      -- North Macedonia's real GDP will expand by a moderate 2.1% this year \par and gradually improve thereafter, averaging 2.7% in 2023-2026, as growth in \par its major export markets begins to recover. \par      -- We anticipate a slower pace of fiscal consolidation, but net general \par government debt will remain broadly stable over the next few years, averaging \par 53% of GDP in 2023-2026. \par      -- We affirmed our 'BB-/B' sovereign credit ratings on North Macedonia. \par The outlook is stable. \par  \par RATING ACTION \par On July 28, 2023, S&P Global Ratings affirmed its 'BB-/B' long- and short-term \par foreign and local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  sovereign credit ratings on North Macedonia. The \par outlook is stable. \par  \par OUTLOOK \par The stable outlook reflects our assessment that potential risks stemming from \par the Russia-Ukraine conflict are largely mitigated by North Macedonia's growth \par prospects, moderate government debt, and contained interest costs. \par  \par Upside scenario \par We could raise the ratings if the country shows strong progress in \par implementing structural reforms, leading to an enhanced institutional \par framework. Additionally, positive triggers for an upgrade would include \par improved fiscal performance with a decreasing trend in net general government \par debt, as well as strong GDP growth. \par Downside scenario \par We could lower the ratings if the country's fiscal or external metrics \par significantly deteriorate, or we see a depletion of its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  \par reserves that pressures the de-facto peg to the euro. Furthermore, if fiscal \par deficits worsen substantially beyond our medium-term projections, it could \par pressure the ratings. This would be especially pertinent if there were a sharp \par increase in government leverage. \par  \par RATIONALE \par Our ratings on North Macedonia are constrained by a weak but gradually \par improving institutional framework, relatively low GDP per capita, high \par government \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  debt, and limited monetary flexibility due to the \par denar's peg to the euro. \par  \par Nevertheless, our ratings are supported by moderate net government and \par external debt levels, the strong outlook for net foreign direct investment \par (FDI) inflows, and a stable banking sector. \par  \par Institutional and economic profile: Formal EU accession talks support \par long-term prospects, but the short-term economic outlook remains constrained \par      -- North Macedonia's economy is set to expand a moderate 2.1% in 2023 and \par 3.0% on average during 2024-2026. \par      -- Political stability has improved since the government increased its \par parliamentary majority but elections scheduled for next year could shift the \par political landscape. \par      -- EU accession talks continue to hinge on the adoption of a proposal, \par backed by France, to recognize the country's Bulgarian minority. \par  \par We anticipate that real GDP will expand a moderate 2.1% in 2023, reflecting a \par similar trajectory to the previous year. This is primarily attributed to low \par external demand, particularly from the EU, which is economically strained by \par the repercussions of the Russia-Ukraine conflict. Growth this year will also \par be hampered by reduced domestic household consumption, amid elevated inflation \par and tighter credit conditions. Nevertheless, the inflow of remittances from \par Macedonians employed overseas will support household consumption, and \par significantly sustain growth. Additionally, investments, including government \par initiatives like the Corridor 8 and 10d highway projects, with a total value \par of EUR1.3 billion, will help boost the economy. Looking beyond 2023, we \par anticipate a mild improvement in growth, reaching approximately 3% annually, \par as the indirect effects of the Russia-Ukraine conflict gradually subside, and \par external demand picks up. \par  \par North Macedonia maintains limited direct trade and financial connections with \par Russia and Ukraine, despite having some exposure to Russia in the energy \par sector. Most natural gas imports are sourced from Russia, but they constitute \par only 10% of North Macedonia's overall energy composition. Furthermore, less \par than 1% of oil imports are sourced from Russia. Despite Russia's suspension of \par gas supplies to Bulgaria, North Macedonia continues to receive Russian gas \par through pipelines via Bulgaria. \par  \par The next parliamentary elections are due next year, which could change the \par political landscape. The previous elections, in July 2020, resulted in the \par formation of a coalition government led by the Social Democratic Union of \par Macedonia (SDSM). A key objective of the current administration is to advance \par toward EU membership. However, in February 2023, a government reshuffle was \par announced, leading to the Alliance for Albanians replacing the Alternative \par party in the coalition. This reshuffle bolstered the ruling coalition's \par parliamentary majority to 68 seats of the 120, from 60. We believe that it has \par enhanced political stability and reduced the likelihood of early elections. \par Nonetheless, the country continues to grapple with long-standing political \par polarization, particularly between the SDSM and the opposition party, the \par Internal Macedonian Revolutionary Organization - Democratic Party for \par Macedonian National Unity (VMRO-DPMNE). This polarization has occasionally \par resulted in parliamentary obstructions, causing delays in the approval of \par legislation, and posing challenges to the country's institutional framework. \par North Macedonia's institutional framework is also marred by various \par deficiencies, including corruption. However, gradual progress has been made in \par recent years to address such issues. \par  \par Accession negotiations between North Macedonia and the EU formally began in \par July 2022. The progression of these talks hinges on parliament's endorsement \par of a France-backed proposal formed to encourage Bulgaria to withdraw its veto \par on North Macedonia's EU membership bid. According to the proposal, North \par Macedonia must enact constitutional amendments to acknowledge a Bulgarian \par minority, among other measures. As a result, the first of two \par intergovernmental conferences (IGC) between North Macedonia and the EU were \par held. The second IGC will only occur once North Macedonia's constitution is \par changed in accordance with the France-backed proposal. Under Macedonian \par legislation, constitutional amendments require at least 80 of 120 votes for a \par proposal to pass. Given the prevailing political polarization and the required \par vote count, the second IGC is unlikely to be held soon. Bulgaria could also \par block North Macedonia's bid for \f28 \uc2 \f2 EU\f28 \uc2 \f2  membership again. Nevertheless, we still \par consider the EU accession bid to serve as an anchor for structural and \par institutional developments over the medium term, supporting the country's \par economic prospects and helping alleviate its substantial emigration flows. \par  \par Flexibility and performance profile: Fiscal and external pressures are \par anticipated to persist throughout 2023-2026 \par      -- We expect a budget deficit averaging 3.5% of GDP over 2023-2026, \par versus our previous forecast of 3.2%. \par      -- Nevertheless, net general government will remain stable, averaging 53% \par of GDP over 2023-2026. \par      -- Despite forthcoming Eurobond redemptions, we expect foreign reserves \par to remain on a stable trajectory. \par  \par Given the government's track record of budget spending underutilization in \par recent years, we anticipate a budget deficit of 4.4% of GDP in 2023, which is \par consistent with last year's fiscal performance and slightly below the \par government's target of 4.6%. The authorities aim to boost revenue by \par streamlining preferential treatments on corporate, personal income, and \par value-added tax. In addition, the \f28 \uc2 \f2 EU\f28 \uc2 \f2  has provided EUR80 million of budget \par support in the form of a grant. On the expenditure side, subsidies to the \par state-owned electricity company will constitute 1.5% of GDP, with consumers \par currently protected from large tariff adjustments. In addition, the \par authorities are targeting a reduction in the government's operating costs \par through a decrease in expense such as electricity consumption, travel, and the \par hiring of external consultants, among others. \par  \par In line with previous years, the budget deficit will be financed with a \par mixture of domestic and foreign borrowing. This year, the authorities returned \par to the Eurobond market, issuing EUR500 million to pre-finance a EUR450 million \par Eurobond due in 2023. In November 2022, the authorities also obtained approval \par from the \f28 \uc2 \f2 IMF\f28 \uc2 \f2  for a two-year EUR530 million precautionary liquidity line (PLL), \par and have since secured a macro-financial assistance (MFA) package from the \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 European Commission\f28 \uc2 \f2  worth EUR100 million. \par  \par Over the long term, we expect a slower pace of fiscal consolidation as the \par government prioritizes its strategy aimed at promoting growth through \par infrastructure projects, such as the Corridor 8 and 10d highway project. As a \par result, the budget deficit will average 3.5% of GDP from 2023-2026, up from \par our previous forecast of 3.2%. Fiscal consolidation efforts face ongoing \par challenges, including the country's large informal sector, potential cost \par overruns in infrastructure projects, and the lingering effects of the \par Russia-Ukraine conflict (although notably less severe than in the past). \par Nevertheless, our projections indicate that net general government debt will \par remain broadly stable at about 53% of GDP in the next few years. \par  \par We note that about 75% of government debt is denominated in foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , \par which could create downside risks if the pegged exchange rate regime comes \par under pressure--although this is not our baseline scenario. Strict adherence \par to the recently adopted Organic Budget Law, which outlines a set of fiscal \par rules similar to the eurozone's Maastricht criteria, and the establishment of \par an independent fiscal council should help accelerate North Macedonia's fiscal \par consolidation efforts. We also note that despite tightening global financing \par conditions, North Macedonia's cost of servicing government debt remains \par contained and we expect it will average about 5% of government revenue over \par the next four years, which is lower than many peers. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 28 Jul 2023 16:07 ET \par Press Release: S&PGR Affirms North Macedonia -2-\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par North Macedonia heavily relies on Germany, which accounted for roughly 45% of \par total exports in 2022. It is also a net energy and food importer. Due to an \par economic slowdown in Germany and slightly elevated hydrocarbon prices this \par year, we expect the country's current account deficit to remain relatively \par wide, at about 3.7% of GDP, albeit down from 6.2% in 2022. We project a \par gradual narrowing of the deficit over time, averaging over 3.3% of GDP until \par 2026. The 2023 current account deficit will most likely be financed via a \par combination of channels that include net FDI inflows and external government \par borrowing (via Eurobonds, \f28 \uc2 \f2 IMF\f28 \uc2 \f2  program disbursements, and international \par financial institution loans). \par  \par In November 2022, the \f28 \uc2 \f2 IMF\f28 \uc2 \f2  approved a two-year EUR530 million PLL line \par requested earlier that year. The authorities plan to draw EUR265 million of \par the PLL funds and treat the remainder as precautionary--or available to be \par draw down if required. Given tightening financial conditions, the program will \par mainly help it meet forthcoming external and fiscal financing needs. \par Priorities under the PLL program include fiscal consolidation, reducing \par inflation, and protecting financial stability. In July, the \f28 \uc2 \f2 European \par Commission\f28 \uc2 \f2  also approved a EUR100 million MFA package in the form of loans. \par Disbursements from the \f28 \uc2 \f2 MFA\f28 \uc2 \f2  will be contingent upon the successful \par implementation of a broad range of structural reforms and a positive track \par record in the \f28 \uc2 \f2 IMF\f28 \uc2 \f2  program. \par  \par We expect net FDI will average roughly 3% of GDP through to 2026. But changes \par in global supply chains represent a risk, particularly in the auto sector as \par it transitions away from traditional combustion engines, at least in Europe. \par Nevertheless, the government is committed to maintaining the country's \par attractiveness for FDI and unveiled its first high-tech economic zone last \par year. Skopje III, which aims to become a national hub for new technologies and \par clean manufacturing, will offer similar incentives to other economic zones \par such as a 10-year holiday on corporate and personal income tax. Currently, \par companies operating within North Macedonia's free economic zones are primarily \par focused on the electronics and auto sectors. However, a significant portion of \par inputs are still imported rather than sourced domestically, which limits the \par integration of these free zones into the wider domestic economy. \par  \par We anticipate the pegged denar-euro exchange rate regime will be unchanged, \par despite a tough external environment. The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's foreign reserves \par shrank to EUR3.2 billion in May 2022 due to a high energy import bill and a \par decrease in securities holdings. However, reserves rebounded strongly to \par EUR4.2 billion as of June 2023, primarily attributed to external government \par borrowing and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . Despite a Eurobond redemption \par due this year, we expect foreign reserves to be stable, supported by improving \par external conditions. \par  \par After peaking at nearly 20% in late 2022, inflation has gradually slowed, \par primarily driven by lower food and energy prices. It stood at 9.3% in June \par 2023 and we anticipate a further deceleration in the coming months given \par easing commodity prices and monetary policy tightening by the \f28 \uc2 \f2 National Bank of \par the Republic of North Macedonia\f28 \uc2 \f2  (NBRM)--in line with the \f28 \uc2 \f2 European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central \par Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . As a result, we project headline inflation will average about 9% this \par year and 4% in 2024. However, it is important to note that there are potential \par risks to the inflation outlook, particularly stemming from the tight labor \par market, which could push up prices. \par  \par North Macedonia's financial sector has limited links with \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Russia\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  so the \par authorities did not need to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to manage the fallout from sanctions on \par Russian bank subsidiaries. This sets North Macedonia apart from neighboring \par countries like Slovenia, Croatia, and Bosnia and Herzegovina. Although \par pressures emerged within the banking system, leading to a brief episode of \par deposit withdrawals in March 2022, the euroization of deposits has decreased \par since then but remains above 40% of total stocks. The NBRM responded by \par adjusting reserve requirements in the past year, reducing them to 5% for the \par domestic \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and increasing them to 19% for foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  deposits. \par The sector's nonperforming loans remain low at 2.8% of total loans, and the \par capital adequacy ratio was stable at 18% in first-quarter 2023. As a result, \par the likelihood of contingent liabilities for the government arising from the \par financial sector remains reasonably low in the near term. \par  \par KEY STATISTICS \par  \par RELATED CRITERIA \par  \par      -- General Criteria: Environmental, Social, And Governance Principles In \par Credit Ratings, Oct. 10, 2021 \par      -- Criteria | Governments | Sovereigns: Sovereign Rating Methodology, \par Dec. 18, 2017 \par      -- General Criteria: Methodology For Linking Long-Term And Short-Term \par Ratings, April 7, 2017 \par      -- General Criteria: Principles Of Credit Ratings, Feb. 16, 2011 \par      -- General Criteria: Methodology: Criteria For Determining Transfer And \par Convertibility Assessments, May 18, 2009 \par  \par RELATED RESEARCH \par      -- Sovereign Ratings List, July 10, 2023 \par      -- Sovereign Ratings History, July 10, 2023 \par      -- Sovereign Risk Indicators, July 10, 2023. A free interactive version \par is available at \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.spratings.com/sri"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.spratings.com/sri}}}\uc2 \f2  \par      -- Sovereign Ratings Score Snapshot, July 7, 2023 \par      -- Global Sovereign Rating Trends Midyear 2023: Fragile Stability, July \par 5, 2023 \par      -- 2022 Annual Global Sovereign Default And Rating Transition Study, \par April 28, 2023 \par  \par In accordance with our relevant policies and procedures, the Rating Committee \par was composed of analysts that are qualified to vote in the committee, with \par sufficient experience to convey the appropriate level of knowledge and \par understanding of the methodology applicable (see 'Related Criteria And \par Research'). At the onset of the committee, the chair confirmed that the \par information provided to the Rating Committee by the primary analyst had been \par distributed in a timely manner and was sufficient for Committee members to \par make an informed decision. \par  \par After the primary analyst gave opening remarks and explained the \par recommendation, the Committee discussed key rating factors and critical issues \par in accordance with the relevant criteria. Qualitative and quantitative risk \par factors were considered and discussed, looking at track-record and forecasts. \par  \par The committee's assessment of the key rating factors is reflected in the \par Ratings Score Snapshot above. \par  \par The chair ensured every voting member was given the opportunity to articulate \par his/her opinion. The chair or designee reviewed the draft report to ensure \par consistency with the Committee decision. The views and the decision of the \par rating committee are summarized in the above rationale and outlook. The \par weighting of all rating factors is described in the methodology used in this \par rating action (see 'Related Criteria And Research'). \par  \par Certain terms used in this report, particularly certain adjectives used to \par express our view on rating relevant factors, have specific meanings ascribed \par to them in our criteria, and should therefore be read in conjunction with such \par criteria. Please see Ratings Criteria at \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.standardandpoors.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 www.standardandpoors.com}}}\uc2 \f2  for further \par information. A description of each of S&P Global Ratings' rating categories is \par contained in "S&P Global Ratings Definitions" at \par \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.standardandpoors.com/en_US/web/guest/article/-/view/sourceId/504352"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://www.standardandpoors.com/en_US/web/guest/article/-/view/sourceId/504352}}}\uc2 \f2  \par Complete ratings information is available to subscribers of RatingsDirect at \par \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.capitaliq.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 www.capitaliq.com}}}\uc2 \f2 . All ratings affected by this rating action can be found on \par S&P Global Ratings' public website at \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.standardandpoors.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 www.standardandpoors.com}}}\uc2 \f2 . Use the \par Ratings search box located in the left column.  Alternatively, call one of the \par following S&P Global Ratings numbers: Client Support Europe (44) 20-7176-7176; \par London Press Office (44) 20-7176-3605; Paris (33) 1-4420-6708; Frankfurt (49) \par 69-33-999-225; or Stockholm (46) 8-440-5914 \par  \par Primary Credit Analyst: Amr Abdullah, London 2071762000; \par                         amr.abdullah@spglobal.com \par Secondary Contact: Niklas Steinert, Frankfurt +  49 693 399 9248; \par                    niklas.steinert@spglobal.com \par Research Contributor: Ashay Gokhale, CRISIL Global Analytical Center, an S&P affiliate, Mumbai \par Additional Contact: Sovereign and IPF EMEA; \par                     SOVIPF@spglobal.com \par  \par  \par The article with statistical information is available in full \par at \f28 {\field{\*\fld