         130-133\par Shareholder Information and Corporate Details              134\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Letter to Shareholders\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Dear shareholders,\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 For the year ended 31 December 2022, \f28 \uc2 \f2 ENR Russia Invest SA\f28 \uc2 \f2  and its subsidiaries produced a consolidated net profit of CHF 0.18 million (2021: consolidated net profit of CHF 3.34 million).\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 During 2022 our profitability was impacted by ruble strength or weakness. In early 2022, the ruble depreciated sharply against the Swiss Franc on the back of events in Ukraine. After \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Russia\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  ("\f28 \uc2 \f2 CBR\f28 \uc2 \f2 ") \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , stability returned. The ruble then strengthened strongly against major \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  for most of the year due to rising export revenues, ruble based gas payments by customers and falling Russian imports (at half-year 2022, ruble strength resulted in an uplift in profits at that time). The ruble remained strong until December 2022, but then depreciated sharply against major \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  during the last few weeks of the year. Year-on-year, it appreciated by 3.72% against the Swiss Franc.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Our flower production facility is the largest Rus- sian domestic flower producer and benefits from its locally produced flowers to substitute imported flowers. The flowers are high-quality and last longer compared to imported flowers and the business provides regular and reliable supply to customers. Greenhouses are at full capacity utilisation with 25.1 hectares in productive use. 15 hectares are planted with more than 40 rose varieties; 2 hectares are planted with more than 20 germini and gerbera varieties; 5\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 hectares are used for a large annual tulip program and a range of seasonal flower programs; 3 hectares are used for chrysanthemums and 0.1 hectares for flower plant propagation. Flower growing, cutting and sales increased in 2022 as rose, gerbera and germini plants mature and yields improve. Clients include retail and wholesale groups and the smaller regional client base continues to grow.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 At the Petrovsky Fort business centre in Saint- Petersburg the business continues to perform solidly. A new supermarket store now brings convenience grocery shopping to our tenants. As part of the optimisation program on the retail floors, a 1'000 square meter area is under refurbishment to create a new value added coworking rentable space. Regular ongoing improvements and capex programs continue.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 At the Turgenevskaya parking garage in Mos- cow, daily parking income is stable and the new card payment system makes it easier for customer payments. The rental of longer-term parking bays to corporates and individuals are also improving.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Due to the sanctions against Russia, Russian en- tities, and individuals, the operating environment in Russia was impacted by import and export restrictions, import substitution, new regional and international sourcing and distribution routes, capital controls, and a lower growth environment.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Notwithstanding, our \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operational\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  businesses, which are all focused on the domestic economy, continue operating at levels comparable to 2021. However, there are challenges as our listed equities and bonds have been blocked (we are restricted from trading or repatriating sales proceeds abroad) and the \f28 \uc2 \f2 CBR\f28 \uc2 \f2  tightened available channels to repatriate cash distributions from our \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operational\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  businesses. We have taken the necessary actions to optimise available channels for cash repatriations. In addition, we renegotiated terms with our service providers and applied a range of measures within our group to cut costs.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 On the economic front, the \f28 \uc2 \f2 CBR\f28 \uc2 \f2  initially increased the key rate in February to 20% to stabilise financial markets, but within a few months reduced this rate via number of cuts. At year- end 2022 the rate was 7,5%, below the 9.5% one year earlier. Notwithstanding the impact of sanctions and higher inflation in 2022, economic growth contracted by 2.9% in 2022, much lower than initial forecasts.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 ENR continues to monitor geopolitical developments and the economic environment to evaluate how the situation is evolving, and to assess what actions to take.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Geneva, 4 April 2023\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Gustav Stenbolt Ben de Bruyn\par Chairman        Chief Executive Officer\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 * \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://enr.ch/wp-content/uploads/ENR-Annual-Report-2022_en.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Original Link}}}\par \par \uc2 \f2 Disclaimer\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 ENR Russia Invest SA\f28 \uc2 \f2  published this content on 04 April 2023 and is solely responsible for the information contained therein. Distributed by \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.publicnow.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Public}}}\uc2 \f2 , unedited and unaltered, on 04 April 2023 05:07:11 UTC.\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument LCDVP00020230404ej44004bl\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
