\par\par \b \uc2 *S&PGR Revises Kazakhstan Outlook To Stable From Negative\b0\par\par\uc2 5,043 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2023\par 22:13\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2023, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 3 Mar 2023 16:13 ET  Press Release: S&PGR Revises Kazakhstan Outlook To Stable From Negative\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par The following is a press release from S&P Global Ratings: \par  \par OVERVIEW \par      -- Government borrowing costs are increasing, given rising interest rates \par and the reliance on domestic debt issuance. \par      -- Kazakhstan relies on the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Caspian Pipeline Consortium\f28 \uc2 \f2  for about 80% of \par its oil exports, a prolonged disruption of which could negatively affect its \par external and fiscal metrics. \par      -- In our view, however, Kazakhstan's strong fiscal and external balance \par sheets make risks related to temporary disruptions to the pipeline manageable. \par      -- We therefore revised our outlook on Kazakhstan to stable from negative \par and affirmed our 'BBB-/A-3' ratings on the sovereign. \par  \par RATING ACTION \par On March 3, 2023, S&P Global Ratings revised its outlook on the Republic of \par Kazakhstan to stable from negative. At the same time, we affirmed our \par 'BBB-/A-3' long- and short-term foreign and local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  sovereign credit \par ratings on the sovereign. We also affirmed our 'kzAAA' national scale rating \par on Kazakhstan. The transfer and convertibility assessment remains 'BBB'. \par  \par OUTLOOK \par The stable outlook reflects our view that Kazakhstan's government and external \par balance sheets will remain strong, providing sufficient buffers to absorb \par temporary disruptions to the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Caspian Pipeline Consortium (CPC) \f28 \uc2 \f2 pipeline. \par  \par Downside scenario \par We could lower the ratings if Kazakhstan's oil exports declined significantly \par for a prolonged period, weakening the economy's external position and \par increasing fiscal deficits. This could be the case, for instance, if the CPC \par pipeline were incapacitated for an extended period. We could also lower the \par ratings if rising borrowing costs continued to increase the government's \par debt-servicing burden. Other disruptions, such as deteriorating domestic \par stability with severe civil unrest, could also lead to a downgrade. \par  \par Upside scenario \par We could raise the ratings if the medium-term fiscal trajectory improves more \par than we expect, reducing the pace of debt accumulation and debt-servicing \par requirements; and monetary policy effectiveness improves, as shown by more \par modest inflation, continued commitment to exchange rate flexibility, and a \par less dollarized economy. \par  \par RATIONALE \par We base the outlook revision on our assessment that, although risks to \par Kazakhstan's oil exports through the CPC pipeline remain, absent a prolonged \par closure, the country's fiscal and external positions should be able to absorb \par temporary disruptions. \par  \par In 2022, at least four incidents affected the CPC pipeline. In our view, \par geopolitical tensions from the Russia-Ukraine conflict could cause further \par disruptions (see "Kazakhstan Remains Vulnerable To Russian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Over \par CPC Pipeline," published July 13, 2022, on RatingsDirect). About 90% of oil \par flowing through the CPC pipeline originates from Kazakhstan, while the \par remaining 10% is Russian. The \f28 \uc2 \f2 U.S. government\f28 \uc2 \f2  explicitly excluded \par Kazakh-origin crude oil from the pipeline from its sanctions related to the \par prohibition of dealing in Russian oil. \par  \par We also account for the government's efforts to develop alternative export \par routes to the CPC pipeline, a significant portion of which is in Russia. \par Still, it seems likely that the CPC pipeline, which accounted for about 52 \par million tons, 1.04 million barrels per day (bbl/d), or 81% of Kazakh oil \par exports over the first nine months of 2022, will remain the dominant export \par route for the foreseeable future. The most significant effort to diversify \par export routes is the government's aim to eventually export 6.0 million-6.5 \par million tons, or 120,000-130,000 bbl/d, of oil through the Baku-Tbilisi-Ceyhan \par (BTC) pipeline. The government expects to begin exporting about 1.5 million \par tons, or 30,000 bbl/d, through BTC in 2023. The BTC pipeline runs through the \par "Middle Corridor," meaning the Caspian Sea, Azerbaijan, Georgia, and Turkey. \par Earthquake damage in the Turkish port of Ceyhan has delayed progress. \par  \par Against the backdrop of the uncertain geopolitical environment, we think \par Russia could disrupt flows through the pipeline to gain leverage against \par Kazakhstan or the oil's consumers, namely countries in Europe that support \par Ukraine; or to hurt Western interests in the Kazakh oil sector (shareholders \par of the pipeline and oil fields include \f28 \uc2 \f2 Chevron\f28 \uc2 \f2 , \f28 \uc2 \f2 ExxonMobil\f28 \uc2 \f2 , \f28 \uc2 \f2 Shell\f28 \uc2 \f2 , and \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 TotalEnergies\f28 \uc2 \f2 ). In such an event, disruptions at the pipeline might not be \par directed at causing economic pain in Kazakhstan. We think the country's fiscal \par and external buffers are sufficiently strong to absorb a temporary reduction \par in oil exports, similar to the incidents of 2022. But if the pipeline's \par closure were to be prolonged, this could be more damaging. \par  \par Oil is the key economic sector in Kazakhstan, directly accounting for about \par 15% of GDP, more than 30% of general government revenue, and over half of \par exports. We expect Brent oil prices will average $90 per barrel (/bbl) in \par 2023, $80/bbl in 2024, and $55/bbl from 2025 and beyond (see "S&P Global \par Ratings Cuts 2023 European, U.S., And Canadian Gas Price Assumptions On Lower \par Demand," published Feb. 23, 2023). \par  \par We expect the government's fiscal deficit to narrow sharply in 2023. However, \par government borrowing costs are increasing, given rising interest rates and \par reliance on domestic debt issuance. We project that interest will average \par close to 10% of revenue over 2023-2026. \par  \par The ratings on Kazakhstan are supported by the country's strong fiscal and \par external balance sheets. These were built primarily with budgetary surpluses \par from the period of high commodity prices that ended in late 2014. The related \par assets were accumulated in the National Fund of the Republic of Kazakhstan \par (NFRK) and largely invested abroad. We forecast that the economy's liquid \par external assets will exceed external debt through 2026. \par  \par Institutional and economic profile: GDP growth averaging close to 4% over \par 2023-2026 will be supported by both the oil and non-oil sectors \par      -- Relations with Russia appear to be relatively strong for now. \par      -- President \f28 \uc2 \f2 Kassym-Jomart Tokayev\f28 \uc2 \f2  was reelected at snap elections in \par 2022. Constitutional changes extended the president's term of office to seven \par years from five. \par      -- Diversifying the economy, developing the private sector, and \par increasing the population's wealth levels will remain policy priorities. \par  \par The impact of the Russia-Ukraine conflict on Kazakhstan has thus far been \par moderate for the country's economic growth and banking sector. Russia is \par Kazakhstan's largest trading partner and a key source of imports. However, \par trading patterns are changing and Russian imports now make up about 30% of \par Kazakhstan's total imports, down from over 40% before the conflict, while \par exports to Russia make up about 10% of the total. Export bans imposed by \par Russia on sugar and grains and the depreciation of the Kazakhstan tenge (KZT) \par against the Russian ruble in 2022 contributed to rising food prices in \par Kazakhstan, driving annual average inflation to 15%. The secondary hit from \par sanctions imposed on Russia likely also made it more difficult for local \par companies to trade in some sectors, although we understand that informal trade \par has likely increased given the free flow of goods and people between the two \par countries. Kazakhstan mostly exports iron ore and uranium to Russia while \par importing cars, semi-finished goods, coal, and refined petroleum. \par  \par The CPC pipeline connects Kazakhstan's Tengiz, Kashagan, and Karachaganak oil \par fields to the Black Sea port of Novorossiysk in Russia. The incidents that led \par to the pipeline's disruption in 2022 included storm-related damage in March \par that reduced exports for almost a month; safety inspections for World War \par II-era deep-sea mines in June; a Russian court ruling in July to stop loading \par for 30 days due to alleged violations of the pipeline's oil spill plan, which \par was overturned a few days later and replaced with a minor fine; and subsea \par cracks discovered on buoyancy tanks that limited loading on two of three CPC \par single-point moorings. As a result, we estimate Kazakh oil production at 84 \par million tons per year (equivalent to about 1.7 million bbl/d), down from 90 \par million tons in 2021. \par  \par Kazakhstan--along with Armenia, Belarus, Kyrgyzstan, and Russia--is a member \par of the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Eurasian Economic Union\f28 \uc2 \f2 , a customs union that provides tariff-free \par trade and uniform custom tariffs for trade with nonmember states. \f28 \uc2 \f2 The Eurasian \par Economic Union\f28 \uc2 \f2  ensures the free movement of goods, services, labor, and \par capital between the states, and since mid-March it announced the ruble's use \par for reimbursement of customs fees with Russia and Belarus. \par  \par Russia is also a key source of investment, accounting directly for about 8% of \par Kazakhstan's total external liabilities, although this could be larger since \par investment in its oil, gas, and mining sectors is often recorded through the \par Netherlands and the U.K. On the other hand, for Kazakhstan, unlike other \par Central Asian sovereigns, remittances from Russia are not an important source \par of income. \par  \par Although Kazakhstan is a member of the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Collective Security Treaty Organization\f28 \uc2 \f2  \par (\f28 \uc2 \f2 CSTO\f28 \uc2 \f2 ), a mutual defense organization with members from ex-Soviet states, we \par think it is unlikely to be directly involved in the Russia-Ukraine war. The \par government has affirmed its policy of neutrality, similar to when Russia \par annexed Crimea in 2014; stating its commitment to the territorial integrity of \par all nations, including Ukraine; and abstaining on \f28 \uc2 \f2 U.N\f28 \uc2 \f2 . votes to condemn the \par invasion. In response to the civil unrest in January 2022, the government \par requested assistance from the \f28 \uc2 \f2 CSTO\f28 \uc2 \f2 , and peacekeeping troops, mostly from \par Russia, arrived quickly to help restore order. In our view, should a similar \par situation occur, it is unclear whether \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Russia\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  would have the resources or \par willingness to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to the same extent, without some reciprocation in \par terms of Kazakhstan indicating more support for Russia's foreign policy. \par  \par The January 2022 protests quickly spread throughout Kazakhstan. They were \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 3 Mar 2023 16:13 ET \par Press Release: S&PGR Revises Kazakhstan Outlook -2-\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par ignited by an increase in the price of liquified petroleum gas (LPG) but \par expanded to encompass broader grievances, including persistent inequality, low \par wages and living standards, aging infrastructure, constraints on political \par representation, and the perceived high level of corruption. Almaty--the \par largest city--saw the most unrest, damage, and loss of life. \par  \par In response to the protests, President Tokayev announced a series of economic \par and governance reforms. The government reinstituted caps on gasoline, LPG, and \par utility prices for six months. The price controls for lower quality gasoline \par were then extended until the end of the year, while tariff increases will be \par tiered based on consumption. The president also announced institutional \par changes to reduce the influence of former President \f28 \uc2 \f2 Nursultan Nazarbayev\f28 \uc2 \f2  and \par his family on politics and business; increase plurality; decentralize power \par away from the president somewhat; and improve the competitiveness and social \par role of the large state-owned enterprises, such as \f28 \uc2 \f2 Samruk-Kazyna\f28 \uc2 \f2 . \par  \par We expect political reforms will be gradual and modest. In our view, \par decision-making will stay centralized, while government checks and balances \par are limited, and there is no credible political opposition party. Moreover, \par limitations on independent media outlets are significant and perceived \par corruption is high, with Kazakhstan ranking 101 of 180 countries in \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Transparency International\f28 \uc2 \f2 's Corruption Perceptions Index. \par  \par President Tokayev held snap elections in November 2022, winning a second term \par in office with 81.3% of the vote. The win gave him a longer second term after \par a recent constitutional reform changed the presidential term to a single, \par seven-year term, from the previous system that was a maximum of two \par consecutive five-year terms. Mr. Tokayev started his new term on Nov. 26, \par 2022. \par  \par Government policy aims to diversify the economy through capital investment \par programs, including infrastructure and housing projects. The pool of \par investment projects under consideration amount to an estimated KZT30 trillion \par (26% of 2023 GDP). The government also focuses on cultivating the investment \par climate, reducing state participation in the economy, increasing \par private-sector competition, improving tax policy, reforming public \par procurement, and reducing administrative barriers for businesses. Although we \par believe the government's prudent and generally effective economic policymaking \par will continue, we do not anticipate that the broad scope of reforms will bring \par about significant economic changes. \par  \par The economy expanded an estimated 3.2% year on year in 2022, supported by \par growth in construction, information and communications, trade, transport, and \par manufacturing. Government stimulus measures have continued since 2019, \par including higher spending on development projects, defense, wages, social \par security, and education. \par  \par Over the next three years, we expect growth will average just under 4% \par annually. A key factor will be the expansion of the Tengiz oil field, which \par should significantly increase oil production. Absent disruptions to \par Kazakhstan's oil exports, we expect oil production could exceed 100 million \par tons per year by 2025, from 85.9 million in 2021. Ongoing investment and \par efforts to attract foreign investment should also support strong non-oil \par sector growth. \par  \par Flexibility and performance profile: Although government debt remains modest, \par the interest bill is rising \par      -- We expect rising oil production and reduced spending of the oil fund \par will support fiscal consolidation in 2023. \par      -- We expect Kazakhstan's external asset position will remain strong, \par with liquid external assets exceeding external debt by 33% of current account \par payments by 2026. \par      -- Inflation averaged 15% year on year in 2022, above the \f28 \uc2 \f2 National Bank \par of Kazakhstan\f28 \uc2 \f2 's (\f28 \uc2 \f2 NBK\f28 \uc2 \f2 's) target range of 4%-6%, amid expansionary fiscal policy \par and higher imported prices. \par  \par We expect the consolidated general government deficit will narrow to about 2% \par of GDP in 2023 from 3% in 2022. We expect higher rising oil production and \par reduced spending of the oil fund to support fiscal consolidation. We expect \par the government deficit to remain at about 2% of GDP annually through to 2026, \par supported by efforts to broaden the revenue base and moderate spending growth. \par  \par The government will implement fiscal rules from 2023 to limit growth in \par spending and transfers from the NFRK to the budget. Still, spending will \par likely stay elevated as social priorities and reliance on government spending \par to boost economic growth will continue to steer fiscal policy in an uncertain \par external environment. Despite strong growth in non-oil revenue after the new \par tax code's introduction in 2017, a large share (30%) of fiscal revenue will \par continue depending on the oil sector over our forecast period to 2026. \par  \par The government's strong balance sheet relies on a relatively large stock of \par liquid assets. We estimate net debt at about 0.5% of GDP in 2023-2026, with \par government liquid assets averaging about 26% of GDP, slightly below our \par estimate of gross government debt at 27% of GDP. We expect the implementation \par of a fiscal rule limiting transfers from the NFRK to the budget, and expanding \par oil production volumes, to help maintain government liquid assets near current \par levels as a percentage of GDP. \par  \par Based on our forecast of relatively modest government deficits alongside \par strong nominal GDP growth, we expect the level of gross government debt to \par remain largely unchanged through 2026. Tenge depreciation will put some upward \par pressure on the debt burden because 36% of government debt is external and \par denominated in foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 --of this, about 60% is commercial and the rest \par is mostly from multilateral lenders. In the past two years, the government has \par increased borrowing from multilateral lenders and issued in the ruble market. \par  \par Despite relatively low government debt levels, interest expenditure is rising \par partly because of the growing proportion of domestic debt. We expect interest \par costs will remain high given past monetary tightening, with average yields on \par short-term local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  government debt having risen to about 16% in \par November 2022 from about 10% on average in 2021. Nevertheless, since August \par 2022, the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has been issuing floating-rate bonds linked to \par the tenge overnight index. This can ease the interest expenditure burden when \par inflation recedes. We expect the government's interest-to-revenue ratio will \par average about 10% over 2023-2026, compared with 5% in 2019. \par  \par In 2022, high oil prices led to the first current account surplus since 2014. \par The strong trade balance was partly offset by net outflows in the primary \par income account, spurred by profit repatriation on foreign direct investment \par related to oil production. We expect that falling oil prices and rising \par imports will bring the current account broadly into balance over 2025-2026. \par  \par Kazakhstan's external asset position is strong, and we expect gross external \par financing needs will stay below 100% of current account receipts and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign \par exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves. Reserve assets at the \f28 \uc2 \f2 NBK\f28 \uc2 \f2  increased slightly, to about $35 \par billion in 2022 from $34 billion in 2021. About 55% of reserves are gold. The \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 NBK\f28 \uc2 \f2  purchases gold with domestic \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , providing the government a way to \par increase reserves despite past current account deficits. We forecast reserves \par will remain broadly stable with the revaluation effect of lower gold prices \par being mitigated by the \f28 \uc2 \f2 NBK\f28 \uc2 \f2 's decision to decrease the share of gold. S&P \par Global Ratings forecasts gold prices of $1,600 per ounce (/oz) in 2023 and \par $1,400/oz over 2024-2025, compared with $1,829/oz in 2021 (see "S&P Global \par Ratings' Metal Price Assumptions: Lower Prices And Higher Costs Start \par Squeezing Profits," published Nov. 1, 2022). \par  \par In our view, the more freely floating exchange rate, following the tenge's \par sharp devaluation in 2015, has helped the economy adjust to external pressure. \par The expected decrease in the current account surplus, to near balance in \par 2024-2025, and the government's plan to reduce transfers from the NFRK, a key \par tenge purchaser in the market, will weigh on the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  over the forecast \par period to 2026. \par  \par Annual average inflation soared to 15% in 2022, leading the \f28 \uc2 \f2 NBK\f28 \uc2 \f2  to raise the \par base rate 700 basis points (bps) to 16.75%. In its most recent monetary policy \par meeting of January 2023, the \f28 \uc2 \f2 NBK\f28 \uc2 \f2  kept the policy rate unchanged and has \par indicated its intention to stay at current levels for an extended period. We \par expect inflation will average 12% in 2023, slowing to 5% by 2025 because of \par improvements to inflation targeting, the higher base rate, and expected slower \par tenge depreciation. \par  \par To increase independence and effectiveness of its monetary policy framework, \par the \f28 \uc2 \f2 NBK\f28 \uc2 \f2  announced it will not take part in new economic lending and support \par programs. It remains a shareholder of oil and gas company KazMunayGas NC JSC, \par with ownership of 9.8%. \par  \par The \f28 \uc2 \f2 NBK\f28 \uc2 \f2  is also working with the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to build out a yield \par curve for local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  debt to improve liquidity in the domestic secondary \par market. To increase transparency in the tenge market, the \f28 \uc2 \f2 NBK\f28 \uc2 \f2  began publishing \par all sales of U.S. dollars from the NFRK. While the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  continued to \par focus on price stability, in 2019, it was reorganized by spinning off \par financial sector regulation to the new Agency for Regulation and Development \par of Financial Markets. The agency is in charge of financial market regulation \par and supervision while the ?entral bank focuses on macroprudential policy. \par  \par Financial stability risks from the Russia-Ukraine conflict have been \par manageable so far, in our view. The three Russian banks that made up about 14% \par of systemwide deposits--\f28 \uc2 \f2 Sberbank\f28 \uc2 \f2 , \f28 \uc2 \f2 VTB Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2 , and \f28 \uc2 \f2 Alfa Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2 --have rapidly \par downsized their \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in Kazakhstan(see "Financial Stability Risks \par Stemming From Sanctioned Russian Bank Subsidiaries In Kazakhstan Have Been \par Limited," published July 7, 2022). This came through acquisitions and the \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 3 Mar 2023 16:13 ET \par Press Release: S&PGR Revises Kazakhstan Outlook -3-\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par partial sale of the loan portfolio to other banks. Currently, only one bank \par with Russian participation (VTB Kazakhstan) operates in the country, with less \par than 0.5% share in systemwide customers funds. \par  \par Despite significant exchange rate volatility, dollarization levels remained \par relatively stable in 2022, at 32% for deposits and 7% for loans. Individuals \par were encouraged to keep their deposits in local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , due to rising \par deposit interest rates and a one-time premium of 10% on some types of \par tenge-denominated deposit accounts, the costs of which were shared by the \f28 \uc2 \f2 NBK\f28 \uc2 \f2  \par and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . The scheme ended in February 2023, and given the more \par stable exchange rate, we do not expect a significant increase in \par dollarization. \par  \par We believe Kazakhstan remains in a prolonged correction cycle after previously \par posting much higher problem loans than peers. Although the stock of problem \par loans in the sector has decreased significantly over the past two years, we \par estimate that the sector average is still relatively high at about 9% of \par banks' lending books (measured as Stage 3 loans under IFRS). We expect that \par systemwide credit costs will remain at 1.5%-1.7% for the next 18 months, due \par to the potential recognition of new problem loans, as well as banks' \par increasing appetite to grow their loan books. Yet several factors could \par overshadow this forecast, among them weaker regional economic growth than \par expected, for example due to a gloomier global economic outlook, or a steep \par rise in geopolitical risks. Higher inflation in the region than the historical \par average could also erode the creditworthiness of some companies and households \par (see "Banking Sector Outlook 2023: Central Asia And Caucasus Remain \par Resilient," published Feb. 9, 2023). We currently classify Kazakhstan in group \par '9' under our Banking Industry Country Risk Assessment on a scale from '1' to \par '10', '10' being the weakest score. \par  \par KEY STA