\par\par \uc2 CE Noticias Financieras English\par \b \uc2 Russian  \highlight22\uc2 central bank \highlight\uc2  forecasts 2.4% year-on-year economic contraction in first quarter\b0\par\par\uc2 310 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 Februar 2023\par \uc2 CE NoticiasFinancieras\par \uc2 NFINCE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 Content Engine LLC \par \par \uc2 MOSCOW, Feb 20 (Reuters) - \uc2 Russia's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said on Monday it expects the country's gross domestic product (GDP) to fall 2.4 percent year-on-year in the first quarter of 2023, with annual inflation at 3.6 percent.\par \par \uc2 The bank warned this month that any increase in Russia's budget deficit could force it to raise interest rates, sending a hard-line signal after it kept borrowing costs unchanged but adjusted forecasts for next year.\par \par \uc2 It maintained its hawkish tone on Monday, expanding those forecasts in a monetary policy report in which it reiterated that a rate hike was more likely than a cut this year, given inflationary and disinflationary risks.\par \par \uc2 According to the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , annual inflation is expected to slow in April to its slowest pace this year, reflecting last year's high base effect, before accelerating again.\par \par \uc2 "In the following quarters, the impact of pro-inflationary factors will gradually increase, while that of disinflationary factors will decrease," the bank said in its monetary policy report.\par \par \uc2 Russian households' inflation expectations will continue to significantly influence the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's monetary policy decisions.\par \par \uc2 Expectations would have to return to levels seen when inflation was approaching the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's 4% target before the policy rate could return to the neutral 5%-6% band.\par \par \uc2 The bank said it expected the banking sector's structural liquidity surplus to be between RUB2.8 trillion and RUB3.4 trillion ($37.5 billion to $45.5 billion) by the end of 2023.\par \par \uc2 The 2022 liquidity surplus stood at 2.8 trillion rubles. The bank said its new forecast took into account the resumption of \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. \b \highlight22\uc2 Russia\b0 \highlight\uc2  is selling Chinese yuan to cover its growing budget deficit.\par \par \uc2 (1 dollar = 74.7455 rubles)\par \par \uc2 Dokument NFINCE0020230221ej2k000qy\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
