\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 2-Russian c.bank sees economy contracting by 2.4% y/y in Q1\b0\par\par\uc2 353 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 Februar 2023\par 17:16\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 (Adds detail on inflation expectations, liquidity surplus)\par \par \uc2 MOSCOW, Feb 20 (Reuters) - \uc2 Russia's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said on Monday it expects the country's gross domestic product (GDP) to fall by 2.4% year-on-year in the first quarter of 2023, with annual inflation standing at 3.6%.\par \par \uc2 The bank this month warned that any further widening of Russia's budget deficit might compel it to raise interest rates, sending a hawkish signal as it kept the cost of borrowing unchanged but adjusted forecasts for the year ahead.\par \par \uc2 On Monday, it retained its hawkish tone as it expanded on those forecasts in a monetary policy report, reiterating that a rate hike was more likely than a rate cut this year when taking into account conflicting pro-inflationary and disinflationary risks.\par \par \uc2 Annual inflation is expected to ease to its slowest pace this year in April, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said, on the back of last year's high base effect, before accelerating again.\par \par \uc2 "In the subsequent quarters the impact of pro-inflationary factors will gradually increase, while the effect of disinflationary factors will decrease" the bank said in its monetary policy report.\par \par \uc2 It said the inflation expectations of Russian households would continue to significantly influence the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's decisions on monetary policy.\par \par \uc2 It said inflation expectations would need to return to levels seen when inflation was near the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's 4% target before the key rate could return to the neutral range of 5%-6%.\par \par \uc2 The bank said it expected the banking sector's structural liquidity surplus to be between 2.8 and 3.4 trillion roubles ($37.5-$45.5 billion) by the end of 2023.\par \par \uc2 The 2022 liquidity surplus stood at 2.8 trillion roubles. The bank said its new forecast took into account the resumption of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \b \highlight22\uc2 Russia\b0 \highlight\uc2  is now selling Chinese yuan to cover its widening budget deficit. ($1 = 74.7455 roubles) (Reporting by Elena Fabrichnaya; Writing by Alexander Marrow Editing by Gareth Jones) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2023-2-20T17:16:42.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020230220ej2k029y1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw103\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal1545\pichgoal600
