\par\par \uc2 CE Noticias Financieras English\par \b \uc2 Western banks struggle to exit  \highlight22\uc2 Russia \highlight\uc2  after Putin's  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 1,330 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 Januar 2023\par \uc2 CE NoticiasFinancieras\par \uc2 NFINCE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 Content Engine LLC \par \par \uc2 Advisors to Western banks trying to leave \b \highlight22\uc2 Russia\b0 \highlight\uc2  say a law introduced by \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  is disrupting sales and allowing businessmen close to the Kremlin to grab business.\par \par \uc2 Nearly a year after the invasion of Ukraine, only a handful of Western banks have managed to leave Russia, albeit at a high cost, while others have opted to keep their businesses in the country.\par \par \uc2 However, for most of those trying to sell their Russian assets, hopes of a quick exit were dashed when Putin declared last year that foreign owners in "enemy" countries could not close deals without his approval. The list of companies involved includes 45 banks with subsidiaries in \b \highlight22\uc2 Russia\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Advisors working on deals expect the Russian president's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to scuttle some sales already under discussion, while fundamentally altering the terms of others.\par \par \uc2 In addition, they expect sale prices already agreed to be cut by as much as half as the Kremlin exerts more influence over the deals. And they claim that potential buyers, who were initially beaten to the punch, have obtained presidential approval and are attempting to hijack sales from rivals who do not have the Kremlin's favor.\par \par \uc2 "There are some very powerful Russians with close ties to the Kremlin who are trying to use their influence to snatch these entities from fleeing foreigners," said a person involved in the negotiations, one of several who spoke to the \uc2 Financial Times\uc2  on condition of anonymity because of the sensitive nature of the talks with the Russian government.\par \par \uc2 "We are working on these kinds of deals every day, but it's getting harder and harder," said Laura Brank, a partner at law firm Dechert, which is advising Western banks on the sale of their Russian subsidiaries; adding that the situation is very fluid and the rules are not really clear.\par \par \uc2 Putin says Russian military \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Ukraine is going Well.\par \par \uc2 Challenges from foreign banks\par \par \uc2 Within days of the invasion, Western banks that had spent decades slowly building up their Russian branches faced a stark choice: sell the business quickly and take a heavy loss, or hold on and scale back gradually.\par \par \uc2 Western sanctions and Moscow's counter-sanctions made it virtually impossible for foreign banks to do business in the country.\par \par \uc2 Austria's \uc2 Raiffeisen Bank International\uc2 , the Western lender with the largest presence in Russia and Ukraine, increased its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  coverage and cash reserves in anticipation of customers withdrawing their savings as troops massed on the border early last year.\par \par \uc2 But the Feb. 24 invasion took bank executives, like most Western bankers, by surprise. "It was one of the most shocking days of my life," said \uc2 Hannes M\u246\'00\'F6senbacher\uc2 , \uc2 Raiffeisen\uc2 's chief risk officer.\par \par \uc2 Raiffeisen\uc2 's subsidiary is the largest on the Kremlin's list, with 4.2 million customers and 9,400 employees in Russia on the eve of the invasion, and the bank has yet to figure out how it will be evicted from the country.\par \par \uc2 Of its \u8364\'20\'AC22.9 billion of assets in Russia at the beginning of 2022, only \u8364\'20\'AC354 million were exposed to financial institutions subject to Western sanctions and \u8364\'20\'AC119 million to other companies affected by sanctions.\par \par \uc2 In late July, \uc2 HSBC\uc2  agreed to sell its Russian subsidiary to local lender Expobank in a deal that would allow it to exit a country that had become politically toxic since Moscow's invasion of Ukraine earlier this year.\par \par \uc2 But that sale has now been delayed. \uc2 HSBC\uc2  said it was still working to try to complete the transaction, but a person with knowledge of its plans said Expobank, as the acquirer, had to get Putin's approval.\par \par \uc2 "For us, there is no change since the deal was signed," the \uc2 HSBC\uc2  executive said. "It just needs to go through these machinations."\par \par \uc2 One bank that managed to change its Russian subsidiary before the presidential decree was France's \uc2 Societe Generale\uc2 , which agreed in April to sell its \uc2 Rosbank\uc2  business, as well as its Russian insurance \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 , to an investment firm founded by billionaire \uc2 Vladimir Potanin\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 Along with \uc2 Raiffeisen\uc2  and Italy's \uc2 UniCredit\uc2 , SocGen had one of the largest exposures to Russia of any Western bank, with \u8364\'20\'AC18.6 billion of assets at the beginning of 2022. \uc2 Rosbank\uc2  employed 12,000 people.\par \par \uc2 SocGen was able to strike a quick deal because it sold to Potanin, one of Russia's richest men with close ties to the Kremlin, who was sanctioned by the U.S. last month. The French bank had also bought the business from Potanin in 2008.\par \par \uc2 "We did it very, very quickly; it helped that we sold it to someone who knew the bank well," said a SocGen executive. "We even got congratulatory calls from rivals saying how efficiently and neatly we were able to get rid of it."\par \par \uc2 However, by arriving at such a hasty sale, SocGen was forced to take a \u8364\'20\'AC3.3 billion hit .\par \par \uc2 Other banks looking for a quick exit did not have a ready buyer waiting in the wings, nor were they prepared to absorb a financial hit like the one SocGen suffered.\par \par \uc2 UniCredit\uc2 's Russian \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  include 2 million customers and 3,500 employees. CEO \uc2 Andrea Orcel\uc2  even considered increasing his exposure by buying Russian bank Otkritie just weeks before the invasion. As of mid-October, its total exposure to Russia was still \u8364\'20\'AC7 billion.\par \par \uc2 The Italian bank's failure to cut ties with Russia has caused friction with the \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the FT reported, after Orcel said over the summer that liquidating the business or selling it at a discount was "not morally right."\par \par \uc2 More recently, however, the bank has said it is "committed to withdrawing from Russia in an orderly and decisive manner," which Orcel said is different from the "get rid of everything" strategies pursued by other banks, without naming them.\par \par \uc2 "You're throwing it at the very people you're trying to fight," he told a \uc2 Bank of America\uc2  conference in September. "We're trying to make sure there's an orderly containment of what we have and ultimately get out, but in a way that's not a giveaway."\par \par \uc2 He did, however, agree to sell RN Bank, his Russian joint venture with \uc2 Renault\uc2  and \uc2 Nissan\uc2 , to Lada maker \uc2 Avtovaz\uc2 . Putin gave the deal the green light in late November.\par \par \uc2 Citigroup\uc2 , whose local subsidiary is subject to the decree, has taken a different approach to dealing with its exposure, which stood at $7.75 billion at the end of December.\par \par \uc2 After failing to find a buyer for its Russian business for more than a year, the U.S. lender decided to liquidate the business. Last month, the bank sold a portfolio of Russian consumer loans to Uralsib, a local commercial lender.\par \par \uc2 It also plans to close most of its institutional banking services in Russia by the end of the first quarter of 2023, although its custody \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  are likely to prove more difficult to unwind, according to people with knowledge of the business.\par \par \uc2 Intesa Sanpaolo\uc2  Chief Executive \uc2 Carlo Messina\uc2  has outlined his intention to turn Italy's biggest lender by assets into a "zero exposure bank to Russia" by winding down cross-border lending between Italian and Russian companies, which makes up the majority of its business in the country.\par \par \uc2 But like the other Western banks trapped in the country, the fate of its Russian subsidiary is in Putin's hands.\par \par \uc2 "It's an extremely difficult situation for us as it is for most banks," said the executive of a bank with a subsidiary on the restricted list.\par \par \uc2 "Not being able to sell initially to sanctioned entities and now holding all these banks hostage plays into what the Russian government wants. There is no incentive to facilitate the exit of banks."\par \par \uc2 He added: "We are in limbo, but it is not for lack of wanting to solve it. It is very difficult to see what is the way out of this."\par \par \uc2 Dokument NFINCE0020230116ej1g00bks\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
