\par\par \uc2 CE Noticias Financieras English\par \b \uc2 Without imports, Russia relies on smuggling to sustain economy in war\b0\par\par\uc2 3,160 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 Dezember 2022\par \uc2 CE NoticiasFinancieras\par \uc2 NFINCE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 Content Engine LLC \par \par \uc2 The suitcases were full of equipment bought in Austria. The products were not particularly original -professional electronic equipment for use in schools-, but they were manufactured by a Western brand that had decided to boycott Russia over the invasion of Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 "It was made to look like it was just for personal use. As if I had bought it all for myself," says Stanislav, who met the men at a hotel in Istanbul, took them to dinner, and then flew to Moscow with the bags.\par \par \uc2 "Of course it was smuggling, pure and simple," he says, speaking on the condition of anonymity due to the illegal nature of the activity.\par \par \uc2 It was an unusual order for Stanislav. He normally specializes in using trucks to smuggle out of Europe the much bulkier and more delicate items that are under sanctions in Russia, such as materials for the construction sector and parts and machinery for heavy industry.\par \par \uc2 Stanislav is part of a growing number of so-called Russian import-export specialists -professionals at finding loopholes and getting goods through customs- who have emerged in response to Western sanctions on the country.\par \par \uc2 Interviews with participants in this underground market reveal a lucrative but highly unpredictable and unstable trade that Russia's beleaguered economy will have difficulty relying on.\par \par \uc2 Increasingly, however, it needs to trust. The severe sanctions applied since the beginning of the war have disrupted Russian supply chains and left many companies struggling to source crucial foreign brand products and parts.\par \par \uc2 When the heaviest sanctions were adopted in March, some economists predicted a rapid collapse of the Russian economy, perhaps as much as 30 percent. But that didn't happen: oil and gas revenues continued to flow and the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  soon recovered.\par \par \uc2 What is emerging is something different - not a dramatic decline, but a steady degradation of its productive capacity that economists in Russia and the West argue is setting the country back decades. Russia is trying to operate a modern economy without the ability to import many components, raw materials, and technologies on which it depends.\par \par \uc2 The impact is felt throughout the economy - from banks that need servers to process payments to the country's poultry industry, which relied on the Netherlands as a supplier of the chicks from which chickens are grown for mass production of eggs.\par \par \uc2 Agricultural companies are struggling to get tractor tires, while airlines can't get foreign components to fix their planes.\par \par \uc2 The blow has already been significant. Data from Russia's trading partners show that Russian imports have fallen by 20 to 25 percent since the start of the war -a blow to a country embedded for decades in the global economy.\par \par \uc2 "If you look at the pharmaceutical industry, chemical production, machine building, metals and mining.... It's hard to find a sector in Russia that doesn't rely on imports for at least 50% [of inputs]," says \uc2 Elina Ribakova\uc2 , an economist at the IFI (Institute of International Finance).\par \par \uc2 In the medium term, the sanctions will likely set Russia's economy back by years. Consumers will be forced to adapt to a more limited choice of low-quality goods and products that may be reminiscent of the deprivations at the end of the Soviet era.\par \par \uc2 "Life will be simpler and there will be less money. People will make do with less. There will be more paper in the sausage," says a Russian oligarch who is under sanctions.\par \par \uc2 "It will be like that for 15 to 20 years, unless [President \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 ] dies. Fundamentally, nothing will change."\par \par \uc2 Ultimately, Russia's long-term economic future depends on whether Moscow can quickly produce domestic alternatives to the foreign goods it can no longer access, or obtain analogues from "friendly" countries such as China. When these two options fail, it is left to rely on imports from smugglers like Stanislav.\par \par \uc2 For him, the new restrictions have created a land of opportunity. Previously, foreign goods were shipped to Russia by official importers, and there were few ways for a new player to sneak in. Trying to import brands illegally was no use either, as there was little demand.\par \par \uc2 "I could have put the goods in a double bass box and brought them in, but nobody would have bought them, because I couldn't give the buyer an official warranty, etc.," Stanislav says.\par \par \uc2 "Now I can import this, I can import that, like everybody else. So for me, of course, it's interesting," he adds. "The doors have opened."\par \par \uc2 'Everything is being imported anyway' Several smuggling routes have already become popular.\par \par \uc2 Stanislav buys most items through front companies established in Europe, with no visible connection to Russia. The products are then shipped on trucks from the \uc2 European Union\uc2  to one of the former Soviet Union countries that share a customs union with Russia, such as Kazakhstan and Armenia.\par \par \uc2 "Any brand that came out of Russia, no matter what it is -aspirators, clothes, alcohol- is being imported anyway," says another Russian citizen living in Europe who works in the import-export trade.\par \par \uc2 But the process is irregular at best. Take the example of clinker brick -a material used, among other things, in decorative facades.\par \par \uc2 "The last deliveries were in June and that's it. No one knows how to bring it in," explains the person. Some builders involved in high-end construction have already clad half of their buildings, he says. "What to do with the other half? It's not clear."\par \par \uc2 Over time, routes will be found to get the material to Russia, but, as with many other imported products, it will be expensive and therefore available only to special customers, the person adds.\par \par \uc2 Russia put its import data under wraps early in the war, but economists are building a picture using information on exports to Russia from its major trading partners.\par \par \uc2 Exports from the United States to Russia fell 85 percent in May compared with the same month a year earlier, according to research by the \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The \uc2 World Bank\uc2 , \uc2 IMF\uc2 , and other institutions predict that Russian imports this year will fall 25 percent from a year earlier. From June to August this year, Russia imported $4.5 billion less per month than it did in 2021, according to the Kiel Institute for World Economics.\par \par \uc2 Demand for imports also fell as the economy went into recession and inflation began to squeeze household incomes, while a growing number of Western companies that had branches in Russia pulled out.\par \par \uc2 Given the shortage of imported parts, automobile production has been one of the hardest hit sectors, with a drop of nearly 80 percent in September compared to the same month in 2021, according to state statistics service \uc2 Rosstat\uc2 . The drop has prompted authorities to loosen some safety requirements over the summer for anti-skid brakes and airbags.\par \par \uc2 So many Western automakers and dealers sold their business in Russia that only 14 automakers remained in the market for Russian buyers, according to an industry analysis published in December. All but three of the brands were Chinese, including the iconic Soviet Lada.\par \par \uc2 A billionaire close to the Kremlin says that the potential profit from smuggled goods is so high that luxury goods will always arrive in the country, regardless of sanctions. He tells how over the summer he bought two Maybachs instead of the Mercedes he wanted, but couldn't get. If the first one breaks, he can use the second one to get parts out, he adds.\par \par \uc2 Others have already glimpsed new opportunities. "It's going to be hard for two, three, four years. Then we will adapt," says another oligarch under sanctions. "Look at Iran. They do everything themselves [...] they have their own supply chains, and if they don't have a spare part, they get it on the black market. They will do anything. We are learning our lessons now, and in the future we will be the same."\par \par \uc2 Collapse delayed So far, the economy has avoided the worst predictions. Economists estimate a drop in gross domestic product of between 3.5 percent and 5.5 percent this year.\par \par \uc2 In part, this is because export revenues have remained strong and Russia is increasingly finding alternative buyers for its oil.\par \par \uc2 There has, for example, been an increase in exports to Russia among the countries through which some companies are redirecting trade. Exports to Russia from countries such as Turkey and Kazakhstan are on the rise. While the \uc2 European Union\uc2  exported 43 percent fewer goods to Russia from June to August, China exported 23 percent more, according to the Kiel Institute.\par \par \uc2 While there has not been a disintegration of the economy, analysts believe that long-term growth will be substantially reduced as import restrictions destroy the potential for technological upgrades. Local industries that end up replacing production are usually less efficient, they say, while parallel market import flows are volatile.\par \par \uc2 "If you go back to the Soviet Union and how they got the technology, it was through the FSB, spies and fronts, buying things in other countries undercover," says economist Jacob Nell, a member of a sanctions expert working group headed by former U.S. Ambassador to Russia Michael McFaul and \uc2 Andriy Yermak\uc2 , chief of staff to Ukrainian President \uc2 Volodimir Zelensky\uc2 . "But it's very difficult to build supply chains when you have these kinds of comprehensive sanctions."\par \par \uc2 Nell adds, "Even if you can steal the designs, it's very difficult to replicate -in an economically and commercially sustainable way, without subsidy- the production of these things."\par \par \uc2 Stanislav agrees. Shipments are volatile, and intermediary countries are adopting new rules designed to eliminate this kind of trade. Earlier this month, the \uc2 EU\uc2  proposed making sanctions evasion a criminal offense, and he immediately felt a massive drop-off from suppliers. One of his deliveries got stuck in Kazakhstan customs.\par \par \uc2 "A loophole was found, and thanks to connections and a small bribe, we found a way to move things along," he says. Still, "it's getting more difficult every day."\par \par \uc2 In general, companies and state agencies are looking for ways to respond to the import crackdown.\par \par \uc2 In July, Putin appointed former Trade Minister \uc2 Denis Manturov\uc2  to a senior government post with a mandate to restore supply chains. Manturov promised to defend Russia's "technological sovereignty" and make import substitution "a matter of national security."\par \par \uc2 Although Manturov later insisted that this would not mean "completely abandoning the principles of the market economy," the push to increase domestic production will inevitably lead to much heavier state \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  that limits competition, according to another Russian oligarch under sanctions.\par \par \uc2 "If you have ten companies making plates, it won't work. You have oversupply and insufficient quality. Nobody needs that," says the oligarch.\par \par \uc2 "Instead, you need to be sure of just a few producers. They need to continue to compete with each other and produce a quality material, but you want to avoid overproduction. You have to be sensitive to the size and power of the market."\par \par \uc2 Some solutions have already been found. A \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  survey of Russian companies found in April that two-thirds were struggling with disrupted supply chains. By the summer, that number had dropped to 50 percent.\par \par \uc2 "So for companies looking for alternative suppliers, there is a marginal improvement," says IFI's Ribakova, referring to the survey. "But on the other hand...these are aggregate numbers. They don't reveal the bottlenecks."\par \par \uc2 The critical list In a new underground complex known as a "bunker" near the Ukraina hotel in Moscow, Russia's cabinet meets regularly to discuss presentations dissecting the country's dire economic prospects on a huge 180-degree screen.\par \par \uc2 One such presentation, prepared by a major state-owned bank since August this year and also seen by the \uc2 Financial Times\uc2 , lists some of these critical areas.\par \par \uc2 In five columns, it grouped the industries by degree of risk, with the last one colored in red and labeled "supercritical." Industries listed in this category include aircraft construction, pharmaceuticals and medical technology, microchip production and high-end IT equipment, and spacecraft-building technology.\par \par \uc2 The cabinet claims that it has already successfully replaced imports in some of the industries. "They wanted to ground our air fleet. Take the sky from us," Prime Minister \uc2 Mikhail Mishustin\uc2  said in November, at a time when there were several reports in the local media about how airlines were struggling to get spare parts. "But we have maintained our planes and are expanding the deployment of domestic technology."\par \par \uc2 One way \b \highlight22\uc2 Russia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support importers, especially of consumer technology goods, was by legalizing what it calls "parallel imports."\par \par \uc2 The law made it legal to pass a long list of Western branded products through customs into Russia without the consent of the brand itself. Previously, this would have been considered piracy, but now it is Stanislav's semi-official trade.\par \par \uc2 The government has estimated that $20 billion worth of goods have been imported into Russia this year, including the new iPhone 14, which was released in September and not officially on sale in the Russian market. "If consumers want to buy these phones, they are welcome," Manturov said in September.\par \par \uc2 Kirill, another Moscow import specialist, opened a parallel import business focusing on furniture and appliances soon after the war began. He says that one of the most common schemes for parallel imports is to work with a company, for example from Kazakhstan, that already has a relationship with a Western brand.\par \par \uc2 This company then places its usual orders, but in significantly larger volumes. The Russian partner pays discreetly to import the surplus into Russia, which is easy when the goods are safe in Kazakhstan, since there is no customs border.\par \par \uc2 But the gray market route is less effective for harder-sourced imports, mainly microchips and servers, says an executive at a Russian technology company.\par \par \uc2 "You can't make it a mass [business] because banks also need servers" and will pay more than smaller buyers, the executive says. "That makes it much more expensive and unsustainable."\par \par \uc2 Producers have become much more wary of the increased volume of microchip orders by Armenia and Kazakhstan, which have become home to large communities of Russian IT specialists exiled since the war, but are also centers of customs-free parallel imports.\par \par \uc2 "Some American companies have stopped shipping to Armenia. They say, 'We had one license a year before. Now you are asking for a hundred licenses?'," says the executive.\par \par \uc2 A senior executive at a major Russian technology company warns of the long-term impact of the microchip shortage.\par \par \uc2 If the sanctions last for two to four years, it will still be worth paying for the most sought-after chips at twice the market price, the executive says. However, if they last longer, the executive believes Russia will be forced to switch to inferior Chinese chips. Expanding Russia's own microprocessor production capacity to the level of China -now struggling under U.S. export restrictions- would likely cost $50 billion a year for ten years, and even then would not be guaranteed to work, the executive adds.\par \par \uc2 To get through the complex compliance procedures required to demonstrate that customers are not circumventing sanctions, some manufacturers require their customers to prove that they are not physically in Russia when placing an order, the executive adds.\par \par \uc2 "Do you want ten mainframe servers for Gyumri?" the executive explains, referring to a city in Armenia, another parallel import hub. To prove it, "go to Zoom with a purchasing officer to show him that you are in Armenia and have people in the office."\par \par \uc2 Even Russian government organizations are turning to parallel imports as a result of the sanctions. Stanislav says he has been approached by two different regional Russian chambers of commerce, one located in Siberia and the other in central Russia, asking him for help in obtaining foreign products that are under sanctions or embargo.\par \par \uc2 "They turn to us businessmen and say, 'Guys, can you bring this to us? Can you import this?'"\par \par \uc2 The 'do-it-yourself' economy Soon after the first sanctions packages were introduced, Grigory Bolotin, head of the Cheboksary Energy Machinery Factory, gathered his team around a large blank sheet of paper and started drawing.\par \par \uc2 He drew a huge map of the company -a vast factory on the banks of the Volga River east of Moscow that produces forklifts, tractors, and other heavy machinery- and outlined all the supply chains on which it depended.\par \par \uc2 The team quickly identified the areas that were heavily dependent on the western parts. These product lines had to be discontinued.\par \par \uc2 In other areas, they got creative. Where they used to import microchips to operate their tractors, they decided to try making their own. They bought basic transistors and other chip parts in Asia and learned to solder them themselves. "They come out pretty simple, but they get the job done," says Bolotin.\par \par \uc2 For other key components, they found domestic substitutes. The Japanese motors used in their forklifts were replaced with alternatives produced in Minsk, the capital of Russian ally Belarus.\par \par \uc2 "Sure, the Minsk engine is noisier, less economical, less reliable. ButIt's there, it's available. And everybody kind of got used to it," says Bolotin.\par \par \uc2 In general, they've managed to get by. But Bolotin could see the broad effect of the import restrictions on quality and the level of technology.\par \par \uc2 Cheboksary's products were once considered of average quality. But since the sanctions were imposed they have "moved to the higher end of the market."\par \par \uc2 "We probably won't be able to produce equipment with the same technological level as products supplied by Western countries," says Bolotin. But now Russian customers don't mind, he says.\par \par \uc2 Economist \uc2 Branko Milanovic\uc2  has labeled this process "technologically regressive import substitution," exchanging imported goods for "inferior and outdated domestic substitutes."\par \par \uc2 Economies of scale from importing raw materials and components are being lost.\par \par \uc2 Ribakova of the IFI explains, "You will be able to produce it, but it will be much more expensive, because it is inefficient." She adds, "If you are a factory in Russia producing buttons for half the world, your unit cost of production is completely different from having to produce a limited number of buttons for a specific production."\par \par \uc2 For example, Finland was a major exporter to Russia of chemicals used in paper bleaching. After their shipments stopped, several Russian pulp mills had to learn to do without or started producing the bleaching chemicals themselves. The Soviet Union was also self-sufficient in these products.\par \par \uc2 But one result of using non-existent or substandard chemicals is that some of the paper used in Russian offices has a grayish-brown tint.\par \par \uc2 A deputy industry minister insisted earlier this year that there is a silver lining, citing new findings showing that excessively white paper can damage the eyes. "Very bright white office paper is really bad for health," he said.\par \par \uc2 Dokument NFINCE0020221215eicf009uk\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw103\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal1545\pichgoal600
