\par\par \uc2 CE Noticias Financieras English\par \b \uc2 Russia holds up much better than expected and forces \uc2 IMF\uc2  to revise upwards its growth by more than 5 points since April\b0\par\par\uc2 682 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 Oktober 2022\par \uc2 CE NoticiasFinancieras\par \uc2 NFINCE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 Content Engine LLC \par \par \uc2 What was going to be an economic debacle a few months ago could end up as a simple, run-of-the-mill recession. Russia is weathering Western sanctions much more boldly and skillfully than expected. It is true that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  (and the government) had been accumulating gold and \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves for years to survive such a scenario, but it is also true that the Russian economy is holding up better than expected, with the invaluable help of rising commodity prices, which have boosted its export revenues and generated a historic current account surplus for the Russian economy.\par \par \uc2 Such is the surprise of Russia's economic resilience that the \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2  has been forced to revise upwards its growth forecast for the economy by 5.1 points from the April WEO and by 2.6 points from the July update.\par \par \uc2 Thus, the \uc2 IMF\uc2  now forecasts a contraction in the economy of 3.4%, compared to 8.5% in April. At the beginning of the war, several analysts and organizations predicted GDP falls of up to double digits for the Russian economy, figures which are now a dead letter.\par \par \uc2 When the first sanctions were imposed, the ruble plummeted violently. Analysts and Western governments predicted the apocalypse for the Russian economy, with inflation running out of control (the depreciation of the ruble partly supported this argument). Today, however, Russian inflation is 13.7% (admittedly very high), not far from the 10% of the Eurozone and even lower than the Baltic countries, which despite being in the euro have an inflation rate of 20%.\par \par \uc2 The rapid \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  of the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Russia\b0 \highlight\uc2 , with drastic interest rate hikes and capital controls, at the beginning of the war in Ukraine, the search for new customers (China and India, above all) to place a much more expensive oil, together with an economy that had been equipped for years to resist a scenario of Western isolation, are allowing Russia to keep the rate. Meanwhile, Ukraine's (war-torn) economy will sink 35% this year, according to \uc2 IMF\uc2  forecasts.\par \par \uc2 A resilient labor market The international body's paper stresses that Russia's economy suffered an intense blow at the start of the war, but soon after began to revive as export revenues rose, the ruble regained all the ground lost against the dollar and the economy adapted to the new normal under sanctions. "Russian domestic demand is showing some stability, thanks to the containment of the effect of sanctions on domestic financial sector policy support and a resilient labor market," the \uc2 IMF\uc2  report admits.\par \par \uc2 Against all odds, Russia's unemployment rate has fallen to 3.8%, when in January 2022 (before the war and sanctions), unemployment stood at 4.4%. The Bank of Russia revealed in a recent document that some sectors such as mining or energy were growing strongly, which could explain this trend.\par \par \uc2 Thus, the \uc2 IMF\uc2  projects growth of -3.4% and -2.3% in Russia in 2022 and 2023, compared to the expected 35% contraction in Ukraine in 2022, as a result of the war. On the price side, inflation will be just over 13% in Russia (versus over 20% estimated by several agencies at the start of the war), while it will fall to 5% in 2023.\par \par \uc2 The strength of the ruble The \uc2 IMF\uc2  admits in the report that "the contraction in Russia's economy is less severe than previously projected, reflecting resilience in crude oil exports and domestic demand with stronger fiscal and monetary policy support and a restoration of confidence in the financial system."\par \par \uc2 A good summary of all of the above is the performance of the ruble/euro cross. Theory and logic would say that the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  of a country immersed in a war and under sanctions from the almighty West should be suffering a debacle. The reality is that the Russian ruble is up more than 30% against the euro so far this year.\par \par \uc2 Dokument NFINCE0020221011eiab007c1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
