\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Russia \highlight\uc2  sets new budget rule, may resume  \highlight22\uc2 FX interventions \highlight\uc2  this year -FinMin\b0\par\par\uc2 527 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 September 2022\par 16:00\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 This content was produced in \b \highlight22\uc2 Russia\b0 \highlight\uc2  where the law restricts coverage of Russian military \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Ukraine\par \par \uc2 By Darya Korsunskaya\par \par \uc2 MOSCOW, Sept 28 (Reuters) - Russia estimates the new cut-off price for its budget rule that diverts excess oil revenues into its wealth fund at $62-$63 per barrel and may resume foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  purchases as early as this year, Finance Minister \uc2 Anton Siluanov\uc2  said on Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 The rule, designed to replenish state reserves by buying foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  when oil prices are high, was fully suspended amid harsh Western sanctions imposed after Moscow launched what it calls a "special military \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2 " in Ukraine on Feb. 24.\par \par \uc2 Siluanov, whose ministry published spending plans from its National Wealth Fund (\uc2 NWF\uc2 ) earlier on Wednesday, said domestic borrowing would be ramped up to the tune of around $43 billion next year, with extra borrowing to reduce \uc2 NWF\uc2  spending also possible.\par \par \uc2 "We are planning on big volumes on the borrowing market - 2.5 trillion roubles ($43 billion) next year on a gross basis," Siluanov said.\par \par \uc2 "Plus, the government will be given the right to carry out additional borrowing of up to 1 trillion roubles to replace the use of \uc2 NWF\uc2  funds under favourable market conditions. That's a total of 3.5 trillion roubles."\par \par \uc2 Russia's finance ministry\uc2  has held just one successful OFZ treasury bond auction since sending thousands of troops into Ukraine on Feb. 24, a 10 billion rouble placement on Sept. 14.\par \par \uc2 Market volatility has forced it to cancel weekly auctions since then.\par \par \uc2 "For us it is important that the budget is not a bottomless barrel in which to take decisions on spending," Siluanov said. "We need to live within our means. We have defined these means as 8 trillion roubles in base oil and gas revenues over three years."\par \par \uc2 Siluanov said that corresponded to a cut-off price of $62-$63 per barrel. Anything above that should be sent to the \uc2 NWF\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "If in foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , then naturally only in a friendly \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , there are no other alternatives. In which foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ? Obviously, right now it is the yuan."\par \par \uc2 Russia considers those countries that have imposed sanctions on it as "unfriendly".\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 FX INTERVENTIONS\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 Siluanov also did not rule out a return to the \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  market this year with \b \highlight22\uc2 currency interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  stopped such \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  earlier this year as sanctions targeted \b \highlight22\uc2 Russia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's state reserves.\par \par \uc2 "We can also save in roubles, but then there will be no impact on exchange rate rations," Siluanov said. "We of course as exporters do not want a strong exchange rate, but we don't want sharp fluctuations either."\par \par \uc2 Siluanov said any \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  purchases made this year would be based on the current budget rule and its cut-off price of $44.2 per barrel.\par \par \uc2 It would be up to the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to decide whether to enter the market or not and whether to make all purchases in yuan or leave some in roubles, Siluanov added. ($1 = 58.2930 roubles) (Reporting by Darya Korsunskaya; Writing by Alexander Marrow; Editing by Hugh Lawson) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2022-9-28T15:00:00.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020220928ei9s02ujr\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw103\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal1545\pichgoal600
