\par\par \uc2 CE Noticias Financieras English\par \b \uc2 The suspicious resilience of the Russian economy\b0\par\par\uc2 717 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 September 2022\par \uc2 CE NoticiasFinancieras\par \uc2 NFINCE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 Content Engine LLC \par \par \uc2 When the first economic sanctions against Putin were announced, politicians and analysts expected a destructive effect on the Russian economy. Six months into the conflict, the exact impact of these measures is still unknown. In fact, headlines about the country's economic performance in the Western media defy expectations, raising suspicions about the veracity of the data.\par \par \uc2 Even during seemingly peaceful years, the transparency of the Russian economy could be compared to a blizzard in Siberia, but since the invasion, national agencies have stopped publishing various time series. For example, the Kremlin no longer publishes monthly data on international trade, the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Russia\uc2  hides data on the monetary base and origin of liabilities, and even the \uc2 Federal Air Transport Agency\uc2  stopped providing information on the evolution of passengers.\par \par \uc2 Moreover, since May of this year, the Russian national statistics institute, \uc2 Rosstat\uc2 , has been under the direction of Sergei Galkin, who previously worked for \uc2 Maxim Oreshkin\uc2 , Putin's economic advisor during the Western financial siege.\par \par \uc2 Based on the available data, it seems reasonable to argue that sanctions have damaged the Russian economy, but not annihilated it. One of the undoubted reasons is the escalation of hydrocarbon prices, which in 2021 accounted for half of exports and 40% of tax revenues.\par \par \uc2 According to Centre for Research on Energy and Clean Air (CREA), Russia has earned almost 160 billion euros (of which 88 billion from the EU) from fossil energy exports since February 24. In the first 100 days since the start of the attacks on Ukraine it has cornered 20% more revenue from this item than in the same period of 2021. Russian military expenditures are estimated at 100 billion euros since the beginning of the conflict.\par \par \uc2 Additionally, the Russian ruble has strengthened considerably due to the authorities' \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Russia\b0 \highlight\uc2  announced on February 28 to raise interest rates from 9.5 % to 20 % in order to curb consumption and encourage savings. In addition, the authorities imposed strong capital controls to prevent investment flight. This combination of economic policies, together with the hydrocarbon bonanza, has stabilized the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and tripled the country's current account balance.\par \par \uc2 A stronger ruble makes imports cheaper and the abundance of energy sources avoids the inflationary pressures found in the Western European energy market. As a result, inflation fell from 18% to 15% between April and July.\par \par \uc2 As for unemployment, it is suspiciously low since the invasion, as many international companies have gone on lay-offs by temporarily halting their activities in the country. However, there has not been an explosion of unemployed people. In fact, employability and lower inflation have allowed Russians to continue to consume.\par \par \uc2 However, the real damage from the sanctions is expected in the longer term. According to The Economist, the most potent sanction lies in limiting Western exports to Russia. These exports include goods such as software, semiconductors and other intermediate products, which are key to the Russian mechanical and military industries.\par \par \uc2 In fact, Russian military equipment is already suffering from a shortage of semiconductors, connectors, transistors and components that used to be imported from the US, Germany, the Netherlands, Taiwan or Japan, and they are forced to dust off old Soviet-era equipment. They cannot even replace them with Chinese components, since the Asian giant does not produce the most sophisticated microchips either and has to import them from Taiwan or South Korea.\par \par \uc2 In the absence of official data on the evolution of GDP, the \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2  predicts a fall in Russian GDP of 6% in 2022 and 3.5% in 2023. The \uc2 World Bank\uc2  expects a contraction of 8.9% in 2022 and 2.2% in 2023. Even the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Russia\uc2  warned that the second half of the year will be tougher and forecasts a recession of 4-6% in 2022 and 1-4% in 2023.\par \par \uc2 In short, Russia will suffer a recession but it will not be a calamity, compared to the turbulent evolution of its economy since the fall of the USSR. And nothing, compared to the expected fall of 35-45% of the Ukrainian economy.\par \par \uc2 Eszter Wirth, Professor of International Economics (\uc2 ICADE\uc2 ), \uc2 Comillas Pontifical University\par \par \uc2 This article was originally published in The Conversation. Read the original.\par \par \uc2 Dokument NFINCE0020220910ei9a0096u\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
