\par\par \b \uc2 *S&PGR Revises Kazakhstan Outlook To Negative; Affirms Ratings\b0\par\par\uc2 4,948 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 September 2022\par 22:09\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2022, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 2 Sep 2022 16:09 ET  Press Release: S&PGR Revises Kazakhstan Outlook To Negative; Affirms Ratings\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par The following is a press release from S&P Global Ratings: \par  \par OVERVIEW \par      -- In our view, Kazakhstan remains vulnerable to potential disruptions to \par the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Caspian Pipeline Consortium (CPC)  \f28 \uc2 \f2 pipeline, which the country relies on \par for about 80% of its oil exports. \par      -- If Kazakhstan's oil export capacity reduced for a prolonged period, \par this could negatively affect external and fiscal metrics. \par      -- An increasing reliance on domestic funding and rising borrowing costs \par could further increase the government's debt-servicing burden and weigh on its \par fiscal accounts. \par      -- We therefore revised our outlook on Kazakhstan to negative from \par stable, and affirmed our 'BBB-/A-3' sovereign credit ratings. \par  \par RATING ACTION \par On Sept. 2, 2022, S&P Global Ratings revised its outlook on Kazakhstan to \par negative from stable. At the same time, we affirmed the 'BBB-/A-3' long- and \par short-term foreign and local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  sovereign credit ratings. \par  \par We also affirmed our 'kzAAA' long-term national scale rating on Kazakhstan. \par  \par The transfer and convertibility (T&C) assessment remains 'BBB'. \par  \par OUTLOOK \par The negative outlook reflects risks to Kazakhstan's oil exports through the \par CPC pipeline, as well as rising debt financing costs. \par  \par Downside scenario \par We could lower the ratings over the next two years if Kazakhstan's oil exports \par declined significantly for a prolonged period, weakening the economy's \par external position and increasing fiscal deficits. This could be the case, for \par instance, if the CPC pipeline's loading capacity were incapacitated for an \par extended period. We could also lower the ratings if inflationary pressures and \par rising borrowing costs continued to increase the government's debt-servicing \par burden. \par  \par Further disruptive factors, such as a deterioration in domestic stability, \par evidenced by incidents of severe civil unrest, could also lead to a downgrade. \par Upside scenario \par We could revise the outlook to stable if the medium-term fiscal trajectory \par improves more than we expect, reducing the pace of debt accumulation and \par debt-servicing requirements. This could be the case, for example, if the \par government materially increases its non-oil revenue base or lowers spending. \par  \par RATIONALE \par The rating action reflects risks to Kazakhstan's oil exports through the CPC \par pipeline, as well as rising government debt financing costs. \par  \par There have been at least four incidents this year affecting the country's main \par oil export artery, the CPC pipeline, through which Kazakhstan exports about \par 80% of its oil production. The pipeline connects Kazakhstan's Tengiz, \par Kashagan, and Karachaganak oil fields to the Black Sea port of Novorossiysk in \par Russia. These incidents include storm-related damage in March that reduced \par exports for almost a month; safety inspections for World War II-era deep-sea \par mines in June; a Russian court ruling in July to stop loading for 30 days due \par to alleged violations of the pipeline's oil spill plan, which was overturned a \par few days later and replaced with a minor fine; and most recently, subsea \par cracks discovered on buoyancy tanks that could limit loading on two out of \par three CPC single-point moorings for around a month. As a result, we expect \par Kazakh oil production of 85.6 million tons per year (equivalent to about 1.8 \par million barrels per day [bbl/day]); this is lower than our previous estimate \par of 87.5 million tons for 2022. \par  \par In our view, geopolitical tensions caused by the Russia-Ukraine conflict could \par be the source of potential disruptions at the CPC pipeline (see "Kazakhstan \par Remains Vulnerable To Russian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Over CPC Pipeline," published on \par July 13, 2022, on RatingsDirect). About 90% of oil flowing through the CPC \par pipeline originates from Kazakhstan (representing 54 million tons or about 1 \par million bbl/day), while the remaining 10% is Russian. Alternative export \par routes to the CPC pipeline can replace less than a third of its capacity and \par are more logistically challenging and expensive. Against the backdrop of the \par uncertain geopolitical environment, in our view, Russia could disrupt flows \par through the pipeline to gain leverage against Kazakhstan or the consumers of \par the oil, namely countries in Europe that support Ukraine, or to hurt Western \par interests in the Kazakh oil sector (shareholders of the pipeline and oil \par fields include \f28 \uc2 \f2 Chevron\f28 \uc2 \f2  , \f28 \uc2 \f2 ExxonMobil\f28 \uc2 \f2  , \f28 \uc2 \f2 Shell\f28 \uc2 \f2  , and \f28 \uc2 \f2 TotalEnergies\f28 \uc2 \f2  ). In such an \par event, disruptions at the pipeline could be unrelated to Russia's relationship \par with Kazakhstan, but damaging nonetheless. \par  \par Oil is the key economic sector in Kazakhstan, directly accounting for about \par 15% of GDP, more than 30% of general government revenue, and over half of \par exports. We expect Brent oil prices will average about $100 per barrel (/bbl) \par in 2022, $85/bbl in 2023, and $55/bbl from 2024 (see "S&P Global Ratings \par Raises Oil And Natural Gas Price Assumptions On Further Market Price \par Step-Ups," published June 8, 2022). Despite high oil prices in the near term, \par upside to the external and fiscal accounts could be limited due to stagnant \par oil production. \par  \par In addition, the government's fiscal response this year to the civil unrest in \par early January could make it more challenging to curb spending growth from \par 2023. Meanwhile, we expect lower oil prices from 2024 will likely weigh on \par revenue. We anticipate the government's net asset position will turn into a \par small debtor position from 2022, largely due to rising debt. At the same time, \par government borrowing costs are increasing, given the rising interest rates and \par reliance on domestic debt issuances. We project that interest as a proportion \par of revenue will average 9.8% over 2022-2025. \par  \par The ratings on Kazakhstan are supported by the country's strong fiscal and \par external balance sheets. These were built primarily with budgetary surpluses \par generated during the period of high commodity prices that ended in late 2014. \par The related assets were accumulated in the National Fund of the Republic of \par Kazakhstan (NFRK) and largely invested abroad. We forecast that the economy's \par liquid external assets will exceed external debt through 2025. \par  \par Institutional and economic profile: GDP growth averaging 3.6% over 2022-2025 \par will be supported by both the oil and non-oil sectors \par      -- We expect spillovers from the Russia-Ukraine war will keep inflation \par elevated and could slow the pace of fiscal consolidation. \par      -- We expect President \f28 \uc2 \f2 Kassym-Jomart Tokayev\f28 \uc2 \f2   will likely maintain his \par position following elections and any political reforms to be modest. \par      -- Diversification of the economy, developing the private sector, and \par increasing the population's wealth levels will remain policy priorities. \par  \par In our view, the impact of the Russia-Ukraine conflict has thus far been \par moderate for the country's economic growth and banking sector. However, Russia \par is Kazakhstan's largest trading partner and a key source of imports. Russian \par imports made up about 35% of total Kazakhstan's imports during the first \par quarter of 2022, while exports to Russia make up about 9% of the total. Export \par bans imposed by Russia on sugar and grains and depreciation of the Kazakhstan \par tenge (KZT) against the Russian ruble have contributed to rising food prices \par in Kazakhstan, driving inflation to 15% year on year in July. The secondary \par hit from sanctions imposed on Russia could also make it more difficult for \par local companies to trade in certain sectors, although we understand that \par informal trade has likely increased given the free flow of goods and people \par between the two countries. Kazakhstan mostly exports iron ore and uranium to \par Russia while importing cars, semi-finished goods, coal, and refined petroleum. \par The \f28 \uc2 \f2 U.S. government\f28 \uc2 \f2   explicitly excluded Kazakh-origin crude oil from the CPC \par pipeline from its sanctions related to the prohibition of dealing in oil of \par Russian origin. \par  \par Kazakhstan--along with Armenia, Belarus, Kyrgyzstan, and Russia--is a member \par of the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Eurasian Economic Union\f28 \uc2 \f2  , a customs union that provides tariff-free \par trade and uniform custom tariffs for trade with non-member states. \f28 \uc2 \f2 The \par Eurasian Economic Union\f28 \uc2 \f2   ensures the free movement of goods, services, labor, \par and capital between the states, and since mid-March it announced the use of \par the ruble for reimbursement of customs fees with Russia and Belarus. \par  \par Russia is also a key source of investment, accounting directly for about 8% of \par Kazakhstan's total external liabilities, although this figure could be larger \par since investment in its oil, gas, and mining sectors is often recorded through \par the Netherlands and the U.K. For Kazakhstan, unlike other Central Asian \par sovereigns, remittances from Russia are not an important source of income. \par  \par Although Kazakhstan is a member of the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Collective Security Treaty Organization\f28 \uc2 \f2   \par (\f28 \uc2 \f2 CSTO\f28 \uc2 \f2  ), a mutual defense organization with members from ex-Soviet states, we \par view it as unlikely that it will be directly involved in the Russia-Ukraine \par war. The government has not recognized the breakaway Luhansk People's Republic \par or the Donetsk People's Republic, and it has abstained on the U.N . vote to \par condemn the invasion of Ukraine, affirming its policy of neutrality, similar \par to when Russia annexed Crimea. In response to the civil unrest in January, the \par government requested assistance from the \f28 \uc2 \f2 CSTO\f28 \uc2 \f2  , and peacekeeping troops, mostly \par from Russia, arrived quickly to help restore order. Should a similar situation \par occur in the future, we think it is unclear whether \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Russia\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  would have the \par resources or willingness to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in Kazakhstan again, without some \par reciprocation in terms of Kazakhstan indicating more support for Russia's \par foreign policy. \par  \par In early January, protests quickly spread throughout Kazakhstan. They were \par ignited by an increase in the price of liquified petroleum gas (LPG) but \par expanded to encompass broader grievances, including persistent inequality, low \par wages and living standards, aging infrastructure, constraints on political \par representation, and the perceived high level of corruption. Almaty--the \par largest city--saw the most unrest and damage. \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 2 Sep 2022 16:09 ET \par Press Release: S&PGR Revises Kazakhstan Outlook -2-\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In response to the protests, President Tokayev announced a series of economic \par and governance reforms. The government reinstituted caps on gasoline, LPG, and \par utilities prices for six months. The price controls for lower quality gasoline \par were then extended until the end of the year, while tariff increases will be \par tiered based on consumption. The president also announced institutional \par changes to reduce the influence of former President Nazarbayev and his family \par on politics and business, increase plurality and decentralize power away from \par the president to some extent, and improve the competitiveness and social role \par of the large state-owned enterprises, such as \f28 \uc2 \f2 Samruk Kazyna\f28 \uc2 \f2  . \par  \par We expect political reforms will be gradual and modest in nature. In our view, \par decision-making will stay centralized, while government checks and balances \par are limited, and there is no credible political opposition party. Moreover, \par limitations on independent media outlets are significant and perceived \par corruption is high, with Kazakhstan ranking 102nd in \f28 \uc2 \f2 Transparency \par International\f28 \uc2 \f2  's Corruption Perceptions Index. \par  \par Economic reforms in response to the unrest largely continue prior efforts. The \par government aims to diversify the economy through capital investment programs, \par including infrastructure and housing projects. The pool of investment projects \par under consideration amount to an estimated KZT30 trillion (33% of 2022 GDP). \par The government is also focusing on cultivating the investment climate, \par reducing state participation in the economy, increasing private-sector \par competition, improving tax policy, reforming public procurement, and reducing \par administrative barriers for businesses. Although we believe the government's \par prudent and generally effective macroeconomic policymaking will continue, we \par do not anticipate that the broad scope of reforms will bring about significant \par changes to the macroeconomic environment. \par  \par We expect economic growth will slow to 3.0% this year, from 4.1% in 2021, \par largely due to stagnant oil production and the impact of high inflation on \par consumption. The economy expanded an estimated 3.3% year on year over \par January-July 2022, supported by growth in construction, information and \par communications, trade, transport, and manufacturing. Government stimulus \par measures have continued since 2019, including higher spending on development \par projects, defense, wages, social security, and education. \par  \par Over the next three years, we expect growth will average just under 4% \par annually. A key driver will be the expansion of the Tengiz oil field that \par should significantly increase oil production. In the absence of disruptions to \par Kazakhstan's oil exports, we expect oil production could exceed 100 million \par tons per year by 2025, from 85.8 million in 2021. Ongoing investment projects \par and efforts to attract foreign investment should also support strong non-oil \par sector growth. \par  \par Flexibility and performance profile: Although government debt remains modest, \par the interest bill is rising \par      -- High oil prices will support strong revenue outcomes in 2022-2023, but \par higher spending this year could be difficult to reverse. \par      -- We expect Kazakhstan's external asset position will remain strong, \par with liquid external assets exceeding external debt by 28% of current account \par payments by 2025. \par      -- Inflation averaged 12% year on year over January-July, above the \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 National Bank of Kazakhstan\f28 \uc2 \f2  's (\f28 \uc2 \f2 NBK\f28 \uc2 \f2  's) target range of 4%-6%, amid expansionary \par fiscal policy and higher imported prices. \par  \par We expect the consolidated general government balance will reach a deficit of \par 3.8% of GDP in 2022, down from 4.6% in 2021. We project that revenue will \par increase by nearly 17% this year, backed by higher oil and other commodity \par prices driving corporate income taxes. At the same time, we expect government \par spending to rise by 11%. To address public grievances from the recent protests \par and mitigate the effects of inflation, the government is prioritizing the use \par of the revenue windfall to increase current and capital spending. \par  \par We expect deficits will decline to 1.3% of GDP on average over 2023-2025 on \par the back of efforts to broaden the revenue base and moderate spending growth. \par The government will implement certain fiscal rules from 2023 to limit growth \par in spending and transfers from the NFRK to the budget based on oil prices. \par That said, spending will likely stay elevated as social priorities and \par reliance on government spending to boost economic growth could continue to \par steer fiscal policy in an uncertain external environment. Despite strong \par growth in non-oil revenue after the introduction of a new tax code in 2017, a \par large share (30%) of fiscal revenue will remain dependent on the oil sector \par over our forecast period to 2025. \par  \par The government's strong balance sheet underpins the rating, with relatively \par large liquid assets. We estimate net debt at 0.4% of GDP in 2022. This has \par deteriorated from a net asset position of nearly 40% of GDP in 2015. The value \par of total NFRK assets declined to about $53 billion (27% of GDP) in July from \par $73 billion at year-end 2014, as oil prices came down from historical highs \par and transfers from the fund to the government were elevated over this period. \par Implementation of a fiscal rule limiting transfers from the NFRK to the budget \par and expanding oil production volumes should help increase assets to about $71 \par billion by year-end 2025, as per our forecasts. However, we project that net \par government debt will continue on an upward trend, reaching 2.8% of GDP by \par 2025. \par  \par We expect gross government debt will rise to 33% of GDP at year-end 2022 from \par 25% at year-end 2019, because of increased funding needs and tenge \par depreciation. About 40% of debt is external and denominated in foreign \par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 --of this, about 60% is commercial and the rest is mostly from \par multilateral lenders. In the past two years, the government has increased \par borrowing from multilateral lenders and issued in the ruble market, because of \par what had been a natural \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  hedge between the tenge and ruble, in our \par opinion. \par  \par Despite the relatively low debt, interest expenditure is rising partly because \par of the growing proportion of domestic debt issuances. We expect interest costs \par will rise further given monetary tightening; average yields on short-term \par local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  government debt had risen to 14% in July from about 10% on \par average in 2021. We expect the interest-to-revenue ratio will increase to \par 11.0% by 2025, relative to 5.4% in 2019. \par  \par Higher oil prices should lead to the first current account surplus since 2014. \par The strong trade balance will partly be offset by net outflows in the primary \par income account, spurred by profit repatriation on foreign direct investment \par related to oil production. We expect that falling oil prices and rising \par imports will revert the current account to deficits averaging just under 2% of \par GDP over 2023-2025. \par  \par Kazakhstan's external asset position is strong, and we expect gross external \par financing needs will stay below 100% of current account receipts and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign \par exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves. Reserve assets at the \f28 \uc2 \f2 NBK\f28 \uc2 \f2   decreased slightly to about $33 \par billion in July from $34 billion at year-end 2021. About two-thirds of \par reserves are gold. The \f28 \uc2 \f2 NBK\f28 \uc2 \f2   purchases gold with domestic \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , providing \par the government a way to increase reserves despite current account deficits. We \par forecast reserves will remain broadly stable despite foreign direct investment \par inflows, partly because of the revaluation effect of lower gold prices. S&P \par Global Ratings forecasts gold prices of $1,600 per ounce (/oz) in 2022 and \par $1,400/oz over 2023-2025, compared with $1,829/oz in 2021 (see "S&P Global \par Ratings' Metal Price Assumptions: Prices Settle Lower As Slowdown Fears Grow," \par published Aug. 8, 2022). \par  \par In our view, the more freely floating exchange rate, following the sharp \par devaluation of the tenge in 2015, has helped the economy adjust to external \par pressures. The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  depreciated by nearly 18% at its peak in mid-March \par compared to end-2021, driven by the risk-off sentiment following Russia's \par invasion of Ukraine. The tenge had recovered a significant part of its losses \par by end-March, and we expect it will depreciate by 9% on average over 2022. \par Ongoing current account deficits and the government's plan to reduce transfers \par from the NFRK, a key tenge purchaser in the market, will weigh on the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  \par over the forecast period to 2025. \par  \par Soaring inflation in 2022 has led the \f28 \uc2 \f2 NBK\f28 \uc2 \f2   to raise the base rate by a total of \par 475 basis points (bps) thus far to 14.5%. We expect inflation will average 13% \par this year, owing to the government's accommodative fiscal stance, higher \par import prices (especially on food), and early withdrawals of savings from the \par government pension scheme by eligible citizens for use in housing, medical \par services, or transfers to private asset managers. We expect inflation will \par slow to about 5% by 2025 because of improvements to inflation targeting, the \par higher base rate, and expected slower tenge depreciation. \par  \par To increase independence and effectiveness of its monetary policy framework, \par the \f28 \uc2 \f2 NBK\f28 \uc2 \f2   announced that it will not take part in new economic lending and \par support programs and will exit from all such programs by 2023. Out of the \par seven initial programs, only two are still running and will be phased out by \par next year: a mortgage program and financing to the Economy of Simple Things \par program. The \f28 \uc2 \f2 NBK\f28 \uc2 \f2   remains a shareholder of oil and gas company KazMunayGas NC \par JSC, with ownership of 9.8%. \par  \par The \f28 \uc2 \f2 NBK\f28 \uc2 \f2   is also working with the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to build out a yield \par curve for local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  debt to improve liquidity in the domestic secondary \par market. To increase transparency in the tenge market, the \f28 \uc2 \f2 NBK\f28 \uc2 \f2   began publishing \par all sales of U.S. dollars from the NFRK. The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  also improved its \par focus on macroeconomic stability in 2019 by spinning off financial sector \par regulation to the newly created Agency for Regulation and Development of \par Financial Markets, which focuses on financial system stability. \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 2 Sep 2022 16:09 ET \par Press Release: S&PGR Revises Kazakhstan Outlook -3-\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Financial stability risks from the Russia-Ukraine conflict have been \par manageable thus far, in our view. The three Russian banks that made up about \par 14% of systemwide deposits, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Sberbank\f28 \uc2 \f2  , \f28 \uc2 \f2 VTB Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2  , and \f28 \uc2 \f2 Alfa Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2  , have rapidly \par downsized their \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in Kazakhstan to about 2% of the deposit base (see \par "Financial Stability Risks Stemming From Sanctioned Russian Bank Subsidiaries \par In Kazakhstan Have Been Limited," published July 7, 2022). This has been \par accomplished through acquisitions and the partial sale of the loan portfolio \par to other banks. \par  \par We expect that banks' asset-quality indicators may be temporarily constrained, \par partly due to the spillover effects from a recession that we expect in Russia \par this year. We also think that the changing operating environment, due to a \par reshuffling of the political class, could affect the competitive landscape, \par since many banks remain reliant on their owners' business and political \par connections. We currently classify Kazakhstan in group '9' under our Banking \par Industry Country Risk Assessment, on a 1-10 scale with '10' being the weakest \par score (see "Ratings On Eight Kazakhstani Banks Affirmed Under Revised Criteria \par Despite Elevated Uncertainties; Outlooks Unchanged," published Jan. 26, 2022). \par The local banking sector is relatively small with the overall loan portfolio \par accounting for only 26% of GDP in 2021, reducing the efficiency of financial \par intermediation on the economy. We consider contingent liability risks to the \par government from the banking system as limited. \par  \par KEY STATISTICS \par  \par RELATED CRITERIA \par  \par      -- General Criteria: Environmental, Social, And Governance Principles In \par Credit Ratings, Oct. 10, 2021 \par      -- General Criteria: Methodology For National And Regional Scale Credit \par Ratings, June 25, 2018 \par      -- Criteria | Governments | Sovereigns: Sovereign Rating Methodology, \par Dec. 18, 2017 \par      -- General Criteria: Methodology For Linking Long-Term And Short-Term \par Ratings, April 7, 2017 \par      -- General Criteria: Principles Of Credit Ratings, Feb. 16, 2011 \par