\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Currency \highlight\uc2  Trading: Strong Ruble Hurts Oil-and-Gas Revenue -- WSJ\b0\par\par\uc2 728 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 Juni 2022\par 08:34\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par A9\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2022, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 The Russian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has risen 41% against the dollar this year, weakening the value of the country's oil-and-gas revenues \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par By Caitlin McCabe and Kate Vtorygina \f28 \par \par \uc2 After its first default on international debt in a century amid a grinding war with Ukraine, one might expect Russia's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to be suffering. But Russia has the opposite problem.\par \par \uc2 The Russian ruble has soared to a roughly seven-year high against the U.S. dollar -- an astounding turnaround for a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  that earlier this year was in free fall after Russia invaded Ukraine. The ruble on Wednesday had risen 42% against the dollar this year, making it the best performer against the greenback, according to a Dow Jones Market Data analysis of 56 \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 That strength has come with a cost. A strong ruble threatens to hit Russia's budget by reducing the value of oil-and-gas tax revenues that are denominated in dollars.\par \par \uc2 The ruble "really is too strong for Russia right now," said Liam Peach, an emerging markets economist at \uc2 Capital Economics\uc2 , who said that with the ruble nearly twice as strong as it was three months ago, Russia is now receiving roughly half as much in oil-and-gas tax revenue in ruble terms. "The damage that this is likely to continue doing to the public finances has probably made policy makers quite concerned."\par \par \uc2 On Wednesday, Russian Finance Minister \uc2 Anton Siluanov\uc2  unveiled a new proposal to counter the ruble's gains: buying the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  of so-called friendly countries to help influence the exchange rate of the ruble and against the dollar and euro.\par \par \uc2 With Western sanctions against \b \highlight22\uc2 Russia\b0 \highlight\uc2  blocking the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's ability to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  traditionally in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, Mr. Siluanov's proposal marks the latest attempt to stabilize the ruble as the country grapples with the effects from its war in Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 "The issue is that Russia can't simply sell rubles and purchase dollars -- there are sanctions on the holdings and transactions from the \uc2 Russian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," Mr. Peach said. "I think that's why they're now trying to do this with friendly \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ."\par \par \uc2 Four months ago, Russia was facing a far different problem: Its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was too weak. In late February, the ruble cratered in the immediate aftermath of the country's invasion of Ukraine. Russian officials were forced to scramble to implement capital controls to prop the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  up.\par \par \uc2 Throughout February and March, Moscow took drastic measures. \uc2 Russia's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  limited the amount of dollars that Russians could withdraw from foreign-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  bank accounts. Moscow initially required companies to change 80% of their foreign-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  revenues into rubles. And the country doubled its key interest rate to 20%, essentially rewarding people for holding rubles.\par \par \uc2 The capital controls worked and pushed the ruble higher. Meanwhile, Russia's commodity exports, which were boosted by high prices, gave the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  further upward momentum. At its worst during the war, the ruble in early March reached a record intraday low of about 158 rubles to the dollar, according to data from Tullett Prebon. On Wednesday, it finished at nearly 53 rubles to the dollar.\par \par \uc2 But Russia in recent months has been taking steps to weaken the ruble, including loosening capital controls and cutting the country's key interest rate -- efforts that have yet to pay off.\par \par \uc2 Now, Russia is considering deploying excess oil-and-gas revenues to purchase "friendly" country \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to try to influence the exchange rate of the ruble against the dollar and euro. Speaking during a meeting of the \uc2 Russian Union of Industrialists and Entrepreneurs\uc2 , a Russian lobby group, Mr. Siluanov said the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  would discuss the idea with the government's economic bloc.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Siluanov didn't specify which \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  Russia could buy. Earlier in the day, the ruble pared its gains and then turned lower in offshore trading Wednesday after Mr. Siluanov's comments. The Russian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  recovered to finish 2.4% higher for the day.\par \par \uc2 Write to Caitlin McCabe at caitlin.mccabe@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 This article is being republished as part of our daily reproduction of WSJ.com articles that also appeared in the U.S. print edition of The Wall Street Journal (June 30, 2022).\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 June 30, 2022 02:34 ET (06:34 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020220630ei6u000o7\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
