\par\par \uc2 CE Noticias Financieras English\par \b \uc2 Italian oil company \uc2 Eni\uc2  to open ruble account to pay for Russian gas\b0\par\par\uc2 592 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 Mai 2022\par \uc2 CE NoticiasFinancieras\par \uc2 NFINCE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 Content Engine LLC \par \par \uc2 Italian oil company \uc2 Eni\uc2  announced Tuesday night the forthcoming opening of an account in euros and another in rubles at the Russian bank \uc2 Gazprombank\uc2  to pay for gas supplies arriving from that country, thus bowing to Moscow's demands and opening a crack within the \uc2 European Union\uc2 , opposed to that mechanism. "The payment obligation can be fulfilled by transferring euros" and that procedure "should not be incompatible with the sanctions" imposed by the \uc2 European Union\uc2  (EU) against Russia in response to the invasion of Ukraine, the company said in a statement.\par \par \uc2 Read moreFollow the latest on the war in UkraineTheItalian group's decision has consequences at the European level and would grant a small victory to Russian President \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  in his strategy of division. The energy company, 30.3% controlled by the Italian state, goes against the recommendations of the \uc2 European Commission\uc2 , which considers that opening an account in rubles violates sanctions and could lead to infringement proceedings by Brussels. "Anything that goes beyond the guidelines" given by the Commission to member states "would be a violation of sanctions," Eric Mamer, spokesman for the \uc2 European Commission\uc2 , said hours before \uc2 Eni\uc2 's announcement. "Member states have taken a collective decision to impose a certain number of sanctions, so it is quite normal that member states have an obligation to enforce this decision that has the force of law in the \uc2 European Union\uc2 . And if the Member States fail to do so, the general framework of infringement procedures that can be applied by the \uc2 European Commission\uc2  applies," Mamer threatened.\par \par \uc2 Italy is heavily dependent on Russian gas - 43% of that energy source comes from that country - and, at present, could not afford a supply cut-off, as Putin has already threatened could happen (Poland and Bulgaria have already suffered). Prime Minister \uc2 Mario Draghi\uc2  has been working hard to secure new suppliers. But the situation is still very complicated. The government, consulted by this newspaper, does not want to comment on the decision and leaves the communication of this matter in \uc2 Eni\uc2 's hands. But some sources recall that Draghi, who had referred to this problem as "the grey area of sanctions", has asked the \uc2 European Commission\uc2  on several occasions to clarify this matter without receiving a clear answer.\par \par \uc2 The Italian company further claims that the Russian authorities have confirmed that "invoicing and payment will continue to be made in euros", which is the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  stated in the contracts. Subsequently, they have been told, "an operator of the Moscow Stock Exchange will carry out the conversion into rubles within 48 hours without the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Russia\b0 \highlight\uc2 ". An element that \uc2 Eni\uc2  wields as mitigating the European guidelines when a Kremlin decree introduced the new two-stage payment procedure: with a payment into a \uc2 Gazprombank\uc2  account in euros or dollars and then conversion into rubles in a second account at the same institution.\par \par \uc2 Initially, this conversion mechanism involved a transaction with the \uc2 Russian \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which the \uc2 EU\uc2  sanctions regime prohibits. Although Brussels said several times that this ruble conversion mechanism is a way to circumvent \uc2 EU\uc2  sanctions, several member states that want to maintain supplies asked the \uc2 European Commission\uc2  for clarification. Italy was one of them and, sources close to the Executive insist, there was no clear answer. Draghi, as an alternative, had proposed setting a ceiling on the price of methane.\par \par \uc2 Follow all the international news on \uc2 Facebook\uc2  and \uc2 Twitter\uc2 , or in our weekly newsletter.\par \par \uc2 Dokument NFINCE0020220518ei5i005gb\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw103\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal1545\pichgoal600
