\par\par \b \uc2 Global  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2  and Fixed Income Roundup: Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,504 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 April 2022\par 23:58\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2022, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 The latest Market Talks covering \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  and Fixed Income. Published exclusively on Dow Jones Newswires throughout the day.\par \par \uc2 1758 ET - S&P Global Ratings said it expects U.K. GDP growth of 3.5% this year, below its previous forecast of 4.6% made in December of last year. "Upward price pressures, aggravated by the fallout from \b \highlight22\uc2 Russia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's military \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  against Ukraine, are driving real incomes lower," S&P said. The U.K. is a net energy importer, and exposed to the conflict's impact on oil and gas prices, according to S&P. Inflation in March hit a 30-year high for the U.K., coming in at 7% year-on-year, S&P said. The agency also said it affirmed its 'AA/A-1+'sovereign credit ratings on the U.K with a stable outlook. (stephen.nakrosis@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1731 ET - S&P Global Ratings said it revised down Italy's GDP growth projection for 2022 to 3.1% from 4.4%. The downward revision is a consequence of the ongoing conflict in Ukraine, S&P said. "The Russia-Ukraine conflict poses a risk to Italy's open industrialized economy via trade, supply chains, oil and gas prices, and confidence effects," S&P said. Even as inflation hit 30-year highs in Italy, S&P said it believes "the government's ongoing delivery of key structural and fiscal reforms will boost long-term potential growth and, ultimately, benefit Italy's public finances." S&P affirmed its unsolicited 'BBB' long-term and 'A-2' short-term foreign and local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  sovereign credit ratings on Italy, with a positive outlook. (stephen.nakrosis@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1654 ET - S&P Global Ratings upgraded its long-term sovereign credit rating on Greece to BB+ from BB with a stable outlook. S&P said the upgrade reflects it expectation of continuous improvement in Greece's policy effectiveness. Fallout from the war in Ukraine appears manageable, S&P said, but it also said the war is the key driver of its projection Greek GDP growth will slow to 3.4% in 2022, from 8.3% last year. Inflation was 8% in March, a 27-year high, but there is no evidence so far of upward pressure on wages, according to S&P. The agency also affirmed Greece's short-term rating at B. (stephen.nakrosis@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1645 ET - The IPC stock index closes down 0.1% at 53191 points with conglomerate \uc2 Femsa\uc2  shares off 1.1%. \uc2 Grupo Financiero Banorte\uc2  jumps 8.2% after the group reports a 26% rise in 1Q net profit. Performing loans grew 4.2% on the year, including a 6.3% rise in consumer loans, but 2022 loan-growth guidance is cut to 5%-7% from 7%-9%. \uc2 Kimberly-Clark de Mexico\uc2  shares rise 2.4%. The consumer products company had record 1Q sales, up 4% on the year, while higher raw materials costs contributed to a 22% decline in Ebitda. "Margins still a ways from prepandemic levels," analysts with \uc2 Barclays\uc2  say in a note, although "the company did produce quarter-on-quarter improvement." The peso weakens to 20.24 against the US dollar from 20.17 yesterday as prospects of more aggressive Fed tightening fuel dollar gains. (anthony.harrup@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1635 ET - Canadian stocks sink, with financials, industrials and materials posting some of the largest sector declines. The benchmark S&P/TSX Composite Index drops 2.1% to 21186.38, ending the week down 3.1%, according to \uc2 FactSet\uc2  . The blue-chip S&P TSX 60 Index slides 2.2% to 1281.87, ending the week down 3%. (maria.armental@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1626 ET - The WSJ Dollar Index is on track to gain 1.1% this week and accumulate a 2.7% increase in three weeks, which would be the largest three-week gain since March 2020. Capital Economics' Jonathan Petersen says in a research note that the equities selloff is behind the recent appreciation this time around. "The dollar is in a 'heads I win, tails you lose' situation," he says. "Either US yields rise further and widening yield gaps help the dollar, or risk appetite worsens on worries about the economic outlook and that drives dollar strength." The USD gains 2% versus the Australian dollar and 1.5% to the pound sterling. (paulo.trevisani@wsj.com; @ptrevisani)\par \par \uc2 1555 ET - The selloff on \uc2 US government\uc2  debt continued this week, sending the 10-year yield up 0.097 percentage point to 2.905%, its fourth consecutive weekly rise, despite falling 0.012 p.p. today. Investors are increasingly confident that the Fed will raise rates by a steep 50-basis point next month. \uc2 Piper Sandler\uc2  's \uc2 Roberto Perli\uc2  tells WSJ the rise in yields signal that inflation expectations could get out of control and become a headache for the Fed. "History has shown that every time the Fed has to raise rates aggressively, growth suffers,' he says. "The risk of recession five to six quarters ahead is high." (paulo.trevisani@wsj.com; @ptrevisani)\par \par \uc2 1509 ET - The chorus of analysts who expect the \uc2 Bank of Canada\uc2  to deliver another mammoth interest rate increase grew after data on Wednesday showed Canada's annual inflation hit a fresh three-decade high, at 6.7%. \uc2 Bank of Canada\uc2  Gov. \uc2 Tiff Macklem\uc2  told reporters the following day that he was ready to be "as forceful as needed," in normalizing monetary policy. While many analysts now expect another half-percentage point increase in June, \uc2 Desjardins Securities\uc2  said Macklem's comments, coupled with this week's inflation report, suggest the \uc2 BoC\uc2  could go even further and raise rates by three-quarters of a percentage point during its next meeting. (kim.mackrael@wsj.com; @kimmackrael)\par \par \uc2 1256 ET - Diverging interest rates will keep the Japanese yen weak and drive the USD/JPY to 140 by the end of this year, "before dropping back as the Fed takes its foot off the gas in 2023," Capital Economics' \uc2 Jonas Goltermann\uc2  says in a research note. Goltermann says the yen has depreciated around 10% against the dollar since early March, mainly because the \uc2 BoJ\uc2  "has stuck to its ultra-loose policy stance," while the Fed is aggressively tightening. Japan's terms of trade "have also worsened significantly on account of the surge in commodity prices," Goltermann says, warning that the \uc2 BoJ\uc2  is under pressure to change its policy, which could support the yen. (paulo.trevisani@wsj.com; @ptrevisani)\par \par \uc2 1127 ET - \uc2 The People's Bank of China\uc2  may welcome a potential further depreciation of the Chinese renminbi, Capital Economics economist Mark Williams says in a research note. The recent rise in USD/CNY has further to go as yield differentials move against the renminbi, consumer demand weakens in key export markets and inventory restocking in many sectors potentially runs its course, he says. The \uc2 PBOC\uc2  has good reason to embrace a gradual depreciation of the renminbi, Williams says. "In an environment of weaker global demand, the renminbi's current strength would increasingly hold exporters back." \uc2 Capital Economics\uc2  raises its year-end USD/CNY forecast to 7.00 from 6.70. USD/CNY rises 0.7% to 6.4971 after earlier hitting a near nine-month high of 6.5067.(renae.dyer@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1114 ET - The euro could rise toward $1.15 from $1.0805 currently if the \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  raises interest rates by more than market expectations to tame inflation, Unicredit Research says. The \uc2 ECB\uc2  could raise its deposit facility rate 100 basis points above the 1.5% peak that is priced in by markets over a two-year horizon, \uc2 Unicredit\b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist Roberto Mialich says in a research note. That could prompt the \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to favor more pronounced EUR/USD long positioning, or bets on the pair price rising, he says. "The intensity of the EUR rally would also depend on the extent to which high inflation also affects the U.S. economy, possibly forcing the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  to deliver even more tightening than that already priced in," Mialich says. (renae.dyer@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1108 ET - The rise in Mexican inflation to a new 21-year high in early April, coupled with worsening inflation expectations and hawkish signals from the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  support another 50bp rise in the \uc2 Bank of Mexico\uc2  's policy interest rate to 7% at the May 12 meeting, \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  chief Latin America economist Alberto Ramos says in a report. Annual CPI stood at 7.72% mid-month, with core CPI up 7.16%. Ramos notes that supercore inflation -- which tracks prices most sensitive to demand conditions -- rose to 5.81% from 5.38% in the second half of March. "This is likely to keep the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  conservative in the calibration of monetary policy despite the weak real activity backdrop," he says. (anthony.harrup@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 April 22, 2022 17:58 ET (21:58 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020220422ei4m004pj\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw103\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal1545\pichgoal600
