\par\par \uc2 CE Noticias Financieras English\par \b \uc2 Bank of Russia admits sanctions hit real economy\b0\par\par\uc2 398 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 April 2022\par \uc2 CE NoticiasFinancieras\par \uc2 NFINCE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 Content Engine LLC \par \par \uc2 Beyond the political rhetoric, Western sanctions are starting to hurt the Russian economy. The governor of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , \uc2 Elvira Nabiullina\uc2 , noted that they first hit the financial sector, but now they will have "a bigger impact on the economy in general". In her opinion, "the main problems will be associated with import restrictions, foreign trade logistics and, in the future, possible restrictions on the export of Russian products," she warned.\par \par \uc2 "The imposition of sanctions caused strong volatility in the market and this was coupled with the nervousness of the population, so that at the beginning of March, the outflow of citizens' funds amounted to 2.4 trillion rubles(27.357 billion euros)," the governor admitted.\par \par \uc2 "The period in which we can live on reserves is finite," Governor \uc2 Elvira Nabiullina\uc2  acknowledged.In her speech, Nabiullina sent another message: Russia's ability to save is limited. "The period when the economy can live on reserves is finite. And already in the second and third quarter we will enter a period of structural transformation and search for new business models," she acknowledged.\par \par \uc2 Long before the Ukrainian invasion, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  began to reduce its exposure to the dollar, bringing its share over the last decade to just under 11% of its total reserves at the end of last year. This would have allowed it, as planned by the Kremlin, to have a large volume of foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  with which to sustain the military onslaught. But more than a third of the total was in euros, plus additional investments in \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  such as sterling and the yen.\par \par \uc2 And this made it possible for international governments to seize about half of the reserves in retaliation for President \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 's attack. Restrictions imposed on the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Russia\b0 \highlight\uc2  prevented the entity from \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market to defend the ruble, forcing it to impose capital controls and raise the emergency interest rate to calm the markets.\par \par \uc2 Its reserves peaked at $643.2 billion on February 18 and then declined sharply, before stabilizing. Moscow wants to go on the counterattack: as half of them cannot be used, it plans to take legal action by blocking gold, foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and assets belonging to Russian citizens, although, as \uc2 Elvira Nabiullina\uc2  said, "the measure would have to be carefully thought out and legally justified."\par \par \uc2 Dokument NFINCE0020220419ei4i000cd\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw103\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal1545\pichgoal600
