\par\par \uc2 CE Noticias Financieras English\par \b \uc2 Economic sanctions: an overused but effective weapon?\b0\par\par\uc2 970 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 April 2022\par \uc2 CE NoticiasFinancieras\par \uc2 NFINCE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 Content Engine LLC \par \par \uc2 In recent weeks, international sanctions have shone as one of the weapons most resorted to by the West to confront Russia and try to persuade Moscow to end its invasion of Ukraine, which began on February 24. But the invasion continues, raising questions about the effectiveness of this strategy. "We have never talked so much about sanctions, it has become a common theme," lawyer Olivier Dorgans of the Ashurst law firm, whose job is to advise Western companies involved in the Russian market in the face of the tangle of sanctions, tells AFP.\par \par \uc2 Russia, the country with the most sanctions Sanctions have been piling up as Russian tanks advanced in Ukraine: exclusion of several banks from the Swift interbank system, freezing of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  assets and oligarchs, export restrictions and embargo on coal. The first rounds of sanctions began on February 22, the day after Putin announced that Russia would recognize the independence of the rebel regions of Donetsk and Lugansk in eastern Ukraine. Russia then had 2,754 sanctions, making it already the second most sanctioned country in the world, behind only Iran, according to data from Castellum.ai, a sanctions monitoring website. Since that day, Russia has accumulated 6,445 sanctions from \uc2 G7\uc2  members, the \uc2 European Union\uc2 , Switzerland and Australia. This brings the total to 9,199 sanctions, nearly triple the 3,616 sanctions on Iran. The \uc2 EU\uc2  is preparing a sixth round of penalties, almost seven weeks after the start of the war. Russian President \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  indicated Tuesday that the Russian economy is still standing despite Western sanctions. "The 'blitzkrieg' (blitzkrieg) our enemies were counting on has not worked," Putin said referring to the financial sanctions at his first press conference in several weeks.\par \par \uc2 An increasingly used tool However, some data show that the Russian economy is not as unperturbed as Putin claims: car production plunged, the risk of a debt default worsens and inflation threatens to skyrocket, as data released a week ago show. The \uc2 Russian Finance Ministry\uc2  announced that it paid in rubles a $650 million debt after a foreign bank refused to make the payment in dollars, exposing it to a risk of default at the end of a 30-day grace period, starting on April 4. For several weeks, Russia dodged the risk of default, as the \uc2 U.S. Treasury\uc2  allowed the use of foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  held by Moscow to pay foreign debts. But last week Washington tightened sanctions, and no longer accepts dollars held by Moscow in U.S. banks. "It is difficult for Russia to avoid a sovereign default," assures Timothy Ash, analyst at Blue Bay Asset, however. "A default might not immediately bring down the markets and the Russian economy, but it will have longer-term consequences," says the analyst, who foresees "an impact on investment, growth, living standards," among others. What do apartheid in South Africa, Libya under \uc2 Muammar Gaddafi\uc2  or the Russian invasion of Ukraine have in common? Sanctions, a tool that Western states have been using for 30 years, but whose effectiveness has yet to be proven.\par \par \uc2 Long-term impact "This tool boomed when military responses were no longer popular," says Dorgans. And they have been increasingly used in inter-state conflicts since 1950, up to about 30 occasions per year until 1990, then clearly increasing. Between 1950 and 2019, 1,101 conflicts between statesinvolved sanctions, according to the Global Sanctions Data Base (GSDB), which keeps detailed records. The multiplication of regional economic agreements meant an increase in sanctions with third countries, Erdal Yalcin, professor of international economics at the University of Konstanz in Germany, and founding member of the GSDB, explains to AFP. Another reason for their recurrent use is the development of international financial integration since the end of the 20th century. "Most countries were integrated into an international financial system, and there is a great temptation to punish countries with economic instruments," he says. The recourse to sanctions was mostly decided in cases of human rights, restoration of democracy and wars, describes the GSDB. Some cases have gone down in the annals of history, such as the struggle against apartheid in South Africa, the US embargo against Cuba, or sanctions against the Iranian nuclear program. But despite their growing use, their effectiveness is in question.\par \par \uc2 In a recent interview with AFP, researcher \uc2 Gary Hufbauer\uc2 , a fellow at the Peterson Institute think tank (PIIE) in Washington, recalled that penalties were effective in less than a third of conflicts, generally when imposed against small countries. "These sanctions are very effective in the economic damage they entail. At the political level, the level of effectiveness is in the order of 30 to 40%," says Erdal Yalcin. According to him, it can take years for these punitive measures to produce real effects. For this specialist, their effectiveness is also difficult to measure because many of them are accompanied by other measures, such as the US sanctions against Iraq during the invasion of Kuwait in 1990, in addition to Washington's military \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . In \b \highlight22\uc2 Russia\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the economic effectiveness of the sanctions is already being felt: the \uc2 World Bank\uc2  forecasts a recession of 11.2% this year and the S&P Global Ratings agency declared that the country was in "selective default" after it paid a dollar debt in rubles. The Russian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  nevertheless remains at a high level after initially falling sharply. On the political level, "sanctions cannot make the tanks turn around, at least not immediately," Juan Zarate, a consultant at the \uc2 Center for Strategic and International Studies\uc2  (CSIS), told AFP. According to this former U.S. counter-terrorism strategy advisor under President George W. Bush, "the full effect of the sanctions will not be felt for weeks, months or perhaps years." With information from AFP\par \par \uc2 Dokument NFINCE0020220413ei4d004i4\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw103\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal1545\pichgoal600
