\par\par \b \uc2 European Stocks Rise as Euro, Pound Weaken -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,462 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 April 2022\par 18:04\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2022, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 1204 ET - European stocks rally in closing trade as the euro and sterling weaken against a stronger dollar, boosting exporters and multinational shares. The Stoxx Europe 600 rises 1.3%, the DAX gains 1.5%, the CAC 40 adds 1.3% and the FTSE 100 advances 1.6%. GBP/USD drops 0.4% to 1.3026, having reached a 17-month low of 1.2982 earlier, according to \uc2 FactSet\uc2  . EUR/USD falls 0.1% to 1.0873 after hitting a one-month low of 1.0836 earlier. "Today's positive session for European markets appears to have more to do with the fact that the strength of the US dollar has pushed both the pound and the euro lower," \uc2 CMC Markets\uc2  analyst Michael Hewson says in a note. (renae.dyer@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1143 ET - The big headline from Canada's jobs report in March was that the unemployment rate fell to a record low 5.3%. The report also indicated hours worked jumped 1.3% in March, after a 3.6% rise in the previous month. \uc2 Bank of Nova Scotia\uc2  's economics team, based on an hours-worked figure, now suggests 1Q GDP in Canada rose 5.1% at annual rate, well above the \uc2 Bank of Canada\uc2  initial forecast of 2%. The March and February employment reports, coupled with high and rising inflation, "make the task of withdrawing monetary stimulus more and more urgent," Scotiabank says. The firm forecasts a half-point increase from the \uc2 Bank of Canada\uc2  Wednesday. (paul.vieira@wsj.com; @paulvieira)\par \par \uc2 1126 ET - The euro could rise against the dollar this year and early next year as the \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  raises interest rates but this appreciation won't last, \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2  says. The \uc2 ECB\uc2  is likely to start raising rates in the third quarter of 2022 and take its deposit rate to positive territory in early 2023, \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2  's \uc2 Ulrich Leuchtmann\uc2  says in a research note. However, later in 2023 the \uc2 ECB\uc2  could pause its rate hike cycle due to falling inflation while the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  could raise rates further than expected, which may weaken EUR/USD, he says. \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2  expects EUR/USD to rise to 1.15 by March 2023 from 1.0878 currently, before falling to 1.12 by December 2023. (renae.dyer@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1058 ET - Some economists and analysts are skeptical that a White House push to boost domestic manufacturing will help tame inflation, as Biden administration officials have contended. The White House is rolling out several policies aimed at increasing domestic manufacturing, and officials have said the aim is to insulate the US from the kinds of global supply disruptions that occurred during the Covid-19 pandemic and helped fuel inflation, which is at a 40-year high of near 8%. But some economists noted that offshore production contributed to the moderate price increases Americans broadly saw for decades prior to the pandemic. From 1990 to 2019, average annual inflation was 2.4%, according to the Labor Department's consumer-price index. (amara.omeokwe@wsj.com, @TheAmaraReport)\par \par \uc2 1053 ET - Supply chain conditions in the U.S. deteriorated in March for the second consecutive month, according to an indicator compiled by Oxford Economics. Pressures increased particularly on the price and transportation fronts as the war in Ukraine pushed commodity prices higher and aggravated logistics logjams, the economic-research firm says. Inventory dynamics and worker shortages improved over the month, but the economy's productive capacity was under more stress as demand remained resilient, Oxford Economics says. (xavier.fontdegloria@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1045 ET - Inventories of U.S. merchant wholesalers rose 2.5% in February compared with the previous month, more than doubling the 1.2% increase registered in January, according to data from the Commerce Department. Economists polled by The Wall Street Journal expected wholesale inventories to advance 2.1% on month. The ratio of inventories to sales was 1.21 in February, up from 1.20 in January but below the 1.27 the same month a year earlier, the data showed. (xavier.fontdegloria@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1041 ET - The recovery of the Russian ruble to pre-Ukraine war levels isn't a reliable indicator of offshore activity, \uc2 TD Securities\uc2  says. The ruble's levels in offshore trading don't reflect normal transactions or indicate any real capability for international investors to trade the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  as they used to before the war, TD analyst \uc2 Cristian Maggio\uc2  says in a research note. "The final hint that the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market is mostly unreliable, and levels should not be taken as proof of success of the [\b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Russia\uc2  's] attempt to regain control of the RUB, is the extreme volatility recorded in [\b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2 ] forwards," Maggio says. Such fluctuations suggest that signs of stress remain, he says. (renae.dyer@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1023 ET - The euro credit market rally of the past few weeks has lost steam and a repeat looks unlikely, \uc2 Societe Generale\uc2  says. There are ongoing concerns over high levels of inflation and central banks becoming increasingly more aggressive to tame prices, as the Fed is now anticipated to raise rates by 50 basis points in May, analysts at the French bank say." The worry is that the faster central banks have to tighten monetary policy, the greater the risks of a policy misstep as the economy slows," they say. While euro credit spreads correctly reflect the threats ahead, \uc2 Societe Generale\uc2  no longer expects a strong rally. Instead, "spreads will move in a range with a very small tightening bias," it says. (lorena.ruibal@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1019 ET - A recent easing in used-car prices might be short-lived, \uc2 JPMorgan\uc2  says. Used prices dropped 3.4% in March from February, the second straight decline after hitting a record in January, according to data released yesterday by Manheim. \uc2 JPMorgan\uc2  says in a note today that the price decline was likely driven by rebuilding of new-vehicle inventories, which in March hit their highest level since June. But the bank expects used-car prices could go back up, because rising commodity costs and continued supply-chain disruption will squeeze new-car inventories and keep new and used prices elevated. That could benefit shares of Avis and Hertz but hurt parts suppliers' stocks, it says. (mike.colias@wsj.com; @MikeColias)\par \par \uc2 1010 ET - Brazil's consumer prices rose more than expected in March and the 12-month inflation rate is likely to remain above 10% through August of this year, according to Alberto Ramos, an economist at \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  . Brazil's statistics agency says its headline measure of inflation, the IPCA, rose 1.62% in March from February and rose 11.30% from a year earlier. The consensus was for an increase of 1.35% in the month, Ramos says in a research note. Price pressures are now highly disseminated throughout the economy, and the recent increase of commodities prices is expected to sustain those pressures in the medium term, he said. There's a growing risk that wage and price indexing could add continued pressure, according to Ramos. (jeffrey.lewis@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1003 ET - The Russian ruble's recovery has accelerated since Russia's government last month announced plans to seek ruble payments for gas sold to "unfriendly" countries,\uc2 TD Securities\uc2  says. "Strength of the ruble would suggest ongoing hoarding of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to allow for these and other payments, which we do not have evidence of, however," TD analyst \uc2 Cristian Maggio\uc2  says in a research note. It could also suggest the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Russia\b0 \highlight\uc2  has found ways around sanctions and has managed to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in offshore markets, he says. The \uc2 CBR\uc2  may have sold part of its \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves denominated in \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  that aren't subject to restrictions, he says. USD/RUB falls to a six-and-a-half-week low of 76.2600, according to \uc2 FactSet\uc2  . (renae.dyer@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0954 ET - Euro high-yield corporate bonds are set to underperform investment-grade debt, says \uc2 Societe Generale\uc2  . The high-yield market has so far proved very resilient compared to the investment-grade market since the beginning of the year, reflecting market expectations of a poor investment-grade performance as the \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  ends its quantitative easing programs, the French bank says. While the corporate sector purchase programme, or CSPP, remains active, current spread levels are already pricing in a market without \uc2 ECB\uc2  distortion, it says. \uc2 Societe Generale\uc2  expects high yield paper to bear the brunt of slower growth, with "HY in both cash and synthetics... set to underperform its IG counterpart," it adds. (lorena.ruibal@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 April 08, 2022 12:04 ET (16:04 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020220408ei48003fz\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
