\par\par \b \uc2 Russian Ruble's Current Levels Seen as Unreliable Indicator of Offshore Activity -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,487 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 April 2022\par 16:41\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2022, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 1041 ET - The recovery of the Russian ruble to pre-Ukraine war levels isn't a reliable indicator of offshore activity, \uc2 TD Securities\uc2  says. The ruble's levels in offshore trading don't reflect normal transactions or indicate any real capability for international investors to trade the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  as they used to before the war, TD analyst \uc2 Cristian Maggio\uc2  says in a research note. "The final hint that the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market is mostly unreliable, and levels should not be taken as proof of success of the [\b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Russia\uc2  's] attempt to regain control of the RUB, is the extreme volatility recorded in [\b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2 ] forwards," Maggio says. Such fluctuations suggest that signs of stress remain, he says. (renae.dyer@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1023 ET - The euro credit market rally of the past few weeks has lost steam and a repeat looks unlikely, \uc2 Societe Generale\uc2  says. There are ongoing concerns over high levels of inflation and central banks becoming increasingly more aggressive to tame prices, as the Fed is now anticipated to raise rates by 50 basis points in May, analysts at the French bank say." The worry is that the faster central banks have to tighten monetary policy, the greater the risks of a policy misstep as the economy slows," they say. While euro credit spreads correctly reflect the threats ahead, \uc2 Societe Generale\uc2  no longer expects a strong rally. Instead, "spreads will move in a range with a very small tightening bias," it says. (lorena.ruibal@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1019 ET - A recent easing in used-car prices might be short-lived, \uc2 JPMorgan\uc2  says. Used prices dropped 3.4% in March from February, the second straight decline after hitting a record in January, according to data released yesterday by Manheim. \uc2 JPMorgan\uc2  says in a note today that the price decline was likely driven by rebuilding of new-vehicle inventories, which in March hit their highest level since June. But the bank expects used-car prices could go back up, because rising commodity costs and continued supply-chain disruption will squeeze new-car inventories and keep new and used prices elevated. That could benefit shares of Avis and Hertz but hurt parts suppliers' stocks, it says. (mike.colias@wsj.com; @MikeColias)\par \par \uc2 1010 ET - Brazil's consumer prices rose more than expected in March and the 12-month inflation rate is likely to remain above 10% through August of this year, according to Alberto Ramos, an economist at \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  . Brazil's statistics agency says its headline measure of inflation, the IPCA, rose 1.62% in March from February and rose 11.30% from a year earlier. The consensus was for an increase of 1.35% in the month, Ramos says in a research note. Price pressures are now highly disseminated throughout the economy, and the recent increase of commodities prices is expected to sustain those pressures in the medium term, he said. There's a growing risk that wage and price indexing could add continued pressure, according to Ramos. (jeffrey.lewis@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1003 ET - The Russian ruble's recovery has accelerated since Russia's government last month announced plans to seek ruble payments for gas sold to "unfriendly" countries,\uc2 TD Securities\uc2  says. "Strength of the ruble would suggest ongoing hoarding of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to allow for these and other payments, which we do not have evidence of, however," TD analyst \uc2 Cristian Maggio\uc2  says in a research note. It could also suggest the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Russia\b0 \highlight\uc2  has found ways around sanctions and has managed to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in offshore markets, he says. The \uc2 CBR\uc2  may have sold part of its \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves denominated in \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  that aren't subject to restrictions, he says. USD/RUB falls to a six-and-a-half-week low of 76.2600, according to \uc2 FactSet\uc2  . (renae.dyer@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0954 ET - Euro high-yield corporate bonds are set to underperform investment-grade debt, says \uc2 Societe Generale\uc2  . The high-yield market has so far proved very resilient compared to the investment-grade market since the beginning of the year, reflecting market expectations of a poor investment-grade performance as the \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  ends its quantitative easing programs, the French bank says. While the corporate sector purchase programme, or CSPP, remains active, current spread levels are already pricing in a market without \uc2 ECB\uc2  distortion, it says. \uc2 Societe Generale\uc2  expects high yield paper to bear the brunt of slower growth, with "HY in both cash and synthetics... set to underperform its IG counterpart," it adds. (lorena.ruibal@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0945 ET - Fed communication, including FOMC meetings and minutes, is responsible for 69 basis points of a 109bp increase in 10-year Treasury yields this year, \uc2 Bank of America\uc2  analysts estimate. This is the highest impact based on data going back to 2004, \uc2 BofA\uc2  says. "The news flow - likely related to inflation - was also a large contributor at 23bp this year, and we find the sensitivity of rates to news this year has been at the top of the historical range as was 2021 with the pandemic," \uc2 BofA\uc2  says. "The hawkish pivot of the Fed may be mostly priced by now." (paulo.trevisani@wsj.com; @ptrevisani)\par \par \uc2 0921 ET - Flows into investment-grade and high-yield corporate bond funds remain in positive territory for the second week in a row, \uc2 Bank of America\uc2  says, citing data from fund-tracker EPFR. Range-bound spreads have allowed corporate credit to enjoy some much needed stability in the week to Wednesday, the bank says. However, equity funds continued to experience outflows and have underperformed fixed income for the past couple of weeks, as the macroeconomic cycle continues to worsen, \uc2 BofA\uc2  says. "We think that as macro data continue to deteriorate, rates market uncertainty remains elevated and geopolitical risks are still unresolved, flows into risk assets will struggle to improve much further." (lorena.ruibal@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0832 ET - Treasury yields keep rising, with the 10-year up for the sixth consecutive day, to 2.676% from 2.654% Thursday. The two-year rises to 2.512% from 2.462%. \uc2 Deutsche Bank\uc2  's economists note in a research report that the 2y-10y yield curve has taken a "round trip" over the past two weeks. "The fundamental outlook hasn't changed dramatically over that time span. Instead, this likely reflects the elevated rates volatility environment we currently sit in," they say, adding that the 10-year real yield rate has been moving up and is now back to March 2020 levels. (paulo.trevisani@wsj.com; @ptrevisani)\par \par \uc2 0739 ET - The cost of insuring against European junk bond defaults edges lower on Friday as sentiment improves and stocks gain, mostly driven by energy shares as investors welcomed decreasing tensions and lower oil prices, says Pierre Veyret, technical analyst at ActivTrades. The iTraxx Europe Crossover index, which tracks euro high-yield credit default swaps, falls eight basis points to 358 bps in morning trading relative to the prior day's close of 366 bps, a nine-day high, \uc2 IHS Markit\uc2  data shows. Stock markets advance as sentiment bounces back, with the pan-continental Stoxx Europe 600 rising 1.2% and S&P 500 futures up 0.3%. (lorena.ruibal@wsj.com; @lorena_rbal)\par \par \uc2 0734 ET - Pandemic-related effects and higher energy prices have played a part in pushing up inflation in the eurozone, but domestically-generated underlying price pressures have also increased, \uc2 Capital Economics\uc2  says. "That should make policymakers more confident that tighter monetary policy will soon be appropriate," CE says. The economic research firm highlights the upward pressure on prices due to increased demand after the easing of pandemic restrictions. Additionally, the supply disruption was also a component and has been increasing since the end of 2020 and is now well above previous peaks. The increase in core inflation is unlikely to be entirely due to temporary supply problems, as stronger demand has also played a role, CE economists say. (maria.martinez@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0708 ET - Capital controls have helped the Russian ruble recover and this seems to have contributed to the Bank of Russia's decision to cut its key interest rate 300 basis points to 17% on Friday, Oanda says. The ruble's recovery appears to have given the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  confidence that rates no longer need to be so high, Oanda analyst \uc2 Craig Erlam\uc2  says in a note. "At 17%, the rate remains extremely high as inflation is still expected to spike and the economy severely contract," he says. "Given how markets have responded, the \uc2 CBR\uc2  may well follow up with further easing later this month." USD/RUB rises 2.1% to 79.6000 but remains below levels seen after Russia invaded Ukraine. (renae.dyer@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 April 08, 2022 10:41 ET (14:41 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020220408ei4800348\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
