\par\par \b \uc2 Global Energy Roundup: Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,532 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 April 2022\par 16:03\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2022, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 The latest Market Talks covering Energy markets. Published exclusively on Dow Jones Newswires throughout the day.\par \par \uc2 1003 ET - The Russian ruble's recovery has accelerated since Russia's government last month announced plans to seek ruble payments for gas sold to "unfriendly" countries,\uc2 TD Securities\uc2  says. "Strength of the ruble would suggest ongoing hoarding of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to allow for these and other payments, which we do not have evidence of, however," TD analyst \uc2 Cristian Maggio\uc2  says in a research note. It could also suggest the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Russia\b0 \highlight\uc2  has found ways around sanctions and has managed to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in offshore markets, he says. The \uc2 CBR\uc2  may have sold part of its \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves denominated in \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  that aren't subject to restrictions, he says. USD/RUB falls to a six-and-a-half-week low of 76.2600, according to \uc2 FactSet\uc2  . (renae.dyer@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0931 ET - European equities could fall on Monday if incumbent French President \uc2 Emmanuel Macron\uc2  's challengers secure more votes than expected in the first round of the country's presidential election on Sunday, \uc2 Barclays\uc2  says. "If the two far right candidates, [Marine] \uc2 Le Pen\uc2  and [Eric] Zemmour, and/or the far left candidate, [Jean-Luc] Melenchon, score better than expected, market reaction could be pretty negative on Monday," \uc2 Barclays\uc2  's head of European equity strategy \uc2 Emmanuel Cau\uc2  says in a note. Utilities, telecoms, real estate and financial shares would be hit by a Le Pen win while exporters, healthcare, technology and materials stocks could fare relatively better and be helped by a potentially weaker euro, he says. (renae.dyer@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0917 ET - A recently-announced US-EU deal to send more US LNG to Europe is important for US gas markets long-term but not short-term since US export capacity is already maxed-out, says \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  in a research note. "As we've discussed, we expect that any incremental volumes from the US to Europe this year will be the result of reallocated volumes [away from Asia and elsewhere] rather than additional exports," it says. "What we believe is more relevant to US gas markets is the longer term impact of Europe's efforts to reduce its dependency on Russia. Specifically, we estimate that US liquefaction capacity can increase by 3-5 Bcf/d beyond what we already basecase." (dan.molinski@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0843 ET - Natural gas prices are up 2% at $6.485/mmBtu as they continue to hit fresh highs not seen in more than 13 years. Yesterday's closing price of $6.359/mmBtu was the highest since Dec. 2, 2008. "Last week's 33bcf [EIA storage] draw was tighter than estimates in the 27-29bcf range, our forecasted 30bcf draw, and well ahead of the 5-year average 8bcf build," says Tudor Pickering in a research note. "With Residential/Commercial increasingly a lesser factor as we shift into shoulder season, power generation remains the story outside of the ebb and flow of LNG exports, with a combination of factors keeping natural gas power burn robust vs. historical levels." (dan.molinski@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0829 ET - US benchmark oil prices climb 1% to $97.03 a barrel, but are on course for a $2 weekly decline that would mark a second straight weekly decline and the fourth weekly retreat in the past five. Russia's invasion of Ukraine in late February caused WTI prices to skyrocket to a 13-year-high $123.70 on March 8, the day the US announced a ban on Russian crude-oil imports. But the market has fallen 22% since then as investors realized Russian oil was just 3%, or 200k bpd, or US imports, and major oil-importers China, India, Europe and others have continued to buy Russian crude. Soaring gasoline prices have weakened demand, which is also pushing down crude prices. (dan.molinski@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0805 ET - The war in Ukraine has completely changed the outlook for the European chemical industry, according to Baader Helvea. The equity-research firm says it views the sector more negatively as the chemical cycle--in which demand has recently risen as economies exit the Covid-19 pandemic period--will end earlier than previously expected due to the effects of the invasion. Order-book visibility will likely get more volatile, and demand is likely to deteriorate in 2Q, \uc2 Baader\uc2  says. There is also the risk of supply-chain effects increasing, which, while increasing sale prices, will also lead to volume declines later on, it says. \uc2 Baader\uc2  downgrades six companies to add from buy--\uc2 BASF\uc2  , \uc2 Covestro\uc2  , \uc2 Fuchs Petrolub\uc2  , \uc2 K+S\uc2  , Lanxess, and \uc2 Sika\uc2  . (edward.frankl@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0704 ET - Pent-up demand should work as a cushion for the European industrial machinery sector through 2022, but all eyes are now on growth prospects into 2023, with risks of U.S. industrial production growth turning negative and a European manufacturing recession in the second half of 2022, says \uc2 Citi\uc2  . At \uc2 Assa Abloy\uc2  , \uc2 Citi\uc2  sees better pricing and costs than others and it remains positive into results, but says \uc2 Atlas Copco\uc2  has a rich valuation and is likely to post a margin miss. At \uc2 Sandvik\uc2  , \uc2 Citi\uc2  cautions on volumes at the manufacturing and machining unit while SKF is facing major cost headwinds and margin pressure is likely at Volvo, it adds. "We don't see any name as a clear long into results." (dominic.chopping@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0703 ET - The prospect of an EU ban on imports of Russian gas is a key risk for capital markets, says LBBW. The bloc's 27 member states has banned coal imports but further proposals to restrict oil and gas imports from Russia are finding resistance from some member states. Russian gas accounted for 40% of the bloc's gas imports last year, according to the \uc2 European Commission\uc2  . "Further escalation of the war and any resulting ban on gas delivery from Russia remain the main risk factor for the capital markets," the German bank says. The U.S. has already declared a complete ban on Russian oil, gas and coal imports. (lorena.ruibal@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0701 ET - \uc2 Carnival Corp\uc2  's \uc2 Holland America Line\uc2  is set this weekend to be the first cruise line to resume \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Canada after a more than two-year shutdown due to the Covid-19 pandemic. The company's Koningsdam ship will call at Victoria, British Columbia on Saturday, marking the return of the industry in Canada. In 2022, six \uc2 Holland America Line\uc2  ships are set to call at Victoria and Vancouver for the critical Alaska cruise season. In total for this year, \uc2 Holland America Line\uc2  is set to operate 141 cruises on eight ships in Canada with more than 250,000 guests visiting both coasts, the company says. (will.feuer@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0629 ET - The FTSE 100 rises 1% to 7624.67, led by oil-linked stocks due to higher oil prices and financial stocks amid expectations of rising interest rates. \uc2 Shell\uc2  rises 3% while \uc2 BP\uc2  is up 2.6%. Miners also gain, with \uc2 Anglo American\uc2  up 2.3% and Antofagasta rising 1.9%. Banking and financial services company \uc2 Standard Chartered\uc2  is the biggest riser, up 3.3%, while \uc2 Barclays\uc2  rises 2.5%, \uc2 NatWest\uc2  is up 1.8% and investment company \uc2 Abrdn\uc2  ticks up 2.2%. Fallers include pharmaceuticals and utility stocks. The FTSE looks set to end the week in positive territory, but the prospect of higher interest rates and rising inflation remain a concern for investors, says \uc2 AJ Bell\uc2  's \uc2 Russ Mould\uc2  . (jessica.fleetham@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0610 ET - \uc2 Ithaca Energy\uc2  is acquiring U.K. North Sea oil-and-gas producer \uc2 Siccar Point Energy\uc2  for $1.1 billion plus $360 million in contingent payments. \uc2 Siccar Point\uc2  is the operator of the Cambo oil field, of which \uc2 Shell\uc2  holds a 30% interest. "The development of the Cambo and Rosebank fields is a huge opportunity to not only help secure the U.K.'s energy future for at least another quarter of a century, but also to create thousands of direct and indirect jobs in the process," Ithaca's CEO Alan Bruce says. Cambo is expected to deliver up to 170 million oil-equivalent barrels during its \b \highlight22\uc2 operational\b0 \highlight\uc2  life, according to Ithaca. (jaime.llinares@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0544 ET - \uc2 OMV\uc2  's first-quarter trading update highlights pressures in downstream and the deconsolidation of \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Russia, \uc2 Citi\uc2  says. The Austrian oil-and-gas company reported significant sequential growth in its European refining margin, but it is still expected to be hit by high utility costs and crude differentials, analysts at the bank say. First-quarter upstream production came in at 457,000 boe/d, down 7% quarter on quarter. From March 1, \uc2 OMV\uc2  also ceased to consolidate Russian \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and according to \uc2 Citi\uc2  the 1Q effect in March only is estimated to be around EUR80 million in lower earnings and around 35 kboed excluded from production. \uc2 Citi\uc2  addsthat it expects production costs to rise as lower-cost Russian production will be excluded going forward. (giulia.petroni@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones 