\par\par \b \uc2 Global Energy Roundup: Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,536 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 April 2022\par 20:41\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2022, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 The latest Market Talks covering Energy markets. Published exclusively on Dow Jones Newswires throughout the day.\par \par \uc2 1441 ET - WTI oil prices finish the session up 2.3% at $98.26 a barrel, but still end with a 1% weekly decline that marks a second straight weekly decline. Today's increase may signal the start of a bullish reversal, although much will depend on a slate of monthly market reports due to be released next week from \uc2 OPEC\uc2  , \uc2 IEA\uc2  and EIA. "It will be the first time the bean counters will comment on a full month of supply/demand dynamics since the beginning of the Russian War in Ukraine," \uc2 Bob Yawger\uc2  at \uc2 Mizuho\uc2  says in a research note. "The general consensus seems to be that Russian oil production will be down by around one-to-two million barrels per day." (dan.molinski@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1335 ET - US oil-drilling activity shows a significant uptick in the latest week, with the number of active oil-targeted rigs climbing by 13 to a two-year-high 546 rigs, says oilfield services company \uc2 Baker Hughes\uc2  . The rig count had largely stagnated over the past two months, posting only minor increases despite the need for more oil amid fears that the Russia-Ukraine war could lead to global oil shortages. This week's sudden, large increase may suggest US producers have decided to rise to the challenge, and if the move continues that could lead to much higher US oil production later this year and into 2023. (dan.molinski@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1105 ET - A weeks-long rally in natural gas prices that pushed the market to a 13-year-high yesterday and even saw some early buying this morning appears to be finally getting winded, with the front-month turning 1.3% lower at $6.277/mmBtu. Gas is still on track for a strong, 10% weekly rise that would mark a fourth straight weekly gain and the seventh weekly increase in eight weeks. But a know-when-to-say-when moment seems to be arriving for bullish investors who see a mild, spring season well underway that's likely to narrow a bullish storage deficit in the coming weeks. They also don't see any near-term demand increase from LNG feedgas giving capacity utilization has already been reached. (dan.molinski@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1045 ET - Inventories of U.S. merchant wholesalers rose 2.5% in February compared with the previous month, more than doubling the 1.2% increase registered in January, according to data from the Commerce Department. Economists polled by The Wall Street Journal expected wholesale inventories to advance 2.1% on month. The ratio of inventories to sales was 1.21 in February, up from 1.20 in January but below the 1.27 the same month a year earlier, the data showed. (xavier.fontdegloria@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1025 ET - \uc2 Bank of America\uc2  downgrades nine of the 28 transportation stocks it covers, including UPS, Union Pacific and Schneider, on what analyst Ken Hoexter says in a note are "deteriorating demand outlooks and rapidly falling freight rates." The bank says its latest Demand Indicator, which measures shippers' freight demand outlook, fell sharply to 64.1 from 69.2. Shipping rates have come down across the board and there are signs that demand is waning, the firm says. It's good news for a host of companies that have faced tight capacity and high rates over the past year to move their goods, but bad news for those that are doing it. In addition to the ratings cuts, the firm cuts price targets at every transportation company covered. All 9 of the stocks are lower this morning. (paul.ziobro@wsj.com and michael.dabaie@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1003 ET - The Russian ruble's recovery has accelerated since Russia's government last month announced plans to seek ruble payments for gas sold to "unfriendly" countries,\uc2 TD Securities\uc2  says. "Strength of the ruble would suggest ongoing hoarding of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to allow for these and other payments, which we do not have evidence of, however," TD analyst \uc2 Cristian Maggio\uc2  says in a research note. It could also suggest the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Russia\b0 \highlight\uc2  has found ways around sanctions and has managed to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in offshore markets, he says. The \uc2 CBR\uc2  may have sold part of its \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves denominated in \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  that aren't subject to restrictions, he says. USD/RUB falls to a six-and-a-half-week low of 76.2600, according to \uc2 FactSet\uc2  . (renae.dyer@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0931 ET - European equities could fall on Monday if incumbent French President \uc2 Emmanuel Macron\uc2  's challengers secure more votes than expected in the first round of the country's presidential election on Sunday, \uc2 Barclays\uc2  says. "If the two far right candidates, [Marine] \uc2 Le Pen\uc2  and [Eric] Zemmour, and/or the far left candidate, [Jean-Luc] Melenchon, score better than expected, market reaction could be pretty negative on Monday," \uc2 Barclays\uc2  's head of European equity strategy \uc2 Emmanuel Cau\uc2  says in a note. Utilities, telecoms, real estate and financial shares would be hit by a Le Pen win while exporters, healthcare, technology and materials stocks could fare relatively better and be helped by a potentially weaker euro, he says. (renae.dyer@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0917 ET - A recently-announced US-EU deal to send more US LNG to Europe is important for US gas markets long-term but not short-term since US export capacity is already maxed-out, says \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  in a research note. "As we've discussed, we expect that any incremental volumes from the US to Europe this year will be the result of reallocated volumes [away from Asia and elsewhere] rather than additional exports," it says. "What we believe is more relevant to US gas markets is the longer term impact of Europe's efforts to reduce its dependency on Russia. Specifically, we estimate that US liquefaction capacity can increase by 3-5 Bcf/d beyond what we already basecase." (dan.molinski@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0843 ET - Natural gas prices are up 2% at $6.485/mmBtu as they continue to hit fresh highs not seen in more than 13 years. Yesterday's closing price of $6.359/mmBtu was the highest since Dec. 2, 2008. "Last week's 33bcf [EIA storage] draw was tighter than estimates in the 27-29bcf range, our forecasted 30bcf draw, and well ahead of the 5-year average 8bcf build," says Tudor Pickering in a research note. "With Residential/Commercial increasingly a lesser factor as we shift into shoulder season, power generation remains the story outside of the ebb and flow of LNG exports, with a combination of factors keeping natural gas power burn robust vs. historical levels." (dan.molinski@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0829 ET - US benchmark oil prices climb 1% to $97.03 a barrel, but are on course for a $2 weekly decline that would mark a second straight weekly decline and the fourth weekly retreat in the past five. Russia's invasion of Ukraine in late February caused WTI prices to skyrocket to a 13-year-high $123.70 on March 8, the day the US announced a ban on Russian crude-oil imports. But the market has fallen 22% since then as investors realized Russian oil was just 3%, or 200k bpd, or US imports, and major oil-importers China, India, Europe and others have continued to buy Russian crude. Soaring gasoline prices have weakened demand, which is also pushing down crude prices. (dan.molinski@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0805 ET - The war in Ukraine has completely changed the outlook for the European chemical industry, according to Baader Helvea. The equity-research firm says it views the sector more negatively as the chemical cycle--in which demand has recently risen as economies exit the Covid-19 pandemic period--will end earlier than previously expected due to the effects of the invasion. Order-book visibility will likely get more volatile, and demand is likely to deteriorate in 2Q, \uc2 Baader\uc2  says. There is also the risk of supply-chain effects increasing, which, while increasing sale prices, will also lead to volume declines later on, it says. \uc2 Baader\uc2  downgrades six companies to add from buy--\uc2 BASF\uc2  , \uc2 Covestro\uc2  , \uc2 Fuchs Petrolub\uc2  , \uc2 K+S\uc2  , Lanxess, and \uc2 Sika\uc2  . (edward.frankl@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0704 ET - Pent-up demand should work as a cushion for the European industrial machinery sector through 2022, but all eyes are now on growth prospects into 2023, with risks of U.S. industrial production growth turning negative and a European manufacturing recession in the second half of 2022, says \uc2 Citi\uc2  . At \uc2 Assa Abloy\uc2  , \uc2 Citi\uc2  sees better pricing and costs than others and it remains positive into results, but says \uc2 Atlas Copco\uc2  has a rich valuation and is likely to post a margin miss. At \uc2 Sandvik\uc2  , \uc2 Citi\uc2  cautions on volumes at the manufacturing and machining unit while SKF is facing major cost headwinds and margin pressure is likely at Volvo, it adds. "We don't see any name as a clear long into results." (dominic.chopping@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 April 08, 2022 14:41 ET (18:41 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020220408ei48003sc\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
