\par\par \uc2 CE Noticias Financieras English\par \b \uc2 Why the ruble is soaring as Russia faces historic recession and massive sanctions\b0\par\par\uc2 1,036 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 April 2022\par \uc2 CE NoticiasFinancieras\par \uc2 NFINCE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 Content Engine LLC \par \par \uc2 Russia's economy is facing the worst economic recession since the breakup of the Soviet Union. In addition, Moscow is dealing with a battery of sanctions imposed by the West that are hitting the country's investment and consumption. To all of the above must be added the uncertainty generated by a war in which Russia is the major player. In theory, all this should have driven the ruble to hell, and initially it did. However, after a violent and rapid fall, the ruble has started a comeback that has allowed it to regain pre-war levels. What is behind this miraculous recovery?\par \par \uc2 Since the ruble hit a low of $0.0073 per unit on March 4, the ruble has rallied more than 70% to over $0.0131 per unit. This rally, which continues this Thursday, has taken place despite the fact that the war is not going well for Russia and, above all, despite the fact that the economic expectations for the country are getting worse and worse and international sanctions keep on coming. Everything suggests that the strength of the ruble is a mirage caused by an \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  exchange rate that does not even remotely reflect the fundamentals of the Russian economy and the sharp rise in the price of raw materials.\par \par \uc2 In a scenario without direct \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Russia\b0 \highlight\uc2  (drastic capital controls) and with total freedom of trade, the Russian ruble should have depreciated much more. The fundamentals of the economy are very negative: GDP is expected to sink 10% this year (a crisis worse than that of 1998), inflation is expected to soar above 15%, while foreign investment has already frozen and is not expected to return, which will weigh on growth, innovation and productivity in the medium term.\par \par \uc2 Deloitte\uc2  economists summarily outline the keys to this surprising resurrection of the ruble: "In part it is because the Russian government imposed severe capital controls, thus limiting the ability of Russians to take money out of the country. It is also evidence that foreign investors are restricted in disposing of their assets in Russia. In addition, the sharp rise in the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's benchmark interest rate provided savers with an incentive to retain their Russian bank deposits. On the other hand, higher prices for oil and other commodities have allowed Russia to continue to generate \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  earnings, despite the obstacles."\par \par \uc2 A dead-end maze for money Russia has become a sort of dead-end maze for the money already inside the country, so the massive capital flight has been stopped by the tight controls of the \uc2 Russian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and authorities. This move has prevented billions from leaving the country (through the sale of rubles for foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) putting further selling pressure on the ruble, which has created a solid short-term 'floor' for the Russian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  exchange rate.\par \par \uc2 The International Institute of Finance (IIF) commented in a note that "the Bank of Russia reacted quickly to the sanctions by more than doubling its interest rates on February 28 from 9.5% to 20%, providing targeted liquidity support to the banking sector and introducing severe capital controls. In the early days of the crisis, the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Russia intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market to stabilize the ruble, whose fall was halted after the asset freeze, as Governor Nabiullina acknowledged."\par \par \uc2 Nevertheless, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  lost $38.8 billion in reserves between February 18 and March 25 (defending the ruble exchange rate), which has reduced total reserves to $600 billion (including the frozen assets). In addition, the Russian authorities also shut down the domestic stock market for a month and reduced the number of ruble trading sessions, any restrictive measure was good to prevent any leakage through which rubles could escape.\par \par \uc2 All of the above has prevented the ruble from sinking further, but there are also other factors that have given 'wings' to the Russian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in recent weeks, such as international trade. Sanctions and the repulsion of multinational companies against the invasion of Ukraine have left Russia without a large part of its imports (almost nobody in the West continues to do business with Russia), while Moscow continues to export large quantities of oil and gas, which have also become much more expensive during the last few months. Sharp falls in imports coupled with exports that have held up better have generated very large current account surpluses for Russia.\par \par \uc2 Current account surpluses These surpluses mean direct inflows of foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  that have helped to contain the exchange rate of the ruble, which in turn has also benefited from \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 's pressure on Europe and other countries to buy gas and oil in rubles (a way of increasing demand for rubles on international markets), although Europe has later managed to partially avoid using the ruble to continue buying gas and crude oil.\par \par \uc2 All of the above, according to IIF data, has generated historically high current account surpluses: $39 billion in January-February and probably an additional $40 billion in March, and possibly more than $250 billion for the whole year (in the absence of an energy embargo). This being the case, "Russia should be able to recover the 'lost' reserves in a relatively short period of time," the IIF assures.\par \par \uc2 In addition, Russian exporters are obliged to convert 80% of their revenues into rubles, which contributes to the inflow of foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the local market. At the same time, families cannot exchange more than $10,000 (rubles to dollars) until September and there is enough evidence that even this amount is difficult to obtain in banks.\par \par \uc2 On the other hand, Russian financial intermediaries must receive explicit approval for debt payments to non-residents (payments in hard \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  such as dollars or euros, usually). All these controls have artificially strengthened the ruble. "Therefore, the recent strengthening of the ruble is not surprising and could be reversed once again if sanctions were to be tightened in the coming weeks. It is unlikely that this position can be maintained for much longer if more evidence of Russian war crimes emerges," they warn from the IIF.\par \par \uc2 Dokument NFINCE0020220407ei47003vk\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
