\par\par \b \uc2 Markets: Companies Put M&A on Hold Amid Russian War on Ukraine\b0\par\par\uc2 1,417 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 April 2022\par 12:05\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2022, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 Futures and bond yields are up to start the second quarter, as investors await employment data to gauge the health of the labor market. I'm James Willhite, and here's what we're watching in Friday's trading.\par \par \uc2 Economists surveyed by The Wall Street Journal estimate that employers added 490,000 jobs in March. That would mark 11 straight monthly gains above 400,000, the longest such stretch of growth in records dating back to 1939. Robust job growth is one of the factors pressuring the Federal Reserve to pick up the pace of interest-rate rises. Read our full market wrap here .\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, our Kristin Broughton reports that the conflict in Ukraine is pushing some companies to abandon planned deals or take more time to analyze them. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    Markets in a Minute   Stocks to Watch \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 GameStop\f28 \uc2 \f2  : The videogame retailer's stock soared 17% premarket after it said it would request shareholder approval for an increase in the number of shares to enable a stock split .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 BlackBerry\f28 \uc2 \f2  : Shares of the Canadian cybersecurity company edged down premarket after its fourth-quarter revenue missed forecasts.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Lulu's Fashion Lounge\f28 \uc2 \f2  : The online retailer's shares soared 14% premarket after it reported a quarterly loss, but a jump in annual net revenue.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Duck Creek Technologies\f28 \uc2 \f2  : Shares of the technology provider to the insurance industry dropped 12% premarket on the heels of its quarterly results.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Enjoying this newsletter? Get more from WSJ and support our work with a special subscription offer .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Companies Put M&A on Hold Amid Russian War on Ukraine\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 By Kristin Broughton\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Companies are scrapping planned mergers in Europe or taking more time to analyze potential deals as the Russian war on Ukraine puts a damper on what is otherwise expected to be a robust year for acquisitions.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A handful of European companies, including U.K.-based \f28 \uc2 \f2 Spectris\f28 \uc2 \f2 , a measurement-device company, and Gett, a corporate-transportation company, called off prospective deals following the Russian invasion in late February, citing market uncertainty.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The conflict also is affecting deals in earlier stages, prompting some acquirers to stay on the sidelines, at least temporarily, as they assess the impact of rising energy prices, or conduct additional fact-finding about a target's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , mergers and acquisitions executives said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Global deal making hit record levels in 2021 as companies took advantage of low interest rates and a surge in private-equity fundraising to expand their businesses through M&A. While the conflict in Ukraine hasn't affected global M&A on a large scale, it has prompted some acquirers, particularly those looking at targets in Europe, to conduct additional due diligence or question whether transactions are worth the legal or financial risk.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Companies through March 29 withdrew European M&A transactions worth $147.1 billion, or 31% more compared with the prior-year period, according to Dealogic, a financial data provider. The increase was driven by large withdrawals before the Russian invasion, including \f28 \uc2 \f2 Unilever\f28 \uc2 \f2 's decision in January not to raise its $68 billion offer for \f28 \uc2 \f2 GlaxoSmithKline\f28 \uc2 \f2 's consumer healthcare business, effectively walking away from the potential deal, as well as \f28 \uc2 \f2 Nvidia\f28 \uc2 \f2  and SoftBank's decision to terminate their planned $40 billion deal for the U.S. semiconductor company to acquire buy chip-design firm Arm.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 For a longer version of this article online, follow this link .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Will global dealmaking jump if a cease-fire can be established in Ukraine? Let us know by replying to this email. Your comments may be edited before publication in future newsletters, and please make sure to include your name and location. Data Watch\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.S. bonds' worst quarter in more than 40 years has come to a close. The question for many investors now is whether it is time to buy the biggest dip in recent memory .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Stocks in China wrapped up their worst quarter in years . The CSI 300 Index, which consists of the largest companies listed in Shanghai and Shenzhen, lost nearly 15% in the three months to March 31, while the 500-stock Shenzhen Component Index dropped 18%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The average rate for a 30-year fixed mortgage loan jumped to 4.67%, the latest in a weekslong move that has brought the figure up from 3.22% in early January.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Commodities wrapped up their best quarter in more than 30 years after Russia's invasion of Ukraine supercharged a rally in markets from oil to wheat and nickel .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Market Facts The Russian ruble has strengthened close to its preinvasion level against the dollar-a byproduct of the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's tight control over the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . Also at play, a possible surge of quarter-end payments for Russia's commodity exports. On the eve of Russia's invasion of Ukraine, the ruble traded at 81 to the dollar. Friday morning it was trading around 83 rubles to the dollar.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Several large Chinese property stocks stopped trading Friday as part of a wave of share suspensions for Hong Kong-listed companies that couldn't publish annual results on time. The city's exchange operator, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Hong Kong Exchanges & Clearing\f28 \uc2 \f2 , said 32 stocks were suspended for failing to meet a reporting deadline at the end of March.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 On this day in 1930, in one of the biggest "suckers' rallies" of all time, the total market value of New York Stock Exchange-listed companies hit $67.5 billion, up roughly 40% from its trough in the Great Crash of 1929. By year-end, however, stocks were down more than 40% from here. Key Events\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.S. nonfarm payrolls, due at 8:30 a.m. ET, are expected to increase by 490,000 in March and the unemployment rate is forecast to fall to 3.7% from 3.8% one month earlier.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Chicago Fed\f28 \uc2 \f2  President \f28 \uc2 \f2 Charles Evans\f28 \uc2 \f2  speaks on the economy at 9:05 a.m.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 IHS Markit\f28 \uc2 \f2 's manufacturing index, due at 9:45 a.m., is forecast to hold at 58.5 in March, unchanged from a preliminary reading.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The \f28 \uc2 \f2 Institute for Supply Management\f28 \uc2 \f2 's manufacturing index, due at 10 a.m., is expected to increase to 59 in March from 58.6 one month earlier.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.S. construction spending for February, due at 10 a.m., is forecast to rise 1% from the prior month.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The \f28 \uc2 \f2 Baker Hughes\f28 \uc2 \f2  rig count is out at 1 p.m.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Must Reads\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The U.S. is probing a meeting between \f28 \uc2 \f2 Activision\f28 \uc2 \f2 's chief executive and an options buyer. Authorities are looking into at least one meeting between \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bobby Kotick\f28 \uc2 \f2 , the chief of the videogame firm, and one of three traders who made a timely options trade just before a merger announcement .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A hospital deal gone bust put a real-estate firm in the spotlight. The failure of a California city's only hospital sets off a scramble to raise as much as $70 million to keep it open.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The SEC told exchanges to treat customer crypto holdings as liabilities. The guidelines reflect SEC Chairman \f28 \uc2 \f2 Gary Gensler\f28 \uc2 \f2 's warning that investors who own cryptocurrency through trading platforms like \f28 \uc2 \f2 Coinbase Global\f28 \uc2 \f2  are effectively making unsecured loans to those companies.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Following the Fed's lead, banks are shifting their forecasts to big rate increases. After key \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  officials opened the door to raising interest rates by more than the customary quarter-percentage point, several of Wall Street's biggest banks now believe the increases will come in jumbo increments .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Carlyle signed a new advisory deal with Fortitude Re. The agreement with the reinsurer will boost Carlyle's fee-paying assets under management by $50 billion.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Guggenheim Securities\f28 \uc2 \f2  hired Orcun Unlu away from \f28 \uc2 \f2 Citigroup\f28 \uc2 \f2 . The investment banking and capital markets division of \f28 \uc2 \f2 Guggenheim Partners\f28 \uc2 \f2  tapped the global head of \f28 \uc2 \f2 Citigroup\f28 \uc2 \f2 's private funds group to launch a new advisory service .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Biden will draw down oil reserves in a bid to ease gas prices. The U.S. will draw down 180 million barrels over six months, in an unprecedented government \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in oil markets following \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Russia\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's invasion of Ukraine. Crude prices fell about 4% on the news.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 What We've Heard on the Street About Us\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 We want to be the first place you go to get ready for the opening bell every day. This newsletter is written and edited by James Willhite ( @jimwillhite ; [james.willhite@wsj.com]) in London.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 This article is a text version of a Wall Street Journal newsletter published earlier today.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 April 01, 2022 06:05 ET (10:05 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020220401ei41001ap\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
