\par\par \b \uc2 *S&PGR Affirms Belarus 'CCC/C' Ratings; Remain On Watch Neg\b0\par\par\uc2 3,461 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2022\par 22:06\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2022, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 18 Mar 2022 17:06 ET  Press Release: S&PGR Affirms Belarus 'CCC/C' Ratings; Remain On Watch Neg\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par The following is a press release from S&P Global Ratings: \par  \par OVERVIEW \par      -- In our view, international sanctions imposed on Belarus due to its \par involvement in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Russia\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's military \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  against Ukraine are severe and \par could tighten further. \par      -- We believe these sanctions will materially disrupt the Belarusian \par economy and create significant financial stability risk. \par      -- Absent an unforeseen positive development, we now consider that \par Belarus could default over the next 12 months. \par      -- We affirmed the '\f28 \uc2 \f2 CCC\f28 \uc2 \f2  /C' ratings on Belarus. The ratings remain on \par CreditWatch with negative implications. \par  \par RATING ACTION \par On March 18, 2022, S&P Global Ratings affirmed its '\f28 \uc2 \f2 CCC\f28 \uc2 \f2  /C' long-and short-term \par foreign and local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  sovereign credit ratings on Belarus. The ratings \par remain on CreditWatch negative. \par  \par CREDITWATCH \par The CreditWatch negative indicates that we could lower the sovereign ratings \par over the next few weeks. We expect to resolve the CreditWatch placement once \par we have more clarity on the impact of the sanctions, as well as the \par willingness of the Belarusian government to honor its debt obligations in full \par and on time. \par  \par RATIONALE \par In our view, Belarus is currently facing mounting economic, \par balance-of-payments, and financial stability risks following the imposition of \par strong international sanctions, which stem from its involvement in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Russia\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \par military \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  against Ukraine. The extent of the full economic damage \par to Belarus is difficult to estimate, but we expect it to be very significant. \par  \par According to independent reports, Belarus has been supporting Russia by \par allowing it to use Belarusian territory as a staging post and to fire \par ballistic missiles into Ukraine. Belarus' political institutions are very \par weak, with power remaining in the hands of Mr. Lukashenko following the \par disputed presidential election in August 2020. The results of this election \par were not recognized by the \f28 \uc2 \f2 EU\f28 \uc2 \f2   and several other countries due to widely \par reported electoral violations. In our view, over the last year and a half, \par this has made Belarus even more dependent on Russia, likely influencing Mr. \par Lukashenko's willingness to support Russia militarily. \par  \par The strong sanctions imposed by the EU, U.S., and U.K., among others, target a \par number of important Belarusian export products, such as potash and refined \par oil; have suspended access to the SWIFT system for several key Belarusian \par banks; as well as restrict the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of the \f28 \uc2 \f2 National Bank of Belarus\f28 \uc2 \f2   (the \par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ) in the EU. The sanctions have been progressively tightening over \par the last two weeks and we consider that they could tighten further. \par  \par Belarus has historically relied on assistance from Russia, such as through \par sales of hydrocarbons at favorable prices or the provision of additional \par credit lines. This reliance has been one of the key factors supporting our \par sovereign ratings on Belarus. In mid-March, the prime minister of Belarus \par announced that Russia's loans to Belarus will be converted into Russian rubles \par from foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and that the repayment terms on that debt will be more \par favorable. The full details of this new agreement are not available yet. \par Despite the announcement, we believe that Russia's future willingness and \par ability to support Belarus could be increasingly questionable in the current \par environment, given the scale of macro-financial distress in Russia itself. \par  \par Overall, we now consider it likely that, absent an unforeseen positive \par development, Belarus could default on its commercial debt over the next 12 \par months. Apart from financial considerations, we also believe it is possible \par that Belarus will become unwilling to honor its obligations in retaliation \par against the sanctions. \par  \par Institutional and economic profile: Belarus is set to experience its sharpest \par economic contraction in decades \par      -- The EU, U.S., and U.K., among others, have imposed substantial \par sanctions on Belarus in response to its support for \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Russia\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's military \par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in Ukraine. \par      -- The full impact of the sanctions is difficult to gauge, but we expect \par they will lead to a significant disruption of economic activity and breakages \par of established supply chains. \par      -- Under our baseline, we forecast Belarus' real GDP to contract by 15% \par in 2022, followed by a 5% contraction in 2023. \par  \par In response to Belarus' support for \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Russia\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's military \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in Ukraine, \par the U.S., EU, and U.K., among others, have imposed unprecedented economic \par sanctions. These include: \par      -- Wide-ranging export prohibitions to the EU, including for key \par Belarusian foreign-\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 -earning sectors such as oil refining and potash \par fertilizers; \par      -- EU measures restricting the provision of SWIFT services to Belarus' \par second-largest domestic bank, Belagroprombank, as well as Bank Dabrabyt and \par the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Development Bank of the Republic of Belarus\f28 \uc2 \f2  . Together with U.S. sanctions \par covering subsidiaries of sanctioned Russian banks operating in Belarus, close \par to one-third of Belarus' banking sector is currently under sanctions; \par      -- EU prohibitions on transactions with the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  related to the \par management of reserves or assets; and \par      -- A wide array of measures against specific Belarusian officials and \par companies as well as the prohibition of exports of dual-use items. \par  \par In aggregate, we estimate that around 75% of Belarusian exports will be \par affected either directly by the conflict itself or by the resulting sanctions. \par We expect significant economic contractions in Russia and Ukraine as a result \par of the sanctions and as a direct consequence of the ongoing military \par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Russia\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and Ukraine account for around 42% and 13%, respectively, \par of Belarus' total goods exports. Another 20% of exports are to the EU and will \par therefore also be significantly affected by the European restrictions. \par  \par An important additional economic effect stems from the rising number of \par Western companies that have announced the discontinuation or suspension of \par their \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in Belarus. This is not necessarily directly related to \par sanctions, and in many cases, stems from these companies' individual decisions \par to manage \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operational\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  or reputational risks. We believe this development will \par essentially aggravate the sanctions, leading to a disruption of supply chains \par with substantial economic consequences. \par  \par We also think that the prospects for Belarus' IT sector--one of the country's \par key growth drivers in recent years--are looking increasingly bleak. \par Reputational risks, flight disruptions, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  volatility, and political \par uncertainty will likely undermine the sector's medium-term potential as \par companies and employees hasten to relocate outside Belarus. \par  \par It is difficult to quantify the overall impact of the aforementioned \par developments on the Belarusian economy, but we consider it could be comparable \par to the 1990s, when real GDP contracted cumulatively by 40% over 1990-1995 \par following Belarus' independence from the Soviet Union. This was a period of \par significant disruption and breakage of supply chains similar to what appears \par to be happening right now. Our baseline scenario now assumes a 15% contraction \par in Belarus' real GDP this year, followed by a 5% contraction in 2023. We \par expect its GDP in U.S. dollar terms to drop by over 35% from $68 billion in \par 2021 to $43 billion in 2022. \par  \par Historically, Russia's willingness to extend financial support to Belarus has \par been one of the key factors supporting our sovereign ratings on Belarus. \par Specifically, Russia has provided a number of subsidies to Belarus, including \par favorable hydrocarbon prices, refinancing maturing debt, and extending \par additional credit lines. In our view, such support could become increasingly \par uncertain given the scale of financial market volatility and economic \par pressures on Russia itself. We believe this could have direct negative \par implications for Belarus' ability to service its commercial debt. \par  \par Flexibility and performance profile: We see an increasing likelihood of \par default on commercial debt over the next 12 months \par      -- Although we still do not consider Belarus' sovereign default to be \par inevitable, it is becoming an increasingly likely outcome over the next 12 \par months. \par      -- We expect Belarus' fiscal performance to deteriorate significantly in \par 2022 on account of the economic contraction, the need to support state-owned \par enterprises, and exchange-rate depreciation. \par      -- We forecast net general government debt to rise above 50% of GDP at \par end-2022 from less than 30% of GDP at end-2021. \par  \par While we currently do not view a Belarus sovereign default on commercial debt \par as inevitable, we see it as an increasingly likely outcome over the next 12 \par months. In our view, there are several scenarios that could lead to a default \par on commercial debt. \par  \par First, a tightening of economic sanctions against more of Belarus' banks or \par more comprehensive restrictions on the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 --perhaps by the U.S. in \par addition to the EU--could raise financial stability risks even further. This \par could also deprive the government of access to large portions of its foreign \par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves, needed for servicing debt. \par  \par Second, Belarus' willingness to pay its commercial debt on time and in full \par could weaken as a way of retaliating against already imposed or new sanctions. \par A presidential decree adopted in mid-March 2022 allows the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  \par to pay maturing debt to the so-called unfriendly countries (effectively those \par that have imposed certain economic restrictions or sanctions on Belarus) in \par local instead of foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . Such an authorization would need to be \par approved by the Council of Ministers of Belarus and it remains to be seen how \par it will be applied in practice. \par  \par Belarus continues to face a heavy foreign-debt servicing schedule. In 2022, \par the government is scheduled to repay $2.5 billion (6% of 2022 GDP) in \par principal and interest. The lion's share pertains to servicing official \par bilateral or multilateral debt but there is still almost $400 million of \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 18 Mar 2022 17:06 ET \par Press Release: S&PGR Affirms Belarus 'CCC/C' -2-\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par foreign commercial debt to service. Payments on domestic foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  \par commercial debt (principal and interest) of $700 million are also due this \par year. Belarus had \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign-exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves of $8.3 billion at the end of \par February 2022, although it remains uncertain whether the reserves can be \par deployed in full, particularly if the existing sanctions become more \par restrictive, as has already happened in Russia. \par  \par We expect a sharp deterioration in fiscal performance in Belarus in 2022. In \par our view, this will be driven by the fiscal headline balance weakening as the \par economy contracts and revenues fall, as well as by possible government actions \par to support state-owned enterprises or parts of the banking sector. The \par depreciation of the Belarusian ruble will also significantly inflate existing \par debt levels given that almost all debt (both foreign and domestic) is \par denominated in foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . \par  \par Consequently, we forecast net general government debt to rise steeply to 52% \par of GDP at the end of 2022 from less than 30% of GDP at the end of 2021. We \par expect the current account to remain broadly balanced over our forecast \par period, principally reflecting the unavailability of funding to cover a \par deficit. In our view, this will effectively act as a hard financing \par constraint, depressing imports. \par  \par The Belarusian banking sector is experiencing significant stress. Asset \par quality deterioration and pressure on deposit withdrawals are likely to \par increase while access to other funding sources remains extremely limited. In \par our view, the financial sector was already in a weak position before the \par military escalation and the risks are now compounded (see "Banking Industry \par Country Risk Assessment: Belarus," published May 27, 2021). The banks \par represent a contingent liability risk for public finances, in our view. \par  \par S&P Global Ratings acknowledges a high degree of uncertainty about the extent, \par outcome, and consequences of the military conflict between Russia and Ukraine. \par Irrespective of the duration of military hostilities, sanctions and related \par political risks are likely to remain in place for some time. Potential effects \par could include dislocated commodities markets--notably for oil and gas--supply \par chain disruptions, inflationary pressures, weaker growth, and capital market \par volatility. As the situation evolves, we will update our assumptions and \par estimates accordingly. See our macroeconomic and credit updates here: \par Russia-Ukraine Macro, Market, & Credit Risks. Note that the timing of \par publication for rating decisions on European issuers is subject to European \par regulatory requirements. \par  \par KEY STATISTICS \par  \par RELATED CRITERIA \par  \par      -- General Criteria: Environmental, Social, And Governance Principles In \par Credit Ratings, Oct. 10, 2021 \par      -- Criteria | Governments | Sovereigns: Sovereign Rating Methodology, \par Dec. 18, 2017 \par      -- General Criteria: Methodology For Linking Long-Term And Short-Term \par Ratings, April 7, 2017 \par      -- General Criteria: Criteria For Assigning 'CCC+', '\f28 \uc2 \f2 CCC\f28 \uc2 \f2  ', 'CCC -', And \par 'CC' Ratings, Oct. 1, 2012 \par      -- General Criteria: Principles Of Credit Ratings, Feb. 16, 2011 \par      -- General Criteria: Methodology: Criteria For Determining Transfer And \par Convertibility Assessments, May 18, 2009 \par  \par RELATED RESEARCH \par      -- Sovereign Ratings Score Snapshot, March 8, 2022 \par      -- Sovereign Ratings List, March 3, 2022 \par      -- Sovereign Ratings History, March 3, 2022 \par      -- Global Sovereign Rating Trends 2022: Despite Stabilization, The \par Pandemic Threatens The Recovery, Jan. 27, 2022 \par      -- Sovereign Risk Indicators, Dec. 13, 2021; a free interactive version \par is available at " \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.spratings.com/sri"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.spratings.com/sri }}}\uc2 \f2 " \par      -- Banking Industry Country Risk Assessment: Belarus, May 27, 2021 \par      -- Default, Transition, and Recovery: 2020 Annual Sovereign Default And \par Rating Transition Study, April 12, 2021 \par  \par In accordance with our relevant policies and procedures, the Rating Committee \par was composed of analysts that are qualified to vote in the committee, with \par sufficient experience to convey the appropriate level of knowledge and \par understanding of the methodology applicable (see 'Related Criteria And \par Research'). At the onset of the committee, the chair confirmed that the \par information provided to the Rating Committee by the primary analyst had been \par distributed in a timely manner and was sufficient for Committee members to \par make an informed decision. \par  \par After the primary analyst gave opening remarks and explained the \par recommendation, the Committee discussed key rating factors and critical issues \par in accordance with the relevant criteria. Qualitative and quantitative risk \par factors were considered and discussed, looking at track-record and forecasts. \par  \par The committee's assessment of the key rating factors is reflected in the \par Ratings Score Snapshot above. \par  \par The chair ensured every voting member was given the opportunity to articulate \par his/her opinion. The chair or designee reviewed the draft report to ensure \par consistency with the Committee decision. The views and the decision of the \par rating committee are summarized in the above rationale and outlook. The \par weighting of all rating factors is described in the methodology used in this \par rating action (see 'Related Criteria And Research'). \par  \par Certain terms used in this report, particularly certain adjectives used to \par express our view on rating relevant factors, have specific meanings ascribed \par to them in our criteria, and should therefore be read in conjunction with such \par criteria. Please see Ratings Criteria at www.standardandpoors.com for further \par information. A description of each of S&P Global Ratings' rating categories is \par contained in "S&P Global Ratings Definitions" at \par  \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.standardandpoors.com/en_US/web/guest/article/-/view/sourceId/504352"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://www.standardandpoors.com/en_US/web/guest/article/-/view/sourceId/504352 }}}\uc2 \f2  \par Complete ratings information is available to subscribers of RatingsDirect at \par www.capitaliq.com. All ratings affected by this rating action can be found on \par S&P Global Ratings' public website at www.standardandpoors.com. Use the \par Ratings search box located in the left column.  Alternatively, call one of the \par following S&P Global Ratings numbers: Client Support Europe (44) 20-7176-7176; \par London Press Office (44) 20-7176-3605; Paris (33) 1-4420-6708; Frankfurt (49) \par 69-33-999-225; or Stockholm (46) 8-440-591