an at 118.26, kijun 118.04 below \u8226\'20\'22 Japanese importers, others eyeing dips to buy in again \u8226\'20\'22 Specs still eyeing test of 118.61-66 double top Dec '16-Jan '17 \u8226\'20\'22 Firmer US yields supportive, Tsy 10s @2.154%, Nikkei +0.4% @25,400 \u8226\'20\'22 JPY crosses mixed, commodity-linked \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  heavy near recent highs \u8226\'20\'22 AUD/JPY 84.72-85.22, CAD/JPY 92.00-31 and NZD/JPY 79.56-89 \u8226\'20\'22 EUR/JPY up some more, 129.34 to 129.93 but further upside maybe limited EUR/USD-Bid in Asia as key resistance is getting challenged \u8226\'20\'22 EUR/USD opened +0.31% at 1.0940 as EUR was bought on the crosses \u8226\'20\'22 After trading at 1.0921 it started to move higher as oil price fell again \u8226\'20\'22 EUR/USD tracked up to 1.0977 - just above 10-day MA at 1.0975 \u8226\'20\'22 A close above the 10-day MS will likely see recovery extended through 1.1000 \u8226\'20\'22 EUR benefiting from the unwinding of the Ukraine crisis trade and lower oil \u8226\'20\'22 Buyers are camped around 1.0900 where support is forming \u8226\'20\'22 EUR/USD unlikely to extend too far ahead of the FOMC tomorrow AUD/USD-Weak tone as early gains fade with commodity slump \u8226\'20\'22 AUD/USD opened -1.47% at 0.7186 as longs bailed on commodity weakness \u8226\'20\'22 It moved up to 0.7206 early Asia on bargain hunting before commodities slid again \u8226\'20\'22 Dalian iron ore fell over 2.0% while WTI crude futures were down over 5% at one stage \u8226\'20\'22 AUD/USD traded down to 0.7165 at one stage before settling around 0.7180 \u8226\'20\'22 There were muted reactions to \uc2 RBA\uc2  minutes and better than expected China data \u8226\'20\'22 AUD/USD support is at the 61.8 of 0.6967/0.7440 move at 0.7146 \u8226\'20\'22 A break below 0.7145 will likely spark a trend lower towards year's low \u8226\'20\'22 Resistance is at the 21-day MA at 0.7247 and break would ease pressure GBP/USD- Bid with euro - Ukraine situation suggests short-term \u8226\'20\'22 +0.2%, at the top of a busy 1.3000-26 range as euro demand leads USD lower \u8226\'20\'22 Ukraine situation shows little sign of a short term resolution \u8226\'20\'22 \uc2 BoE\uc2  to raise rates Thursday - inflation to peak in Q2 - poll \u8226\'20\'22 Charts; momentum studies, 5, 10 & 21 daily and weekly moving averages fall \u8226\'20\'22 21 day Bollinger bands slide - signals suggest downtrend remains strong \u8226\'20\'22 1.3000 resilient, no major support until 1.2831, 50% 2020-2021 climb \u8226\'20\'22 Close above the falling 1.3151 10 day moving average would end downside bias AUD uptrend ends as focus shifts from supply to demand The Australian dollar has fallen nearly 3.5% against its U.S. counterpart over the past week. The drop from its 2022 high coincides with a large correction lower in oil and other key commodities on hopes peace talks between Russia and Ukraine will end the conflict. Australia's position as a leading exporter of liquid natural gas buoyed the AUD during the Ukraine crisis as Western sanctions on Russia cut off a major source of the world's energy supply. The contrast with Europe and its dependence on Russian energy supplies saw EUR/AUD fall more than 10% between Feb 4 and March 7. The cross has bounced back 4.5% over the past week, as part of the unwinding of the Ukraine-Russia conflict hedge. Supply concerns will likely keep commodity markets volatile. But there are also growing fears that global demand will wither, as stagflation forces build. As a growth \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the AUD cannot be expected to outperform in an environment of slowing global growth. China's Omicron outbreak is an additional weight on the AUD, as the decision to reintroduce partial lockdowns could undermine demand for industrial metals supplied by Australia. AUD/USD support comes in at the 61.8 Fibonacci retracement of the 2022 low-high at 0.7148. A break below that level could open the way for a test of the Jan 28 low at 0.6967. EUR/JPY retracement may be over, time to sell? EUR/JPY has screamed higher in recent sessions, surging to 129.84 on Tuesday from a 124.41 low on March 7, retracing a big chunk of its drop from 133.15 on Feb 10. Whether further upside is warranted remains a question, especially with the Ukraine-Russia conflict still raging. Optimism over peace talks between Ukraine and Russia provided a catalyst for the current EUR/JPY retracement up. However, such hopes could easily be dashed given Russia's continued bombardment of Ukraine in what it terms a "special military \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2 ". Europe would also still bear the economic and political brunt of any fallout from the conflict. A more hawkish \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  stance amid raging inflation, and higher euro zone yields have helped. The yield on 10-year French OATs is at its highest since October 2018 around 0.834%, and that on Bunds is moving towards the 0.515% spike high on Feb 16. With EUR/JPY effectively achieving the 61.8% retracement of the Feb 10-March 7 drop at 129.81, there seems to be less incentive to chase the feel-good effects of peace talks or eventual \uc2 ECB\uc2  tightening at this time. Good resistance has been seen from the descending 100-day moving average at 129.70. The 55- and 200-DMAs are above at 129.80 and 130.05. The daily Ichimoku cloud is between 129.77-130.22, and will help cap the upside along with 600 million euros in option expiries at 130.05 Wednesday and 542 million at 132.00 Thursday. Sterling on track for 1.2850, despite event risk Volatility is certain on headlines into the U.S. Federal Reserve and Bank of England rate decisions, but the sterling downtrend that began in late February, should extend. The trend is fuelled by safe-haven U.S. dollar strength which should continue as war rages in Ukraine alongside peace talks. Ukrainian negotiator \uc2 Mykhailo Podolyak\uc2  said "communication is being held yet it's hard", with no breakthrough in sight. BOEWATCH shows a quarter-point rate hike to 0.75% fully priced on March 17, with an implied 34.98 basis points, so there is a slight expectation of a bigger move. Economists expect UK inflation to reach 8% later this year, as the Ukraine crisis drives up commodity prices. This puts the \uc2 BoE\uc2  in a difficult position, balancing runaway inflation, against the risk of higher yields stalling growth. On the U.S. dollar side of GBP/USD, the Fed is in a similar position at its March 16 rate decision. \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  projections will be key for \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves. If they contradict market pricing there could be more volatility, but with global markets in turmoil over Ukraine, central banks will be loathe to create further uncertainty. Technically GBP/USD momentum studies, 5, 10 and 21 daily and weekly moving averages plus 21-day Bollinger bands slide, which is a strong trending setup. The next major support is 1.2831, 50% of the 2020-2021 climb, while a close above the 1.3150 10-day moving average would end the downside bias.\par \par \uc2 To read our latest \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  news and analysis services, click on\par \par \uc2 Released: 2022-3-18T05:47:57.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020220315ei3f00qe1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
