\par\par \b \uc2 *S&PGR Puts Fortum And \uc2 Uniper\uc2  'BBB' Ratings On CreditWatch Neg\b0\par\par\uc2 4,079 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2022\par 15:02\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2022, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 14 Mar 2022 10:02 ET  Press Release: S&PGR Puts Fortum And \uc2 Uniper\uc2   'BBB' Ratings On CreditWatch Neg\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par The following is a press release from S&P Global Ratings: \par  \par      -- Amid the Russia-Ukraine conflict, we see significant risks to \f28 \uc2 \f2 Fortum\f28 \uc2 \f2   \par group's financials from the heightened risk of disruption to its natural gas \par procurement from Russian gas giant \f28 \uc2 \f2 Gazprom\f28 \uc2 \f2  , which supplies about 50% of the \par company's annual total of about 370 terawatt-hours (TWh). \par      -- If this unprecedented situation materializes, the group could be \par exposed to high market risk. The group may need to buy missing volumes on the \par energy market at very high prices to fulfill its gas sales commitments. The \par downside from this market price exposure is difficult to quantify, given the \par significant size of the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , extremely volatile gas prices, and \par unknowns regarding potential government \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to mitigate such risks. \par      -- Furthermore, we believe the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Russia\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 -based subsidiaries of Germany \par energy company \f28 \uc2 \f2 Uniper\f28 \uc2 \f2   and its parent \f28 \uc2 \f2 Fortum\f28 \uc2 \f2   will be unable to transfer \par earnings outside of Russia, implying a 20% drop in EBITDA contributions. That \par said, both companies have ample headroom in their credit metrics and financial \par flexibility, which could buffer the loss of earnings contributions. \par      -- As a result, we placed our 'BBB/A-2' long- and short-term ratings on \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Fortum\f28 \uc2 \f2   and our 'BBB' long-term rating on \f28 \uc2 \f2 Uniper\f28 \uc2 \f2   on CreditWatch with negative \par implications. \par      -- The CreditWatch placements reflects the possibility that we could \par downgrade \f28 \uc2 \f2 Fortum\f28 \uc2 \f2   and \f28 \uc2 \f2 Uniper\f28 \uc2 \f2   in case of significant gas market disruptions. We \par aim to resolve the CreditWatch in the coming months, depending on the \par evolution of market and geopolitical conditions and how resilient both \par companies are to them. \par  \par NEW YORK (S&P Global Ratings) March 14, 2022--S&P Global Ratings today said it \par placed its 'BBB/A-2' long- and short-term ratings on \f28 \uc2 \f2 Fortum Oyj\f28 \uc2 \f2   and its 'BBB' \par long-term rating on \f28 \uc2 \f2 Uniper SE\f28 \uc2 \f2   on CreditWatch with negative implications. \par  \par We see potential downside pressure to the group's financials given the \par elevated risk that its long-term gas procurement contracts with \f28 \uc2 \f2 Gazprom\f28 \uc2 \f2   could \par be disrupted. \par The risk of disruptions to \f28 \uc2 \f2 Fortum\f28 \uc2 \f2  's subsidiary \f28 \uc2 \f2 Uniper\f28 \uc2 \f2  's gas procurement from \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Gazprom PJSC\f28 \uc2 \f2   (CCC-/Watch Neg/--) has rapidly increased over the past few days. \par Disruptions to business could be significant, given that \f28 \uc2 \f2 Gazprom\f28 \uc2 \f2   supplies \par about 200TWh of \f28 \uc2 \f2 Uniper\f28 \uc2 \f2  's 370 TWh of annual natural gas procurement. Prices of \par natural gas, namely Dutch TFF spot prices, have been fluctuating between \par EUR100 and more than EUR200 per megawatt-hour (MWh) since the end of February, \par which compares with less than EUR25/MWh over past years up until mid-2021. \par  \par We expect that a severe disruption to Germany's gas import would trigger a \par state of emergency under the German legal framework, making the German \par regulator Federal Network Agency (\f28 \uc2 \f2 BNetzA\f28 \uc2 \f2  ) the emergency authority for \par demand-side steering, in coordination with and monitored by EU authorities. \par But we lack visibility on how effectively and quickly this would mitigate \par pressure on \f28 \uc2 \f2 Uniper\f28 \uc2 \f2  's financials. Additionally, we don't have visibility on the \par extent to which this would invoke timely majeure/market disruption clauses on \par contract obligations with Western counterparties. \par  \par Meaningful disruption to gas import could have significant financial impact. \par Cash outflows for margin movements over recent months indicate the magnitude \par of this gas wholesale market exposure (consolidated \f28 \uc2 \f2 Fortum\f28 \uc2 \f2   margin receivables \par of EUR9.2 billion at the end of 2021). According to \f28 \uc2 \f2 Fortum\f28 \uc2 \f2  's management, the \par company received inflows in terms of unwinding of variation margins of EUR2.3 \par billion in January and February this year. In general, we consider risks \par associated with large volume hedging arrangements elevated in a volatile \par commodity market environment, in particular, access to liquidity is dependent \par on market sentiment and counterparty confidence in the credit quality of the \par company (see "Uniper Ratings Affirmed Following Commodity Price Surge, \par Increased Liquidity Needs In A Volatile Market; Outlook Stable," published \par Jan. 21, 2022, On RatingsDirect). Generally, we consider that risks associated \par with large volume hedging could include a rapid increase in margin \par requirements and reduced access to liquidity and funding. \par  \par In recent weeks, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Uniper\f28 \uc2 \f2   has been able to introduce measures to significantly \par reduce its exposure to margin postings whose effectiveness we are witnessing \par in the light of recent reduced margin posting requirements, despite the \par current gas-price surge. We are monitoring how the company's risk management \par reduces its exposure to margining requirements or procures sources of \par liquidity on a permanent basis. \par  \par We believe that the Russia-based \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of \f28 \uc2 \f2 Fortum\f28 \uc2 \f2   and \f28 \uc2 \f2 Uniper\f28 \uc2 \f2   will be \par unable to transfer earnings outside of Russia, implying a 20% drop in EBITDA \par contributions. \par Russia's military conflict with Ukraine has prompted a new round of \f28 \uc2 \f2 G7\f28 \uc2 \f2   \par government sanctions, including targeting the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves of The \par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of Russia\f28 \uc2 \f2   (\f28 \uc2 \f2 CBR\f28 \uc2 \f2  ); this has rendered a large part of these reserves \par inaccessible, undermining the \f28 \uc2 \f2 CBR\f28 \uc2 \f2  's ability to act as a lender of last resort \par and impairing what had been--until recently--Russia's key credit strength: its \par net external liquidity position. To mitigate the resulting high exchange rate \par and financial market volatility, and to preserve remaining foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  \par buffers, Russia's authorities have--among other steps--introduced capital \par controls that we understand could constrain nonresident government bondholders \par from receiving interest and principal payments on time. We also see a \par heightened risk of expropriation of any assets in Russia. \par  \par Consequently, we do not expect the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Fortum\f28 \uc2 \f2   group will receive any dividends \par from its Russian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . A permanent exclusion of Russian exposure could \par lead us to revise the group's country risk exposure. We acknowledge that \par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  are still ongoing, but \f28 \uc2 \f2 Fortum\f28 \uc2 \f2   and \f28 \uc2 \f2 Uniper\f28 \uc2 \f2   have announced that no \par additional investments will be made in Russia and the sought-after divestment \par of \f28 \uc2 \f2 Uniper\f28 \uc2 \f2  's Russian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Unipro (started in 2021) will be resumed as \par soon as feasible. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Uniper\f28 \uc2 \f2   also wrote down its EUR1.0 billion (non-cash \par effective) loan and accrued interest in \f28 \uc2 \f2 Nord Stream 2\f28 \uc2 \f2   and announced it will no \par longer renew long-term gas contracts with \f28 \uc2 \f2 Gazprom\f28 \uc2 \f2   as these come due. \par      -- \f28 \uc2 \f2 Uniper\f28 \uc2 \f2  's Russian subsidiary contributed to about 16% of its EBITDA in \par 2021 and has represented about 15%-20% of EBITDA historically. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Uniper\f28 \uc2 \f2   has \par installed coal and gas generation units in Russia of about 11 gigawatt (GW) \par that receive capacity payments. \par      -- \f28 \uc2 \f2 Fortum\f28 \uc2 \f2  's reported comparable EBITDA amounted to EUR3.8 billion in \par 2021, and its Russian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  represented about 20% of EBITDA in 2021. \par While the share of earnings is relative low, we acknowledge that the \par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  are sizable, with in total 4.67 GW of installed capacity, \par constituting about 35% of \f28 \uc2 \f2 Fortum\f28 \uc2 \f2  's installed capacity generation fleet. That \par said, we expected earnings will decrease further to about EUR240 million by \par 2023, as Russian capacity supply agreement contracts related to thermal \par generation expire. \par  \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Fortum\f28 \uc2 \f2  's and \f28 \uc2 \f2 Uniper\f28 \uc2 \f2  's recent operating performance and deleveraging have been \par above our previous base case, which could mitigate the abrupt stop in \par contributions from its Russian business. \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Fortum\f28 \uc2 \f2   and \f28 \uc2 \f2 Uniper\f28 \uc2 \f2   both benefited from high commodity prices, especially power \par and gas in 2021, and their operating performance in 2021 was well above our \par previous expectations. To some extent, this mitigates the stop of \par contributions from the Russian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , at least in the short term. \par      -- \f28 \uc2 \f2 Fortum\f28 \uc2 \f2  's reported consolidated comparable EBITDA for 2021 amounted to \par about EUR3.8 billion, which is EUR0.7 billion-EUR1.0 billion above our \par previous expectations of EUR2.9 billion-EUR3.2 billion, which compares with \par EUR404 million in EBITDA from \f28 \uc2 \f2 Fortum\f28 \uc2 \f2  's Russian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operation\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . Credit ratios, such \par as funds from \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  (FFO) to debt at year-end therefore stood above our \par expected ratio of 35%-50% (netting the impact of margin receivables and \par collaterals). \par      -- In 2021, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Uniper\f28 \uc2 \f2   generated EBITDA of about EUR1.9 billion compared with \par our previous expectation of above EUR1.5 billion--and adjusted debt was \par negative compared with about EUR2.0 billion anticipated previously (see "Full \par Analysis: \f28 \uc2 \f2 Uniper\f28 \uc2 \f2  ," published Sept. 3, 2021). Next to the operating performance \par described above, the latter has benefitted by about EUR300 million from \par pension discount rate movement. \par  \par We believe electricity prices are likely to remain elevated, at least until \par the mid-2020s. Despite large parts of its generation being hedged, the group's \par nuclear and hydro generation units should benefit from favorable market prices \par and outperform our previous expectations. Additionally, we believe current \par market conditions are beneficial to \f28 \uc2 \f2 Uniper\f28 \uc2 \f2  's storage business, its non-Russian \par natural gas procurement business, and liquified natural gas business. \par  \par We believe that the commodity price surge and risk of disruptions could \par increase liquidity needs, and therefore revised our assessment of \f28 \uc2 \f2 Fortum\f28 \uc2 \f2  's \par liquidity to adequate from strong. \par In January, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Fortum\f28 \uc2 \f2   provided \f28 \uc2 \f2 Uniper\f28 \uc2 \f2   liquidity support as a result of extremely \par high and volatile gas prices and needed to provide additional margining \par payments for its hedging positions (see "Uniper Ratings Affirmed Following \par Commodity Price Surge, Increased Liquidity Needs In A Volatile Market; Outlook \par Stable," and "Finland-Based Power And Gas Company Fortum 'BBB/A-2 Ratings \par Affirmed; Outlook Stable," both published Jan. 21, 2022). As such, we cannot \par exclude the risk of increased working capital volatility to cover its \par positions as a result of the turbulent market situation and extreme changes in \par energy prices. As such, we now view \f28 \uc2 \f2 Fortum\f28 \uc2 \f2  's liquidity as adequate. This is \par based on our estimate that its liquidity resources, including cash, FFO, and \par facility availability, will cover expected cash outflow by more than 1.4x in \par the 12 months started Jan. 1, 2022, on a consolidated basis. In light of our \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 14 Mar 2022 10:02 ET \par Press Release: S&PGR Puts Fortum And \f28 \uc2 \f2 Uniper\f28 \uc2 \f2   'BBB' -2-\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par liquidity assessment, we continue to monitor how the company's risk management \par reduces its exposure to margining requirements or procures sources of \par liquidity on a permanent basis. \par  \par We note that \f28 \uc2 \f2 Fortum\f28 \uc2 \f2   and \f28 \uc2 \f2 Uniper\f28 \uc2 \f2   have access to an additional EUR5.9 billion of \par short-term revolving credit facilities, of which EUR2.0 billion is from the \par German state-owned development bank \f28 \uc2 \f2 KfW\f28 \uc2 \f2  , which signals the importance of the \par group. However, since the facilities are maturing within 12 months, we do not \par incorporate them in our liquidity calculations, notwithstanding the current \par process with the German state to extend the \f28 \uc2 \f2 KfW\f28 \uc2 \f2   line. \par  \par S&P Global Ratings acknowledges a high degree of uncertainty about the extent, \par outcome, and consequences of the military conflict between Russia and Ukraine. \par Irrespective of the duration of military hostilities, sanctions and related \par political risks are likely to remain in place for some time. Potential effects \par could include dislocated commodities markets--notably for oil and gas--supply \par chain disruptions, inflationary pressures, weaker growth, and capital market \par volatility. As the situation evolves, we will update our assumptions and \par estimates accordingly. See our macroeconomic and credit updates here: \par onflict"". Note that the timing of publication for rating decisions on \par European issuers is subject to European regulatory requirements. \par  \par COMPANY DESCRIPTION \par Fortum \par Fortum is the third-largest power generator in Europe and Russia, with about \par 50 GW of installed capacity in total. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Fortum\f28 \uc2 \f2   has about 8,000 employees, and \par about 20,000 including \f28 \uc2 \f2 Uniper\f28 \uc2 \f2  . In addition to its strong position in \par generation, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Fortum\f28 \uc2 \f2   is the third-largest CO2-free generator in Europe because \par of its zero-to-low CO2emission fleet, with 45% of its generation fleet being \par CO2-free. The remaining share of the fleet is geared toward gas, and carbon \par intensity from total energy production in Europe were 188 gram of CO2 per \par kilowatt-kWh in 2020. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Fortum\f28 \uc2 \f2   engages in the generation and sale of electricity \par and heat in the Nordic countries, Russia, Poland, and Central Europe. It has \par the following five segments: Generation, 33% of 2021 EBITDA, City Solutions \par (8%), Consumer Solutions (3%): Russia (10%) and \f28 \uc2 \f2 Uniper\f28 \uc2 \f2   (45%). \par  \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Fortum\f28 \uc2 \f2   held 78.0% of \f28 \uc2 \f2 Uniper\f28 \uc2 \f2   as of Dec. 31, 2021, increased from 76.1% at the \par end of 2020. \par  \par Uniper \par Uniper is an international, diversified energy company that operates in more \par than 40 countries and has about 12,000 employees. Its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  include power \par generation, commodity trading, energy storage, energy sales, and energy \par services. Its core markets are Germany, Russia, the U.K., Sweden, the \par Netherlands, and North America. \par  \par The company owns and operates a well-diversified power generation portfolio, \par including facilities running on fossil fuels such as gas (17 GW as of Dec. 31, \par 2021) and coal (8.6 GW), as well as hydroelectric (3.6 GW) and Swedish nuclear \par (1.4 GW). It has a total generation capacity of 33.3 GW of which one-third is \par in Russia. In 2021, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Uniper\f28 \uc2 \f2   produced 109.1 TWh of electricity, of which 54% \par stemmed from gas and 24% from carbon-free hydroelectric and nuclear \par generation. \par  \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Uniper\f28 \uc2 \f2   was created in 2016 from the spinoff of the gas and power activities of \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 E.ON SE\f28 \uc2 \f2  . \f28 \uc2 \f2 Fortum\f28 \uc2 \f2   owns about 78% of \f28 \uc2 \f2 Uniper\f28 \uc2 \f2  . \par  \par CREDITWATCH \par We aim to resolve the CreditWatch within the coming months, depending on the \par evolution of market and geopolitical conditions and how resilient both \par companies are to them. \par  \par We expect to resolve the CreditWatch placement on both companies once we have \par clarity on the potential disruption of Russian gas flows and the corresponding \par full impact on the group. When we resolve the CreditWatch, we will also \par consider the extent of government support and legal remedies. \par  \par In case of a downgrade, we see a lowering of the rating by one-notch as most \par likely for both entities, but we cannot fully exclude a multi-notch downgrade, \par ultimately depending on how severely the market risk exposure materializes. \par  \par We could downgrade \f28 \uc2 \f2 Fortum\f28 \uc2 \f2  , if we expect its credit metrics to deteriorate such \par that FFO to debt falls below 35% or debt to EBITDA increases above 2.5x \par (excluding margin impact). \par  \par As per our assessment of \f28 \uc2 \f2 Uniper\f28 \uc2 \f2  's core group status within \f28 \uc2 \f2 Fortum\f28 \uc2 \f2  , our \par long-term issuer credit rating on \f28 \uc2 \f2 Uniper\f28 \uc2 \f2   is at the same level as our rating on \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Fortum\f28 \uc2 \f2  , or one notch lower, depending on \f28 \uc2 \f2 Uniper\f28 \uc2 \f2  's stand-alone credit profile \par (SACP). \par  \par We could revise downward our assessment of \f28 \uc2 \f2 Uniper\f28 \uc2 \f2  's SACP of 'bbb', if we \par expect FFO to debt to deteriorate below 55% and debt to EBTIDA above 1.7x \par (excluding margin impact and including lease income). \par  \par We could revise these ratios when we resolve the CreditWatch, depending on the \par evolving business mix, including how we expect the operating environment for \par the gas business in Europe to develop. \par  \par LIQUIDITY \par We view consolidated \f28 \uc2 \f2 Fortum\f28 \uc2 \f2  's liquidity as adequate. This is based on our \par estimate that its liquidity resources, including cash, FFO, and facility \par availability, will cover expected cash outflow by more than 1.4x in the 12 \par months started Jan. 1, 2022. \par  \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Fortum\f28 \uc2 \f2  's liquidity, as of end-December 2021, continues to be supported by the \par group's access to in capital markets and sound relationships, which was proven \par over December 2021-January 2022 with the signing of new bank facilities with \par short notice. Our view of the group's sound banking relationship was further \par supported by our discussions with some of the group's banks. Alongside the \par long-term credit lines we weigh into our calculation, we note \f28 \uc2 \f2 Fortum\f28 \uc2 \f2  's access \par to shorter-term credit facilities. Its debt documents do not contain any \par financial covenants. We also note that \f28 \uc2 \f2 Uniper\f28 \uc2 \f2   has an committed revolving \par credit facility with \f28 \uc2 \f2 KfW\f28 \uc2 \f2   amounting to EUR2.0 billion. However, since the \par facilities are maturing within 12 months, we do not incorporate them in our \par liquidity calculations, notwithstanding the current process with the German \par state to extend the \f28 \uc2 \f2 KfW\f28 \uc2 \f2   line. \par  \par Principal liquidity sources for the group as per of end-December 2021: \par      -- Our estimate of cash and cash equivalents of about EUR7.4 billion; \par      -- Access to EUR400 million of undrawn facilities maturing after 12 \par months; \par      -- Positive cash FFO, which we estimate will near EUR2.3 billion in the \par next 12 months; and \par      -- Working capital inflows in terms of unwinding of variation margins of \par EUR2.3 billion (received in January and February). \par  \par Principal liquidity uses for the group as of the same date: \par      -- Debt of about EUR6.6 billion maturing in the next 12 months; \par      -- Maintenance capex of about EUR630 million; \par      -- Dividends of about EUR1.2 billion in the next 12 months. \par  \par ENVIRONMENTAL, SOCIAL, AND GOVERNANCE \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Fortum\f28 \uc2 \f2   \par ESG Credit Indicators: E-4, S-2, G-3 \par  \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Uniper\f28 \uc2 \f2   \par ESG Credit Indicators: E-4, S-2, G-3 \par  \par RELATED CRITERIA