\par\par \b \uc2 *S&PGR Downgrades \uc2 Eurasian Development Bank\uc2  ; On Watch Neg\b0\par\par\uc2 2,494 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2022\par 16:32\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2022, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 11 Mar 2022 10:32 ET  Press Release: S&PGR Downgrades \uc2 Eurasian Development Bank\uc2  ; On Watch Neg\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par The following is a press release from S&P Global Ratings: \par  \par      -- We expect that the current challenging and volatile operating \par environment will pose risks to \f28 \uc2 \f2 Eurasian Development Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2  's (\f28 \uc2 \f2 EDB\f28 \uc2 \f2  ) loan \par portfolio growth and therefore its policy relevance. We also think its ability \par to access funding in the capital markets could be curtailed. \par      -- We foresee a heightened probability of rapid asset quality \par deterioration that will weigh on \f28 \uc2 \f2 EDB\f28 \uc2 \f2  's capital adequacy position over the next \par three months given the high concentration of its loan exposures to Russia and \par Belarus. \par      -- We therefore lowered our long- and short-term foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  issuer \par credit ratings on \f28 \uc2 \f2 EDB\f28 \uc2 \f2   to 'BBB-/A-3' from 'BBB/A-2' and placed them on \par CreditWatch with negative implications. We also placed the 'kzAAA' Kazakhstan \par national scale issuer credit rating on CreditWatch with negative implications. \par  \par NEW YORK (S&P Global Ratings) March 11, 2022--S&P Global Ratings today lowered \par its foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  credit ratings on \f28 \uc2 \f2 EDB\f28 \uc2 \f2   to 'BBB-/A-3' from 'BBB/A-2 and \par placed our ratings on CreditWatch with negative implications. \par  \par At the same time, we placed our 'kzAAA' Kazakhstan national scale rating on \par CreditWatch with negative implications. \par  \par CREDITWATCH \par We expect to resolve the CreditWatch placement in the next 90 days when we \par have more clarity on how the significant economic and financial stress in both \par Russia and Belarus will affect \f28 \uc2 \f2 EDB\f28 \uc2 \f2  's loan portfolio, and once we understand \par the consequences for its capital base and financial risk profile. The negative \par implications of the CreditWatch indicate that we could lower our ratings on \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 EDB\f28 \uc2 \f2   during the 90-day period. \par  \par The downgrade reflects our view that, given the challenging operating \par environment, \f28 \uc2 \f2 EDB\f28 \uc2 \f2  's loan portfolio growth will likely remain subdued and weigh \par on its policy relevance. Its ability to access funding in the capital markets \par if needed also remains uncertain. We also see a further downgrade as highly \par likely given that \f28 \uc2 \f2 EDB\f28 \uc2 \f2  's asset quality could rapidly weaken, reflecting its \par large lending concentration to Russia and Belarus. Combined, they account for \par about 60% of the lending portfolio as of June 2021. \par  \par While it operates as a regional multilateral institution, \f28 \uc2 \f2 EDB\f28 \uc2 \f2   has a high \par concentration in Russia and Belarus. Both countries are under severe sanctions \par due to the military \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in Ukraine. We expect business growth to be \par subdued and any new lending to Russia and Belarus to be marginal. We think the \par bank's lending activity will likely reduce, which will continue to constrain \par our view of its mandate and overall rating. \par  \par As of June 2021, \f28 \uc2 \f2 EDB\f28 \uc2 \f2   had posted very robust funding and liquidity ratios (and \par we do not expect this will have changed materially). However, if the military \par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is protracted and sanctions on \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Russia\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  remain for a long time, \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 EDB\f28 \uc2 \f2  's access to funding from the international capital markets, when and if \par needed, could become uncertain. \par  \par As of today, \f28 \uc2 \f2 EDB\f28 \uc2 \f2   has two outstanding international bond issuances: US$500 \par million due in September 2022 and EUR300 million due in March 2026. As of June \par 2021, the bank also had local debt issuance of US$879 million, of which about \par US$170 million matures in the next 12 months. Therefore, the total remaining \par debt maturities in 2022 are US$670 million. \par  \par Also as of June 30, 2021, \f28 \uc2 \f2 EDB\f28 \uc2 \f2   had US$357 million in customer deposits, which \par have been sticky historically. In 2018, the bank started to provide settlement \par and clearing services to its customers. As of June 30, 2021, all deposits were \par from corporate customers based in the bank's member states, of which more than \par 60% was from state owned companies. \par  \par On June 30, 2021, the bank's liquid assets totalled approximately US$1.35 \par billion. Therefore, we expect the bank to be able to cover upcoming maturities \par in 2022 as well as a potential withdrawal of deposits without any new market \par funding. As of June 2021, we consider the bank to be properly hedged from a \par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  perspective with a significant surplus of U.S. dollar assets. \par  \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 EDB\f28 \uc2 \f2   should be exempted, as per its charter and in line with other multilateral \par lending institutions (MLIs), from \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  restrictions imposed by member \par governments--which in this case would be \f28 \uc2 \f2 Russia's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  . \par  \par Furthermore, \f28 \uc2 \f2 EDB\f28 \uc2 \f2   is not on any sanctions list issued by the U.S, EU , or the \par U.K., and therefore should be able to receive foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  from borrowers \par (residents and nonresidents) as well as execute outgoing \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and \par local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  payments. However, this remains to be fully tested in these \par unprecedented times. \par  \par The increasing risk of Russia and/or Belarus defaulting--reflected in our \par recent downgrades of both sovereigns--has already negatively affected \f28 \uc2 \f2 EDB\f28 \uc2 \f2  's \par risk-adjusted capital (\f28 \uc2 \f2 RAC\f28 \uc2 \f2 ) ratio. After MLI adjustments, this ratio has \par reduced to 16% from 20% (based on financial data as of June 2021). The \f28 \uc2 \f2 RAC\f28 \uc2 \f2  \par reduction has pushed the bank's capital adequacy very close to a weaker \par assessment. Given the ratings on both sovereigns are on CreditWatch negative, \par any further weakening in their credit standings could weigh further on \f28 \uc2 \f2 EDB\f28 \uc2 \f2  's \par capital adequacy in the near term. We also think the US$700 million capital \par increase assumed under the bank's 2022-2026 strategy will likely be delayed. \par  \par Following our action on Russia, we also downgraded all our rated Russia-based \par corporates by several notches (see "Russian Corporates Downgraded To 'CCC-' \par After Similar Action On Sovereign; Ratings Placed On CreditWatch Negative," \par March 7, 2022). Although \f28 \uc2 \f2 EDB\f28 \uc2 \f2  's portfolio in both countries is highly \par concentrated in state-owned companies and quasi-sovereign institutions and the \par majority of its Russian loans are in roubles, we expect significant asset \par quality pressure could build in the near term. Asset-quality deterioration \par could weigh on \f28 \uc2 \f2 EDB\f28 \uc2 \f2  's S&P Global Ratings-adjusted \f28 \uc2 \f2 RAC\f28 \uc2 \f2  ratio and indicate a less \par favorable loss experience, which could weaken our view of its capital \par adequacy. This could lead us to take a further negative rating action. \par  \par Our long-term rating does not incorporate extraordinary shareholder support \par from \f28 \uc2 \f2 EDB\f28 \uc2 \f2  's callable capital. \par This is because, while our sovereign rating on Kazakhstan is at the level of \par our 'bbb-' assessment of \f28 \uc2 \f2 EDB\f28 \uc2 \f2  's stand-alone credit profile (SACP), our \par assessment of the bank's policy importance as moderate precludes the notion of \par extraordinary support. \par  \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 EDB\f28 \uc2 \f2  's enterprise risk profile (ERP) is very weak, in our view. \par Our assessment is constrained by the bank's limited geographic diversification \par compared with other supranational peers and its highly idiosyncratic ownership \par structure, with Russia and Kazakhstan accounting for 99% of shares. Our ERP \par assessment also factors in the bank's inability to meet its growth targets \par previously, as well as the heightened challenges it faces to pursue its \par business goals. \par  \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 EDB\f28 \uc2 \f2   is the key MLI for the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Eurasian Economic Union\f28 \uc2 \f2  . \par Established in 2006 by an intergovernmental agreement, \f28 \uc2 \f2 EDB\f28 \uc2 \f2  's purpose is to \par contribute to the development and growth of the market economy in member \par states and promote trade and economic integration among them by engaging in \par investment activities. \f28 \uc2 \f2 EDB\f28 \uc2 \f2   has improved its role and competitive position in \par its member countries, shown by the significant increase in the loan portfolio \par in 2018 and 2019, but it has remained very small compared with the economies \par of its members. \f28 \uc2 \f2 EDB\f28 \uc2 \f2   is owned by Russia (65.97%), Kazakhstan (32.99%), Belarus \par (0.99%), Armenia (0.01%), Tajikistan (0.03%), and Kyrgyzstan (0.01%). \par  \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 EDB\f28 \uc2 \f2  's private-sector-focused mandate precludes it from being treated as a \par preferred creditor. \par This is because private-sector companies cannot selectively default to one \par group of creditors while paying others as sovereigns can. We therefore do not \par incorporate preferred creditor treatment (PCT) in our assessment of the \par enterprise risk profile. However, given \f28 \uc2 \f2 EDB\f28 \uc2 \f2  's status as an MLI, we do consider \par PCT granted by the governments of countries in which it operates, and we \par incorporate this into our assessment of \f28 \uc2 \f2 EDB\f28 \uc2 \f2  's financial risk profile. Contrary \par to our current expectations, a weaker performance of sovereign-related \par exposures could also weigh on our adjusted \f28 \uc2 \f2 RAC\f28 \uc2 \f2  ratio in the future. \par  \par Environmental, social, and governance (ESG) credit factors for this change in \par credit rating/outlook and/or CreditWatch status: \par      -- Other governance factors \par  \par RELATED CRITERIA \par  \par      -- General Criteria: Environmental, Social, And Governance Principles In \par Credit Ratings, Oct. 10, 2021 \par      -- Criteria | Governments | General: Multilateral Lending Institutions \par And Other Supranational Institutions Ratings Methodology, Dec. 14, 2018 \par      -- General Criteria: Methodology For National And Regional Scale Credit \par Ratings, June 25, 2018 \par      -- Criteria | Financial Institutions | General: Risk-Adjusted Capital \par Framework Methodology, July 20, 2017 \par      -- General Criteria: Methodology For Linking Long-Term And Short-Term \par Ratings, April 7, 2017 \par      -- General Criteria: Principles Of Credit Ratings, Feb. 16, 2011 \par  \par RELATED RESEARCH \par      -- Russian Corporates Downgraded To 'CCC-' After Similar Action On \par Sovereign; Ratings Placed On CreditWatch Negative, March 7, 2022 \par      -- Belarus Ratings Lowered To '\f28 \uc2 \f2 CCC\f28 \uc2 \f2  ' And Placed On CreditWatch Negative On \par Mounting Risks Related To Russia-Ukraine Conflict, March 4, 2022 \par      -- Russia Ratings Lowered To 'CCC -' And Kept On CreditWatch Negative On \par Increasing Risk Of Default, March 3, 2022 \par  \par Certain terms used in this report, particularly certain adjectives used to \par express our view on rating relevant factors, have specific meanings ascribed \par to them in our criteria, and should therefore be read in conjunction with such \par criteria. Please see Ratings Criteria at www.standardandpoors.com for further \par information. A description of each of S&P Global Ratings' rating categories is \par contained in "S&P Global Ratings Definitions" at \par  \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.standardandpoors.com/en_US/web/guest/article/-/view/sourceId/504352"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://www.standardandpoors.com/en_US/web/guest/article/-/view/sourceId/504352 }}}\par \par \uc2 \f2 11 Mar 2022 10:32 ET \par Press Release: S&PGR Downgrades Eurasian -2-\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Complete ratings information is available to subscribers of RatingsDirect at \par \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.capitaliq.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 www.capitaliq.com}}}\uc2 \f2 . All ratings affected by this rating action can be found on \par S&P Global Ratings' public website at \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.standardandpoors.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 www.standardandpoors.com}}}\uc2 \f2 . Use the \par Ratings search box located in the left column.  Alternatively, call one of the \par following S&P Global Ratings numbers: Client Support Europe (44) 20-7176-7176; \par London Press Office (44) 20-7176-3605; Paris (33) 1-4420-6708; Frankfurt (49) \par 69-33-999-225; or Stockholm (46) 8-440-5914 \par  \par Primary Credit Analyst: \f28 \uc2 \f2 Standard & Poor's\f28 \uc2 \f2  , New York \par Additional Contact: Sovereign and I