\par\par \b \uc2 *S&PGR Affirms Kazakhstan Ratings At 'BBB-/A-3'; Outlook Stable\b0\par\par\uc2 5,035 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2022\par 22:07\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2022, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 4 Mar 2022 16:08 ET  Press Release: S&PGR Affirms Kazakhstan Ratings At 'BBB-/A-3'; Outlook Stable\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par The following is a press release from S&P Global Ratings: \par  \par OVERVIEW \par      -- The mass protests and civil unrest in January 2022 reflect the \par structural weakness and fragility of Kazakhstan's institutional framework, in \par our view. \par      -- Fallout from \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Russia\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's military \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  against Ukraine poses \par manageable risks to Kazakhstan's macroeconomic stability at this time. \par      -- We expect Kazakhstan's strong government and external balance sheets, \par along with elevated oil prices and expanding production, to support the rating \par despite institutional shortcomings and event risk. \par      -- We therefore affirmed our 'BBB-/A-3' foreign and local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  \par sovereign credit ratings on Kazakhstan and maintained the stable outlook. \par  \par RATING ACTION \par On March 4, 2022, S&P Global Ratings affirmed its 'BBB-/A-3' long- and \par short-term foreign and local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  sovereign credit ratings on Kazakhstan. \par The outlook on the long-term ratings is stable. \par  \par We also affirmed our 'kzAAA' national scale rating on Kazakhstan. \par  \par The transfer and convertibility assessment remains 'BBB'. \par  \par OUTLOOK \par The stable outlook reflects our expectation that the government and external \par balance sheets will remain strong over the next two years. We assume these \par buffers will be sufficient to mitigate potential macroeconomic instability \par arising from Kazakhstan's close links to Russia. \par Upside scenario \par We could raise the ratings if we see a meaningful improvement in the health of \par the banking sector. This could, for example, be supported by further advances \par in regulatory oversight or improvements in corporate governance, improving our \par view on monetary policy effectiveness and reducing risks to the government's \par fiscal position. \par  \par We could also raise the ratings if we see improvements in institutional and \par policymaking effectiveness, evidenced for instance, by significant progress on \par devolution of power, increased transparency and accountability of \par institutions, and a strengthened business environment. \par  \par Downside scenario \par We could lower the ratings if fallout from \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Russia\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's military \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  \par against Ukraine hits Kazakhstan's external position, for example, if there is \par a significant depletion of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves to stabilize the \par Kazakhstani tenge (KZT) market. We could also lower the ratings if the fiscal \par position deteriorates, for instance, from a material increase in government \par spending to support the economy, or a decline in revenue due to reduced \par foreign trade activity. \par  \par A deterioration in domestic stability, shown via further incidents of severe \par civil unrest, could also pressure the ratings. \par  \par RATIONALE \par The sudden and acute outburst of civil unrest in early January highlighted \par shortcomings in Kazakhstan's institutional framework. The centralized \par decision-making inherent in Kazakhstan's institutional settings continues to \par cloud the predictability of government decision-making and the protests show \par there is a latent risk of social upheaval, in our view, absent the \par implementation of government policy that is more responsive to societal \par priorities. \par  \par We do not currently expect our ratings on Kazakhstan to be affected by the \par Russian military \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  against Ukraine. However, there are contagion \par risks, which in the first instance are being transmitted to Kazakhstan via the \par correlation between the KZT and the Russian ruble (RUB). The KZT has tumbled \par almost 15% from beginning of the year to March 1, 2022. KZT trading was \par partially halted on Feb. 28 when the base rate was moved to 13.50% from 10.25% \par and the \f28 \uc2 \f2 National Bank of Kazakhstan\f28 \uc2 \f2   (\f28 \uc2 \f2 NBK\f28 \uc2 \f2 ; the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ) \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . The \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 NBK\f28 \uc2 \f2  remains committed to the principles of a floating exchange rate but has \par the right to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to smoothen KZT volatility. We currently assume any \par secondary effects of economic sanctions on Russia will be manageable and do \par not foresee a significant disruption to Kazakhstan's access to its key oil and \par gas export markets. \par  \par In addition to concerns over the institutional framework, the ratings remain \par constrained by challenges to monetary policy credibility, such as the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central \par bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's perceived limited, albeit improving, independence and the relatively \par weak banking system, which is small compared to the economy. In addition, the \par economy remains dependent on hydrocarbons and economic wealth is moderate, as \par measured by GDP per capita. \par  \par The ratings on Kazakhstan are supported by the country's strong fiscal and \par external balance sheets. These were built primarily with budgetary surpluses \par generated during the period of high commodity prices that ended in late 2014. \par The related assets were accumulated in the National Fund of the Republic of \par Kazakhstan (NFRK) and largely invested abroad. We forecast that the government \par will remain in a net asset position and the economy's liquid external assets \par will exceed external debt. \par  \par Institutional and economic profile: Growth over 2022-2025 will be supported by \par both the oil and non-oil sectors \par      -- We forecast the economy will expand 3.6% in 2022 and average just \par under 4% growth to 2025. \par      -- Diversification of the economy, developing the private sector, and \par increasing population wealth levels will remain policy priorities. \par      -- We expect President \f28 \uc2 \f2 Kassym-Jomart Tokayev\f28 \uc2 \f2   will serve his term to 2024 \par and any political reforms to be modest. \par  \par Kazakhstan and Russia are closely connected through political, financial, and \par economic links. The two are also close allies, with Russia notably providing a \par peacekeeping mission in Kazakhstan to restore domestic stability in January. \par However, we understand that Kazakhstan is not considering sending its troops \par to join Russian forces in Ukraine and the government has not recognized the \par break-away Luhansk People's Republic and the Donetsk People's Republic. In \par addition to Russia, Kazakhstan maintains strong relationships with China, \par which has invested heavily in the country over the past 10 years, and the EU, \par where most of Kazakhstan's oil is sold. We expect Kazakhstan will remain \par neutral in its diplomatic stance toward the Russia-Ukraine conflict, and not \par to attract direct sanctions. The secondary hit from sanctions imposed on \par Russia could make it more difficult for Kazakhstan to trade with or export its \par oil through Russia. However, we currently do not see strong evidence to this \par effect. \par  \par Kazakhstan and Russia share the second-longest land border in the world and \par about 20% of Kazakhstan's population is ethnically Russian. Russia is also a \par key trading partner, accounting for almost one-quarter of total trade turnover \par in first-half 2021. Furthermore, Kazakhstan is part of the Eurasian Economic \par Union --a customs union--with Russia, Belarus, Armenia, and Kyrgyzstan. \par However, unlike some other Central Asian countries, Kazakhstan's economic \par activity is not dependent on remittances from Russia. The KZT and RUB are \par closely correlated because of the similar economic structures and close trade \par ties between the two countries. The \f28 \uc2 \f2 NBK\f28 \uc2 \f2 's monetary policy often coincides with \par that of the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of Russia\f28 \uc2 \f2  . Europe is the main destination for \par Kazakhstan's oil exports, which are transported via two main pipelines that \par pass through Russia. \par  \par In early January, protests quickly spread throughout Kazakhstan. They were \par ignited by an increase in the price of liquified petroleum gas but expanded to \par encompass broader grievances, including persistent inequality, low wages and \par living standards, aging infrastructure, constraints on political \par representation, and the perceived high level of corruption. Almaty--the \par largest city--saw the most unrest and damage. \par  \par In response to the protests, President Tokayev undid the fuel price increase, \par put caps on other socially sensitive prices, including utilities, for \par first-half 2022, and dismissed the government. The president also declared a \par two-week state of emergency started Jan. 5 (the first since 2011) and \par requested assistance from the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Collective Security Treaty Organization\f28 \uc2 \f2   (\f28 \uc2 \f2 CSTO\f28 \uc2 \f2  ), \par a mutual defense organization with members from ex-Soviet states. \f28 \uc2 \f2 CSTO\f28 \uc2 \f2   \par peacekeeping troops, mostly from Russia, arrived quickly and order was \par restored. Banks reopened, KZT trading resumed on Jan. 12, and by Jan. 19 the \par state of emergency was lifted and \f28 \uc2 \f2 CSTO\f28 \uc2 \f2   troops completed their withdrawal (see \par "Kazakhstan Unrest Unlikely To Spur Fundamental Reform," published Feb. 2, \par 2022, on RatingsDirect). \par  \par Following the protests, President Tokayev announced updates to the \par government's economic reform agenda and stated that there will be political \par and institutional changes as well, although details are yet to be released. We \par believe political reforms will be difficult and structural challenges remain. \par In our view, government checks and balances are limited, there is no credible \par political opposition party, limitations on independent media outlets are \par significant, and perceived corruption is high, with Kazakhstan ranking 94th in \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Transparency International\f28 \uc2 \f2  's Corruption Perceptions Index. \par  \par We do not expect significant changes to Kazakhstan's institutional setting, \par which exhibits centralized decision-making and limited checks and balances \par between institutions, in our view. Despite the transfer of power in June 2019 \par when Tokayev was elected president, former president \f28 \uc2 \f2 Nursultan Nazarbayev\f28 \uc2 \f2   \par retained significant government influence. In January 2022, Mr. Nazarbayev \par resigned from the chairmanship of the security council and later the \par chairmanship of the ruling Nur Otan political party, which appears to have \par finally completed the transfer of power. We believe succession risks remain \par given that elections lack credibility--according to many third-party \par observers--there are reduced assurances of a smooth transfer of power, and \par there are limitations on the electorate's ability to make its voice heard. Our \par base case is that President Tokayev serves the remainder of his term to 2024. \par  \par Economic reforms in response to the unrest largely continue current efforts. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 4 Mar 2022 16:08 ET \par Press Release: S&PGR Affirms Kazakhstan Ratings -2-\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The government aims to diversify the economy through capital investment \par programs, such as the Nurly Zhol and Nurly Zher infrastructure and housing \par programs, both of which have been extended to 2025, and created the Agency for \par Strategic Reforms to coordinate reform efforts in 2021. The government is \par focusing on the inviolability of private property, cultivating the investment \par climate, reducing state participation in the economy, increasing private \par sector competition, improving tax policy, reforming public procurement, and \par reducing administrative barriers for businesses. The government also aims to \par increase the population's income by protecting purchasing power and increasing \par employment and entrepreneurship opportunities. Although we believe the \par government's prudent and generally effective macroeconomic policymaking will \par continue, we are less sure that the broad scope of reforms will bring about \par significant changes to the macroeconomic environment based on its previous \par attempts. \par  \par The economy expanded an estimated 4.0% in 2021, after real GDP contracted 2.5% \par in 2020, spurred by a strong rebound in the services sector and the \par information, communication, and transportation sector. Government stimulus \par measures continued supporting the economy into 2021. We forecast growth will \par average 3.8% over 2022-2025, supported by oil and non-oil sectors, although \par threats to the recovery remain from the lingering COVID-19 pandemic. However, \par the government has made a strong COVID-19 vaccination push with two doses \par administered to almost 80% of the eligible population, up from about \par one-quarter around mid-year 2021. \par  \par The oil sector should provide support to growth in the coming years. In 2022, \par as OPEC+ cuts taper off, we expect production to increase to 1.7 million \par barrels per day (mbpd). Further production will come online in the Kashagan \par field in 2023 and the Tengiz oilfield expansion should start producing in 2024 \par and 2025 after an original start time of 2023. We expect oil production to \par rise to about 2.0 mbpd by 2025. \par  \par Despite diversification efforts, oil remains the key economic sector in \par Kazakhstan, directly accounting for about 15% of GDP, more than 30% of general \par government revenue, and over half of exports. Although oil revenue supports \par the economy when prices are high, it also exposes Kazakhstan to terms-of-trade \par fluctuations and fiscal revenue volatility. \par  \par Flexibility and performance profile: The \f28 \uc2 \f2 NBK\f28 \uc2 \f2  is exiting from state-support \par programs to strengthen monetary policy effectiveness \par      -- Increasing oil production should support government revenue, keeping \par debt modest. \par      -- Despite current account deficits, Kazakhstan's external position will \par remain strong, with liquid external assets exceeding external debt by 19% of \par current account payments over 2022-2025. \par      -- Inflation averaged 8% over 2021, above the \f28 \uc2 \f2 NBK\f28 \uc2 \f2 's corridor of 4%-6%, \par amid expansionary fiscal policy and higher imported prices. \par  \par The consolidated general government balance was in a deficit of 5% of GDP in \par 2021, following a deficit of 7% in 2020. We expect the deficit to fall to 2.6% \par of GDP over 2022, supported by higher oil prices and production. Government \par expenditure has increased significantly since 2019, with a focus on social \par spending to support economic growth and combat the economic fallout from \par COVID-19. Expenditure will likely remain elevated compared with 2017-2019 \par levels and there are risks that the social spending needs of the economy will \par put fiscal consolidation on a slower path than forecast. For instance, as the \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 NBK\f28 \uc2 \f2  exits economic support programs, budgeted support to the economy may \par increase. \par  \par Despite strong growth in non-oil revenue after the introduction of a new tax \par code in 2017, a large share (30%) of fiscal revenue will remain dependent on \par the oil sector over our forecast period to 2025. Relatively high oil prices \par and increasing production over our forecast period will help strengthen the \par government's revenue position. This takes into account our assumption that the \par Brent oil price will average $85 per barrel (/bbl) for the remainder of 2022, \par $70/bbl in 2023, and $55/bbl in 2024 and beyond (see "S&P Global Ratings \par Raises Near-Term Oil And Gas Price Assumptions Following Russian Invasion Of \par Ukraine," published Feb. 28, 2022). \par  \par The government's fiscal stock position is strong, with low debt and a large \par level of liquid assets. The net asset position was 4% of GDP at year-end 2021. \par Although strong, this is down considerably after peaking at 38% of GDP in \par 2015. The value of the NFRK declined to about $55 billion (29% of GDP) at \par year-end 2021 from $73 billion at year-end 2014, as oil prices came down from \par historical highs and transfers from the fund to the government were elevated \par over this period. Implementation of a fiscal rule limiting the volume of \par transfers from the NFRK to the budget and expanding oil production volumes \par should help the government maintain a net asset position, which we forecast to \par average 3% of GDP over 2022-2025. \par  \par Gross government debt stood at a modest 27% of GDP at year-end 2021, up from \par 22% at year-end 2019, because of increased funding needs and KZT depreciation \par over the past two years. About 40% of debt is external and denominated in \par foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , while about 60% of external debt is commercial and the rest \par mostly from multilateral lenders. In the past two years, the government has \par increased borrowing from multilateral lenders and issued in the RUB \par market--because of the natural \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  hedge between the KZT and RUB. Despite \par the low debt stock, interest expenditure is relatively high, at about 7.3% of \par revenue in 2021, because average interest rates on domestic debt have been \par about 8% and increased to about 10% in early 2022. \par  \par The current account deficit improved to 3.0% of GDP in 2021, from 3.8% in \par 2020, due to a reduction in the deficit on the services balance. The trade \par balance increased strongly, by $10 billion, from higher oil prices and \par production, although this was offset by a $10 billion net outflow in the \par primary income account, spurred by profit repatriation on foreign direct \par investment related to oil production. We expect an improvement in the current \par account deficit to 2.4% of GDP in 2022 on expected higher oil prices and \par production. We also forecast the current account deficit at about 2.4% of GDP \par over 2022-2025, with the trade balance averaging a surplus of almost 10% of \par GDP a year, offset by the deficit in the services balance and the large \par deficit in the primary income balance. We note there is upside potential, \par since oil prices are currently above our assumed prices. \par  \par Kazakhstan's external asset position is strong and gross external financing \par needs are relatively low. Reserve assets at the \f28 \uc2 \f2 NBK\f28 \uc2 \f2  decreased slightly to \par $34.4 billion at year-end 2021 from $35.6 billion at year-end 2020, despite \par the gain from the \f28 \uc2 \f2 IMF\f28 \uc2 \f2  's Special Drawing Rights allocation of about $1.5 \par billion. About two-thirds of reserves are gold. The \f28 \uc2 \f2 NBK\f28 \uc2 \f2  purchases gold with \par domestic \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , providing the government a way to increase reserves despite \par current account deficits. We forecast reserves will decrease in 2022 and 2023, \par driven by our assumption of a fall in gold prices to 16,000 per ounce (/oz) in \par 2022 and 14,000/oz in 2023 (see "S&P Global Ratings Metal Price Assumptions: \par Tight Markets Could Support Another Year," published Feb. 7, 2022). \par  \par In our view, the more freely floating exchange rate, following the sharp \par devaluation in 2015, has helped the economy adjust to external pressures. For \par instance, it has reduced import demand when external conditions worsen, as \par seen during the oil price shock in 2020. The KZT depreciated sharply, by \par almost 15%, in response to weaker sentiment from \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Russia\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's military \par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  against Ukraine. We expect the KZT will strengthen in the second \par half of the year and that total depreciation will be about 8% over 2022. \par Ongoing current account deficits and the government's plan to reduce transfers \par from the NFRK, a key KZT purchaser in the market, will weigh on the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  \par over the forecast period to 2025. The \f28 \uc2 \f2 NBK\f28 \uc2 \f2  reserves the right to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to \par prevent dramatic KZT exchange rate fluctuations as well as to ensure the \par financial system's stability. It \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  on Feb. 28 by providing $98 \par million, and over Feb. 21-Feb. 25 with $176 million, because of KZT market \par instability amid weaker sentiment from \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Russia\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's military \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  against \par Ukraine. The \f28 \uc2 \f2 NBK\f28 \uc2 \f2  also \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  on Jan. 12 and Jan. 13 when the KZT market \par reopened following the domestic unrest, selling U.S. dollars to keep the \par exchange rate stable. Other \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  came last year because of unusually \par large outflows in November and a lack of liquidity in December around the \par holidays. \par  \par On Feb. 24, the \f28 \uc2 \f2 NBK\f28 \uc2 \f2  raised the base rate 325 basis points (bps) to 13.50%, to \par preserve price stability in the face of uncertainty as \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Russia\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  began its \par military \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  against Ukraine. In late January, the \f28 \uc2 \f2 NBK\f28 \uc2 \f2  also raised \par the base rate 50 bps to 10.25% to combat inflation, after three tightening \par rounds of 25 bps each over second-half 2021. Inflation averaged 8% in 2021, up \par from 6.8% in 2020, owing to the accommodative fiscal stance of the government, \par higher import prices (especially on food), and early withdrawals of savings \par from the government pension scheme by eligible citizens for use in housing, \par medical services, or transfers to private asset managers. These withdrawals \par totaled KZT2.7 trillion (3% of GDP) from the KZT12.9 trillion (16% of GDP) \par fund. We expect inflation will slow over the forecast period to about 5% by \par 2025 because of improvements to inflation targeting, the higher base rate, and \par a lower expected rate of KZT depreciation. \par  \par Monetary policy credibility faces challenges, such as the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \par perceived limited independence and the relatively weak banking system. \par However, the \f28 \uc2 \f2 NBK\f28 \uc2 \f2  is taking steps to increase its independence and \par effectiveness. It announced it will not take part in new economic lending and \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 4 Mar 2022 16:08 ET \par Press Release: S&PGR Affirms Kazakhstan Ratings -3-\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par support programs and will exit from all such programs by 2023, an accelerated \par timeline from the original target of 2025. In 2021, the \f28 \uc2 \f2 NBK\f28 \uc2 \f2  exited from five \par programs; a small and midsize enterprise (SME) support program, two mortgage \par programs, an automobile loan program, and the employment roadmap economic \par support program. Ending the largest program, the KZT800 billion (1% of GDP) \par support program for SMEs, has reduced excess KZT liquidity in the market \par significantly and closing the others also decreased credit segmentation in the \par economy, both helping to improve monetary transmission channels. In 2022, the \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 NBK\f28 \uc2 \f2  plans to end financing to the Economy of Simple Things program and another \par mortgage program. It remains a large shareholder of oil and gas company \par KazMunayGas NC JSC. \par  \par The \f28 \uc2 \f2 NBK\f28 \uc2 \f2  is also working with the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to build out a yield \par curve for KZT assets to improve liquidity in the domestic secondary market. To \par increase transparency in the KZT market, the \f28 \uc2 \f2 NBK\f28 \uc2 \f2  began publishing all sales of \par U.S. dollars from the NFRK. The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  also improved its focus on \par macroeconomic stability in 2019 by spinning off financial sector regulation to \par the newly created Agency for Regulation and Development of Financial Markets, \par which focuses on financial system stability. \par  \par We continue to observe high potential risks in Kazakhstan's banking sector, \par elevated by current geopolitical tensions and changes in the institutional \par setup. We expect that banks' asset-quality indicators may be temporarily \par pressured by worsening payment discipline among borrowers, and they are likely \par to face additional provisioning needs in first-half 2022. We also think that \par the changing operating environment, due to a reshuffling of the political \par class, could affect the competitive landscape, since many banks remain reliant \par on their owners' business and political connections. We currently classify \par Kazakhstan in group '9' under our Banking Industry Country Risk Assessment, on \par a 1-10 scale with '10' being the weakest score (see "Ratings On Eight \par Kazakhstani Banks Affirmed Under Revised Criteria Despite Elevated \par Uncertainties; Outlooks Unchanged," published Jan. 26, 2022). The local \par banking sector is relatively small with the overall loan portfolio accounting \par for only 22.5% of GDP at year-end 2020, reducing the efficiency of financial \par intermediation on the economy. \par  \par KEY STATISTICS \par  \par RELATED CRITERIA \par  \par      -- General Criteria: Environmental, Social, And Governance Principles In \par Credit Ratings, Oct. 10, 2021 \par      -- General Criteria: Methodology For National And Regional Scale Credit \par Ratings, June 25, 2018 \par      -- Criteria | Governments | Sovereigns: Sovereign Rating Methodology, \par Dec. 18, 2017 \par      -- General Criteria: Methodology For Linking Long-Term And Short-Term \par Ratings, April 7, 2017 \par      -- General Criteria: Principles Of Credit Ratings, Feb. 16, 2011 \par      -- General Criteria: Methodology: Criteria For Determining Transfer And \par Convertibility Assessments, May 18, 2009 \par  \par RELATED RESEARCH \par      -- Sovereign Ratings List, March 3, 2022 \par      -- Sovereign Ratings Hi