\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 5-\uc2 Fitch\uc2 , \uc2 Moody's\uc2  slash Russia's sovereign rating to junk\b0\par\par\uc2 700 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2022\par 06:30\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 (Updates with more from \uc2 Fitch\uc2 , analyst quote)\par \par \uc2 March 3 (Reuters) - Ratings agencies \uc2 Fitch\uc2  and \uc2 Moody's\uc2  downgraded Russia by six notches to "junk" status, saying Western sanctions threw into doubt its ability to service debt and would weaken the economy.\par \par \uc2 Russia's financial markets have been thrown into turmoil by sanctions imposed over its invasion of Ukraine, the biggest attack on a European state since World War Two.\par \par \uc2 The invasion has triggered a flurry of credit rating moves and dire warnings about the impact on Russia's economy. S&P lowered Russia's rating to junk status last week.\par \par \uc2 It also prompted index providers FTSE Russell and MSCI to announce on Wednesday that they will remove Russian equities from all their indexes, after a top MSCI executive earlier this week called Russia's stock market "uninvestable".\par \par \uc2 FTSE Russell said the decision will be effective from March 7, while MSCI said its decision will be implemented in one step across all MSCI indexes as of the close on March 9. MSCI said it is also reclassifying MSCI Russia Indexes from emerging markets to standalone markets status.\par \par \uc2 Russia has a weighting of 3.24% in MSCI's emerging market benchmark and a weighting of around 30 basis points in the index provider's global benchmark .\par \par \uc2 The Institute of International Finance predicts a double-digit contraction in economic growth this year.\par \par \uc2 Fitch\uc2  downgraded Russia to "B" from "BBB" and placed the country's ratings on "rating watch negative". \uc2 Moody's\uc2 , which last week had flagged the possibility of a downgrade, also cut the country's rating by six notches, to B3 from Baa3.\par \par \uc2 Fitch\uc2  said the only other precedent to such a large six-notch downgrade on a single sovereign entity was South Korea in 1997.\par \par \uc2 "The severity of international sanctions in response to Russia's military invasion of Ukraine has heightened macro-financial stability risks, represents a huge shock to Russia's credit fundamentals and could undermine its willingness to service government debt," \uc2 Fitch\uc2  said in a report {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.fitchratings.com/research/sovereigns/fitch-downgrades-russia-to-b-on-rating-watch-negative-02-03-2022"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://www.fitchratings.com/research/sovereigns/fitch-downgrades-russia-to-b-on-rating-watch-negative-02-03-2022}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Fitch\uc2  said that U.S. and \uc2 EU\uc2  sanctions prohibiting any transactions with the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Russia\uc2  would have a "much larger impact on Russia's credit fundamentals than any previous sanctions," rendering much of \b \highlight22\uc2 Russia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's international reserves unusable for \b \highlight22\uc2 FX intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "The sanctions could also weigh on \b \highlight22\uc2 Russia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's willingness to repay debt," \uc2 Fitch\uc2  warned. "President Putin's response to put nuclear forces on high alert appears to diminish the prospect of him changing course on Ukraine to the degree required to reverse rapidly tightening sanctions."\par \par \uc2 Fitch\uc2  said it expects further ratcheting up of sanctions on Russian banks.\par \par \uc2 Moody's\uc2  said on Thursday the scope and severity of the sanctions "have gone beyond \uc2 Moody's\uc2  initial expectations and will have material credit implications."\par \par \uc2 The sanctions imposed by Western countries will also markedly weaken Russia's GDP growth potential relative to the ratings agency's previous assessment of 1.6%, \uc2 Fitch\uc2  said.\par \par \uc2 "In this case, the sanctions-driven frozen/falling assets tail-wagged the ratings dog," analysts at \uc2 Mizuho\uc2  wrote. They added that "ratings and benchmark risks revealed may compound further capital exodus as benchmark funds are forced to liquidate rather than hold."\par \par \uc2 Sanctions imposed on Russia have significantly increased the chance of the country's defaulting on its dollar and other international market government debt, analysts at \uc2 JPMorgan\uc2  and elsewhere said on Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 Russia has responded to the sanctions with a range of measures to shore up its economic defenses and retaliate against Western restrictions. It hiked its main lending rate to 20%, banned Russian brokers from selling securities held by foreigners, ordered exporting companies to buttress the rouble, and said it would stop foreign investors selling assets.\par \par \uc2 The government also plans to tap its National Wealth Fund (NWF), a rainy day cushion, to help counter sanctions.\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Mehr Bedi in Bengaluru and Megan Davies in New York; Additional reporting by Andrew Galbraith in Shanghai and Vidya Ranganathan in Singapore; Editing by Leslie Adler and Stephen Coates) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2022-3-3T06:30:05.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020220302ei32056q9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
