\par\par \uc2 CE Noticias Financieras English\par \b \uc2 Fitch\uc2  downgrades Russia's rating from BBB to B, citing possible impact of sanctions\b0\par\par\uc2 283 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2022\par \uc2 CE NoticiasFinancieras\par \uc2 NFINCE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 Content Engine LLC \par \par \uc2 Fitch\uc2  downgraded on Wednesday, 2, Russia's rating from BBB to B, a rating in which the outlook is not usually offered. According to the rating agency, the country is now in the Rating Watch Negative (RWN) category.\par \par \uc2 Fitch\uc2  assesses that Russia has suffered a "severe shock to credit fundamentals. The severity of international sanctions in response to the military invasion of Ukraine has increased stability risks, represents a major shock to Russia's credit fundamentals, and may undermine its willingness to repay government debt, it points out.\par \par \uc2 "The developments will weaken Russia's external and public finances, severely restrict its financing flexibility, sharply reduce trend GDP growth, and elevate domestic and geopolitical risk and uncertainty," \uc2 Fitch\uc2  projects. The RWN category reflects the high degree of volatility in international relations, including the potential for further sanctions and uncertainty about Russia's policy response, such as not paying its debt, and the risk of a more acute loss of domestic economic confidence, the agency explains.\par \par \uc2 The announced sanctions from the United States and the \uc2 European Union\uc2  that prohibit any transactions with the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Russia\uc2  (\uc2 CBR\uc2 ) will have a much greater impact on the country's credit fundamentals than any previous sanctions, \uc2 Fitch\uc2  assesses. Full implementation could render much of \b \highlight22\uc2 Russia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's international reserves unusable for \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and a large proportion could be subject to asset freezes, it projects. About 32 percent of Russia's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves are denominated in euros and 16 percent in U.S. dollars, and more than half are held in countries participating in sanctions, the agency points out.\par \par \uc2 Dokument NFINCE0020220303ei32000pf\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
