\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 4-Rouble hits record low in Moscow, remains volatile outside Russia\b0\par\par\uc2 742 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2022\par 23:19\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 (Updates prices, adds comment)\par \par \uc2 NEW YORK, March 2 (Reuters) - The rouble touched a record low of 110 to the dollar in Moscow on Wednesday and crawled back near 100 in other trading platforms, though it continued under pressure as Russia's financial system teetered under the weight of Western sanctions imposed over the invasion of Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 The Russian stock market remained closed and trading on bonds showed wide bid-ask spreads and little-to-no volume.\par \par \uc2 The rouble fell 4.5% to 106.02 against the dollar in Moscow trade, earlier hitting 110.0, a record low. It has lost 30% of its value against the dollar since the start of the year. Against the euro, it shed 2.5% on Wednesday to finish the day at 115.40.\par \par \uc2 But trading outside of Russia saw the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rebound to end the day up 6% to 100 on the EBS platform and 7.6% at 97.6 elsewhere.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is still over 20% weaker than where it traded at during the first half of February.\par \par \uc2 On the EBS platform, the rouble has this week had the three widest daily ranges since 2010, with Monday the widest range on record.\par \par \uc2 "Who gosh-darn knows what's going to happen tomorrow," said Colin Stewart, head of Americas at Quant Insight in New York.\par \par \uc2 "It's just too volatile."\par \par \uc2 Russia has responded to the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  weakness by more than doubling its benchmark interest rate to 20% and telling companies to convert 80% of their foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  revenues on the domestic market as the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which is now under Western sanctions, has stopped \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The weak rouble will hit living standards in \b \highlight22\uc2 Russia\b0 \highlight\uc2  and fan already high inflation, while Western sanctions are expected to create shortages of essential goods and services such as cars or flights.\par \par \uc2 Many international companies have announced plans to exit Russia, while the country's credit ratings are coming under pressure as a result of the crisis.\par \par \uc2 Credit rating agency \uc2 Moody's\uc2  said it was reviewing Russia's rating for a downgrade, a move that "reflects the negative credit implications for Russia's credit profile from the additional and more severe sanctions being imposed."\par \par \uc2 JPMorgan\uc2  said about $4.2 billion in Russian debt is at risk of being kicked out of investment-grade bond indexes.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, Scope Ratings said capital controls "raise significant questions surrounding the Russian state's willingness to service its debt owed to foreign residents" a day after cutting its Russia rating to junk status.\par \par \uc2 The measures, Scope added, make Russia "more vulnerable to banking and liquidity crises."\par \par \uc2 In a separate note, \uc2 JPMorgan\uc2  said there was a deep recession in the making for Russia and the bank was reassessing its regional macro forecasts.\par \par \uc2 "The most recent measures targeting the \uc2 CBR\uc2  have completely changed the picture," \uc2 JPMorgan\uc2  said.\par \par \uc2 "Russia's large current account surplus could have accommodated large capital outflows, but with accompanying \uc2 CBR\uc2  and SWIFT sanctions, on top of the existing restrictions, it is likely that Russia's export earnings will be disrupted, and capital outflows will likely be immediate."\par \par \uc2 Several Russian banks have been barred from the SWIFT global financial network that facilitates transfers between banks.\par \par \uc2 As households and businesses in Russia have rushed to convert the falling rouble into foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , banks raised rates for foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  deposits to attract those flows.\par \par \uc2 Russia's largest lender \uc2 Sberbank\uc2  is offering to pay 4% on deposits of up to $1,000, while the largest private lender \uc2 Alfa Bank\uc2  is offering 8% on three-month dollar deposits. For rouble deposits, \uc2 Sberbank\uc2  offers a 20% annual return.\par \par \uc2 Sberbank\uc2  said on Wednesday it was quitting almost all European markets, blaming big cash outflows and threats to its staff and property, after the \uc2 ECB\uc2  ordered the closure of its European arm.\par \par \uc2 The bank's London-traded shares fell to 4.5 cents from $16 at the start of the year.\par \par \uc2 A U.S.-traded ETF of Russian companies and others heavily exposed to Russia fell 13% on Wednesday, for a 72% drop since mid-February.\par \par \uc2 Moscow calls its actions in Ukraine a "special \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2 " that it says is not designed to occupy territory but to destroy its neighbour's military capabilities and capture what it regards as dangerous nationalists. (Reporting by Reuters; Editing by Jane Merriman, Jonathan Oatis and Grant McCool) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2022-3-2T23:19:00.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020220302ei3201zol\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw103\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal1545\pichgoal600
