\par\par \uc2 CE Noticias Financieras English\par \b \uc2 Russia-Ukraine conflict: A first negotiation amidst the bombs\b0\par\par\uc2 1,560 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2022\par \uc2 CE NoticiasFinancieras\par \uc2 NFINCE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 Content Engine LLC \par \par \uc2 From Paris\par \par \uc2 Triple billiards around the Russian occupation of Ukraine. The Russian army intensified its offensive in several points of Ukraine at the same time that Russia and Ukraine held the first negotiations in Belarus and that the French President, \uc2 Emmanuel Macron\uc2 , met again by telephone with \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  to agree on the conditions that could put an end to the conflict. Both the direct negotiation and the talks between Macron and Putin do not contain, so far, elements to lower skepticism. Moscow increased its military actions in the second city of the country, Kharkov, with the prospect of dialogue between the Russian and Ukrainian delegations. Former Russian Culture Minister \uc2 Vladimir Medinsky\uc2  is leading the negotiations and has said that they are seeking to reach an agreement that "takes into account the interests of both sides". Ukrainian Defense Minister \uc2 Oleksii Reznikov\uc2 , leading the Ukrainian delegation, stressed that the condition was an immediate "cease-fire" and the departure of Russian troops from his country. The talks will continue on Tuesday, March 1.\par \par \uc2 Almost all the cards seem to be in Putin's hands, despite the unprecedented package of sanctions adopted in the last hours by the \uc2 European Union\uc2  and the group of the most industrialized countries, the \uc2 G7\uc2 . The measures have already caused the ruble to plummet by 20% and the \uc2 Russian \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has doubled its interest rate (9.5 to 20%). FIFA joined this cannonade of sanctions when, on Monday, it excluded Russia from the World Cup in Qatar (2022). Despite this encirclement, the head of the Russian state has a double military advantage: the first is that he is about to take control of the city of Berdiansk and this places Russian forces very close to seizing Mariupol. This is the port town on the Sea of Azov. If Moscow manages to control Mariupol it would have within its reach the famous corridor that connects with Crimea, which Moscow annexed in 2014, and the Donbas, which is Russia's priority target. That is the meaning of the telephone conversation between Macron and Putin. The Russian presidency issued a statement with the new fold of conditions it presented to Macron in which the "recognition of Russian sovereignty in Crimea," not recognized by the West, figures. Putin also demanded "demilitarization and denazification of the Ukrainian state" as well as a "neutral status" prior to any solution. Paris, in its turn, advanced that Putin had been asked not to bomb civilians, not to destroy civilian infrastructure and not to violate roads.\par \par \uc2 According to the Elys\u233\'00\'E9e Palace, the Russian president "confirmed his willingness to commit himself" to these points. Paris also insisted on "the need for a cease-fire" and the importance of maintaining an open channel of dialogue. The French president proposed "to remain in contact in the coming days. We will exchange again". Putin has been adding many powerful pieces on this war chessboard with a very effective anticipation. Not least of all, last Sunday, a referendum was held in Belarus in complete secrecy, opening the door to a modification of the Constitution to put an end to the "neutrality" of Belarus and to the prohibition of the installation of nuclear weapons on its territory. The consultation was approved by 65% of the population.\par \par \uc2 Europe is both proud of its unprecedented decisions and at the same time blocked from going any further. It cannot call on the Atlantic Alliance (\uc2 NATO\uc2 ) and, as it lacks a common defense, cannot \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  as a whole. The division of responsibilities between \uc2 NATO\uc2  and the \uc2 EU\uc2  limits a military response, all the more so since the \uc2 EU\uc2 's vocation is not to return to the times of war that the continent has already known. The "activation" of the nuclear response announced on Monday by Putin also raises fears of an abyssal conflict.\par \par \uc2 NATO\uc2  and the UN NATO took part in several missions that were not its own responsibility and left in its wake a trail of deaths and shipwrecks: in Kosovo (1999), in Afghanistan (2003) and, more recently, in the international coalition that put an end to the Libyan regime of President \uc2 Muammar Gaddafi\uc2  (2011). In Afghanistan it lost the war alongside the United States and in Libya, in 2011, it decapitated a country and unleashed the greatest humanitarian crisis of the century. This \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  (Unified Protector (OUP), requested by the United Nations, led to the flight of millions of people across the Mediterranean Sea to Europe. Previously, in Kosovo, starting in 1999, another mission was undertaken at the request of the \uc2 UN\uc2 , the result of which can be summed up in the phrase left by that conflict: "collateral damage". Each time it was the Security Council that requested its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . It did not do so this time for three reasons: Russia is a nuclear power: neither Europe nor \uc2 NATO\uc2  wants a direct confrontation with \b \highlight22\uc2 Russia\b0 \highlight\uc2 : to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 , among other conditions, \uc2 NATO\uc2  requires a request from the \uc2 UN Security Council\uc2 . In this month of February, the Council was chaired by \b \highlight22\uc2 Russia\b0 \highlight\uc2  and, therefore, Moscow was not going to request an \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  against itself.\par \par \uc2 Ukraine asks to join the \uc2 EU\uc2  The \uc2 EU\uc2  and \uc2 NATO\uc2  divide tasks; the \uc2 EU\uc2  is in charge of managing crises outside the Union and \uc2 NATO\uc2  is in charge of defending the territories of the 30 countries of the Atlantic Alliance. Ukraine is neither a member of the \uc2 EU\uc2  nor of \uc2 NATO\uc2 . The Ukrainian president, Volodymir Zelenski, reiterated on Monday, February 28, his request for the \uc2 European Union\uc2  to accept his country as a member by means of "a special procedure". We will have to wait. In fact, the EU hit Russia for what it is, an economic giant, and got stuck in its geopolitical and military smallness. Yet, this conflict did upset the course: for the first time in its recent history, the \uc2 EU\uc2  came out of its mold when it agreed to deliver arms to Ukraine through an "assistance" fund in the amount of 450 million euros. The conflict in Ukraine pushed Europe into another world. 77 years after World War II (1939-1945) Europeans set aside their creed of peace on which the Europe we know was built. The turnaround is gigantic, even if the help may seem modest. The countries of Eastern Europe played a decisive role in this shift. Occupied first by the Nazis and then by the Soviets, Eastern Europe sees the specter of a new occupation rising again. Even countries like Sweden broke the doctrine of not supplying arms to a country at war. The last time it had done so was in 1939, when Finland was attacked by the Soviet Union. This Monday, February 28, the \uc2 EU\uc2  expanded its range of action. It agreed to coordinate the purchase of arms requested by Ukraine and to enable its satellites to provide "intelligence" support to Kiev.\par \par \uc2 Gas The \uc2 EU\uc2  is also constrained by the energy emergency. The Brussels Commission and the Member States blocked the assets of the \uc2 Russian \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , removed certain Russian banks from the SWIFT international banking exchange system and even proposed to ban Russian channels such as Russia Today and Sputnik from broadcasting in EU countries (a measure, moreover, very difficult to implement). However, the one thing that would have set Russia back was left untouched: gas. It is all the more decisive because not even in the most tense moments of the Cold War did Russia stop supplying gas to Europe and Europe stopped buying it. Russian gas exports are as key for Russia as they are for the West. Europe is Moscow's first customer, for whom gas exports account for 60% of the foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  it earns. In turn, Europe is Russia's first gas customer with an overall dependence of 40%. The percentage reaches 65% in Germany and 75% in countries such as Austria and the crown of nations that were once satellites of the USSR: the Baltic countries, Hungary, Poland, the Czech Republic and Slovakia. The German Chancellor, \uc2 Olaf Scholz\uc2 , decided to halt the process by which the Russian \uc2 Nord Stream 2\uc2  pipeline, which passes under the Baltic Sea and goes directly to Germany and from there to Europe, was to be put into \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2 . However, this pipe-line, whose construction ended in 2021 and which was always opposed by the United States, was not yet in \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2 . In contrast, the other pipeline, Nord Stream 1, has been supplying gas since 2012 and no one, neither in Germany nor elsewhere, has mentioned suspending supplies. There are, in addition, three other Russian-European gas pipelines in activity: Yamal, Brotherhood and Soyouz. Neither Algeria, Turkey, Azerbaijan nor the United States are in a position to replace all the gas coming from Russia.\par \par \uc2 The future comes with another immense problem. The management of refugees and displaced persons. For the \uc2 EU\uc2  it is a head-scratcher. The magnitude of what is expected has no recent examples: four million refugees, 7 million displaced persons and 18 million people affected by war. Those are the projections emerging from last Sunday's meeting between EU Interior Ministers. "The war will be long," said Macron. And like all wars, impossible to foresee. A major catastrophe lurks in Europe. However, there was time and diplomatic means to avoid it.\par \par \uc2 efebbro@pagina12. com. ar\par \par \uc2 Dokument NFINCE0020220301ei31002az\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
